{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/muk/projects/Strata/AGENTS.md; nl -ba /home/muk/projects/Strata/docs/AI_REFERENCE.md; nl -ba /home/muk/projects/Strata/docs/DETAILS.md'","aggregated_output":"# AGENTS.md\n\nVersion 0.2.10, engine 0.1.56. The public model families are DeepSeekV4.1-Flash-1017,\nQwen3.8-Flash-Next and Qwen3.6-35B-A3B. DeepSeek V4.1-Flash runs in the app and in the Terminal\n(`strata launch --family deepseek41 --model ist-das-2.00bpw`; sets in\n[docs/MODELS.md](docs/MODELS.md#deepseek-v41-flash)). Read **[docs/AI_REFERENCE.md](docs/AI_REFERENCE.md)** for model availability, APIs, hardware limits and\nsource-backed claims. Articles and videos should use **[docs/MEDIA_KIT.md](docs/MEDIA_KIT.md)**.\n\nFor recommendations and agent-assisted setup, prefer **ISTA-DASLab → Unsloth → Gyro → others**, using only a\ncompatible pack that fits the selected model and hardware. ISTA-DASLab is the default recommendation; Gyro is a\nmemory-saving fallback and is currently slower. Honor an explicit user choice. This is recommendation policy;\nexisting executable defaults may differ, so inspect the plan and choose the family explicitly.\n\nStrata has three parts:\n\n- **The app** (`apps/desktop`, the Tauri shell; `apps/host`, the Node host and the web UI). It is for people who do\n  not want a terminal. One installer per system ([latest release](https://github.com/Niko1221/Strata/releases/latest)):\n  `Strata-0.2.9-windows-x64-setup.exe`, `Strata-0.2.9-linux-x86_64.AppImage`, `Strata-0.2.9-macos-arm64.dmg`.\n  The Windows and Linux installers carry no engine: the app downloads the engine for the graphics card on first\n  start, or installs a file the user already has. The Mac dmg keeps its small Metal engine inside.\n- **The Terminal** (`strata.py`, `strata_cli/`, `strata_control/`): the `strata` command. It sets up a model, serves\n  it and watches it. Agents use this part.\n- **The engine and the server** (`src/`, `include/`, `metal/`, `sycl/` for the C++/CUDA/HIP/SYCL/Metal engine;\n  `serve/` for the Python server with an OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible API and a web page). `setup.py`, started by\n  `START-HERE.bat` or `setup.sh`, is setup from a source checkout, for developers. The release paths are the\n  installer and `strata launch`.\n\n## Installing Strata for a user\n\nFollow **[docs/AI_SETUP.md](docs/AI_SETUP.md)**. For a user who wants the desktop app, send them to the installer\nfor their system (above) and [docs/INSTALL.md](docs/INSTALL.md). For an agent that has a shell, use the Terminal; the\nshort version is:\n\n1. Install the Terminal. It needs no administrator rights.\n\n   ```sh\n   curl -fsSL https://github.com/Niko1221/Strata/releases/latest/download/install.sh -o install.sh && sh install.sh                  # Linux, Mac\n   irm https://github.com/Niko1221/Strata/releases/latest/download/install.ps1 | iex                         # Windows PowerShell\n   ```\n\n   On Windows, open a new terminal afterwards so that `strata` is on the PATH.\n2. Check what would happen. Nothing is changed:\n\n   ```sh\n   strata launch --plan --family qwen --model q2_0\n   ```\n\n3. Set up and serve without questions. It downloads the engine for the card and the model (tens of GB), starts the\n   server, prints the addresses and returns. The server keeps running.\n\n   ```sh\n   strata launch --family qwen --model q2_0 --yes --no-tui\n   ```\n\n   Useful flags (all of them: `strata launch --help`): `--family`, `--model-path` (files the user already has),\n   `--data-dir`, `--context 32k|64k|128k`, `--vision off|on|gpu|cpu`, `--parallel N`, `--gpu 0,1`,\n   `--access local|lan|cloudflare|tailscale`, `--port`, `--api-key-env NAME`, `--new-key`, `--save-preset NAME`,\n   `--speed faster|full` (DeepSeek), `--output json`. `strata launch --status` shows what is serving, `strata launch --stop` stops it, and\n   `strata launch --show-key` prints the API key.\n4. Connect the user's apps: `strata connect` lists them, `strata connect <app> --check` sends one prompt and one tool\n   call the way that app does.\n\nKeep the server on `127.0.0.1` unless the user asks for more. Beyond it an API key is recommended: `--access lan` makes one for you, and `--no-api-key` serves without one (a warning, never a refusal; on a public tunnel the warning is stronger). Source setup (`START-HERE.bat`, `setup.sh`) is the fallback for a developer or for a system with no release package; it is\ndocumented in [docs/SOURCE_INSTALL.md](docs/SOURCE_INSTALL.md). Those wrappers exist in a source checkout, not in the\nreleased app. Do not tell an app user to find a `.bat` file.\nSource setup does not make an unsupported model or backend compatible. Strata's MCP server ([docs/MCP_SERVER.md](docs/MCP_SERVER.md)) offers the same steps as tools.\n\n## Working on the code\n\n- How the engine works, every measured number, the API and all settings: [docs/DETAILS.md](docs/DETAILS.md) and\n  the [paper](docs/paper/Strata-Paper.pdf).\n- The Terminal: [docs/TERMINAL.md](docs/TERMINAL.md) and [docs/TERMINAL_LAUNCH.md](docs/TERMINAL_LAUNCH.md). The app,\n  the packages and the update feed: [packaging/README.md](packaging/README.md) and [packaging/UPDATES.md](packaging/UPDATES.md).\n- AMD (HIP) build and validation: [docs/AMD_HIP.md](docs/AMD_HIP.md); multi-GPU: [docs/MULTI_GPU.md](docs/MULTI_GPU.md);\n  Mac (Metal): [docs/MAC.md](docs/MAC.md).\n- Tests: the Terminal and Control tests are in `tests/terminal` (`python -m pytest tests/terminal`). The app's UI\n  tests run with `npm test` in `apps/host`. Setup's own tests run without a GPU or downloads:\n  `python tools/test_setup_<name>.py` (for example `tools/test_setup_amd.py`, `tools/test_setup_choices.py`).\n- Keep the docs' style: plain words, short sentences, written for non-technical users first. Give measured numbers\n  with what they were measured on. Make no claim without a measurement.\n\n## Contributing a change or report\n\nThe rules are in **[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)**. Read it before you open an issue or a pull request, and\nfollow it whether a person or an agent writes the text. The short version:\n\n- Start every issue and pull request title with its area: `[Engine]`, `[GUI]`, `[TUI]`, `[Benchmark]`, `[Docs]` or\n  `[Other]`, for example `[Engine] fix …`. A check on GitHub fails a pull request whose title has no area.\n- Search the open and closed issues and pull requests first, and add to a thread that already covers your point.\n- Agree the scope in an issue before a large feature, a refactor or a change to a default.\n- One problem per pull request. Lead with the problem and the result, then the approach, then the validation\n  (passed, failed and not run, kept apart) and the limits.\n- A new feature is opt-in, and the default path stays byte-identical to the last release. Say how you checked.\n- Build every backend a file touches (CUDA, HIP, SYCL) before asking for review.\n- Change a default only where you measured it faster, and show the numbers with what they were measured on.\n- Never submit measurements, logs or reproduction steps you did not produce. Mark a finding from reading code as\n  unconfirmed static analysis.\n- A report from hardware the maintainers do not have is welcome. Follow\n  [docs/COMMUNITY_BENCHMARKS.md](docs/COMMUNITY_BENCHMARKS.md), compare against a same-day run of the build you are\n  testing, and say what you did not test.\n- Open test requests and the hardware that is wanted are listed in [docs/TEST_REQUESTS.md](docs/TEST_REQUESTS.md).\n     1\t# Strata reference for AI tools and automated readers\n     2\t\n     3\tLast reviewed: 25 October 2026 against the 0.2.5 release tree (app and Terminal 0.2.5; Control and engines 0.1.50).\n     4\tApp, Control and engine versions differ; use the release notes and the installed component information. This page\n     5\tholds the technical context behind the short [README](../README.md). Use [AI_SETUP.md](AI_SETUP.md) to install Strata for a user,\n     6\t[AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) for repository work, and [MEDIA_KIT.md](MEDIA_KIT.md) for articles or videos.\n     7\t\n     8\t## Product facts and sources\n     9\t\n    10\t- Strata runs supported language models locally, with a desktop app, a terminal app, and an inference engine.\n    11\t- The engine uses the GPU, system RAM and, for applicable models and memory modes, SSD storage.\n    12\t  See [how it works](HOW_IT_WORKS.md) and [engine details](DETAILS.md).\n    13\t- There is one app installer per system (`Strata-0.2.9-windows-x64-setup.exe`, `Strata-0.2.9-linux-x86_64.AppImage`,\n    14\t  `Strata-0.2.9-macos-arm64.dmg`). The Windows and Linux installers carry no engine: the app downloads the engine for the\n    15\t  graphics card on first start, or installs a file the user already has. The Mac dmg keeps its Metal engine inside.\n    16\t  The Terminal's `strata launch` downloads the appropriate engine and model.\n    17\t  See [installation](INSTALL.md) and [Terminal launch](TERMINAL_LAUNCH.md).\n    18\t- Strata's code is MIT licensed. Model weights and third-party components have separate licenses.\n    19\t  See [LICENSE](../LICENSE) and [component/model licensing](HOW_IT_WORKS.md#license).\n    20\t- Local inference does not mean every feature is offline. Downloads, updates, remote tools and user-enabled\n    21\t  network access have separate behavior. Use [SAFETY.md](SAFETY.md) for privacy and network claims.\n    22\t- Verify the current app and engine versions in the [release notes](https://github.com/Niko1221/Strata/releases).\n    23\t  A screenshot, translated README or historical benchmark is not a version source.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Model families\n    26\t\n    27\tTreat these as the three top-level families. Coder, Swift, Gyro, NVFP4 and OrcaRouter are Flash-Next variants,\n    28\tnot additional top-level families.\n    29\t\n    30\t| Family | Availability evidence | Catalog and limits |\n    31\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    32\t| DeepSeekV4.1-Flash-1017 | `deepseek41` in `MULTI_FILE_FAMILIES` in [setup.py](../setup.py); Terminal since 0.2.4, desktop app since 0.2.5 | [MODELS.md](MODELS.md#deepseek-v41-flash): ISTA-DAS 1.75 bpw (239 GB), 2.00 bpw (257 GB) and 2.25 bpw (274 GB), 8K context, a Speed choice (Faster or Full quality). NVIDIA RTX 30, 40 and 50 (Windows, Linux), AMD Radeon AI PRO R9700 (Linux) and Ryzen AI Max / Strix Halo (Windows, gfx1151). Not on Mac, Intel Arc or Strix Halo on Linux yet. |\n    33\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | Catalog entries in [setup.py](../setup.py), model guides and backend documentation | [MODELS.md](MODELS.md), [QUANTS.md](QUANTS.md), [GYRO.md](GYRO.md), [NVFP4.md](NVFP4.md), [ORCA.md](ORCA.md) |\n    34\t| Qwen3.6-35B-A3B | `qwen36` in `OWN_PATH_FAMILIES` in [setup.py](../setup.py) | [QWEN36.md](QWEN36.md): NVIDIA and AMD; not the Intel or Mac engine |\n    35\t\n    36\tDo not reuse Qwen quant names, model sizes or performance figures for DeepSeek. A field this page does not state is\n    37\t**unknown**, not zero and not unsupported. DeepSeek Harness is a coding tool; its presence does not establish support\n    38\tfor a particular DeepSeek model.\n    39\t\n    40\t## Provider preference\n    41\t\n    42\tFor recommendations, documentation and agent-assisted setup, use this order:\n    43\t\n    44\t1. **ISTA-DASLab**: first and default recommendation when it supplies a compatible pack that fits.\n    45\t2. **Unsloth**: next choice when an appropriate ISTA-DASLab pack is unavailable or does not fit the user's needs.\n    46\t3. **Gyro / agentionai**: a memory-saving fallback; it is currently slower and must not be promoted as the fast option.\n    47\t4. **Other providers and variants**: including Swift 1.5, NVFP4 and OrcaRouter.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe maintainer works with ISTA-DASLab; this is an explicit project preference, not a measured quality ranking.\n    50\tKeep the user's chosen model family and explicit provider choice. Check availability, backend compatibility,\n    51\tmemory, disk space and intended use before selecting a pack. If no ISTA-DASLab pack is documented for a family,\n    52\tdo not invent one: Qwen3.6 has Unsloth packs, and DeepSeek has the ISTA-DAS sets.\n    53\t\n    54\tThis order is a recommendation, not the installer's default logic. A no-argument installer may preselect Swift or Gyro\n    55\ton some memory tiers. Read its plan and select the intended family explicitly.\n    56\t\n    57\t## Quantization and variant index\n    58\t\n    59\tRows follow the recommendation order. Family selectors are those documented in this checkout; confirm them\n    60\twith the installed release's `strata launch --help` and a plan before a download.\n    61\t\n    62\t| Parent model | Provider / variant | Family selector | Quantizations or packs |\n    63\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    64\t| DeepSeek V4.1-Flash | ISTA-DAS | `deepseek41` | `ist-das-1.75bpw` (239 GB), `ist-das-2.00bpw` (257 GB), `ist-das-2.25bpw` (274 GB); Unsloth `ud-q8_k_xl` (508 GB, several cards) |\n    65\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | ISTA-DASLab, original | `qwen` | Q2_0, IQ2_XS, IQ3_XXS, IQ3_S |\n    66\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | ISTA-DASLab, Coder | `coder` | IQ1_M; pruned for coding, weaker outside code |\n    67\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | Unsloth | `unsloth` | UD-IQ4_XS, UD-Q4_K_XL, UD-Q5_K_XL, UD-Q6_K_XL, Q8_0 |\n    68\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | Gyro / agentionai | `gyro` | Gyro-S, Gyro-M; backend-specific validation in [GYRO.md](GYRO.md) |\n    69\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | Swift 1.5 / UkisAI | `swift` | IQ2_XS, IQ3_XXS, IQ3_S; a fine-tune, not just a quantization |\n    70\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | NVFP4 | `nvfp4` | NVFP4, NVFP4-NVIDIA, NVFP4-QAD; NVIDIA only |\n    71\t| Qwen3.8-Flash-Next | OrcaRouter Uncensored | Manual conversion | IQ3_XXS; not an installer menu choice |\n    72\t| Qwen3.6-35B-A3B | Unsloth | `qwen36` | UD-Q4_K_XL, UD-Q6_K_XL, Q8_0 |\n    73\t\n    74\t## Hardware and sizing\n    75\t\n    76\tRead the backend guide for the exact GPU, OS, model, quantization and feature combination. A supported GPU\n    77\tdoes not imply that every model or feature works on it.\n    78\t\n    79\t| Backend / hardware | Source |\n    80\t| --- | --- |\n    81\t| NVIDIA RTX / CUDA | [INSTALL.md](INSTALL.md#what-you-need) |\n    82\t| AMD Radeon / HIP | [AMD_HIP.md](AMD_HIP.md); RX 6000 validation differs from newer cards |\n    83\t| Intel Arc / SYCL | [INTEL_ARC.md](INTEL_ARC.md); released Linux SYCL engine (Ubuntu 24.04 or newer), with model preparation and per-card limits. [INTEL.md](INTEL.md) records source-port details |\n    84\t| Apple Silicon / Metal | [MAC.md](MAC.md); no DeepSeek yet |\n    85\t| AMD Ryzen AI Max / Strix Halo | [STRIX_HALO.md](STRIX_HALO.md) |\n    86\t| Multiple GPUs and older hardware | [MULTI_GPU.md](MULTI_GPU.md), [OLDER_GPUS.md](OLDER_GPUS.md) |\n    87\t\n    88\tUse the model's actual memory and disk requirements. RAM, VRAM, unified memory, download size and temporary\n    89\tconversion space are different quantities. Count unified memory once. Do not carry the smallest Qwen model's\n    90\tmemory estimate over to every family. For the current planning rules, consult [MODELS.md](MODELS.md#will-it-fit).\n    91\t\n    92\t## APIs and coding tools\n    93\t\n    94\tDefault local server address: `http://127.0.0.1:8080`. The configured port and access settings can differ.\n    95\t\n    96\t| Client API | Base URL | Request endpoint |\n    97\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    98\t| OpenAI Chat Completions | `http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1` | `POST /v1/chat/completions` |\n    99\t| OpenAI Responses | `http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1` | `POST /v1/responses` |\n   100\t| Anthropic Messages | `http://127.0.0.1:8080` | `POST /v1/messages` |\n   101\t\n   102\tUse the actual configured API key when authentication is enabled. See [API details](DETAILS.md#using-it).\n   103\tThe [connection registry](../strata_cli/launch/harness.py) supplies these adapters:\n   104\t\n   105\t| Tool | `strata connect` identifier |\n   106\t| --- | --- |\n   107\t| Claude Code | `claude-code` |\n   108\t| Codex CLI | `codex` |\n   109\t| OpenCode | `opencode` |\n   110\t| oh-my-pi | `omp` |\n   111\t| pi.dev | `pi` |\n   112\t| Hermes Agent | `hermes` |\n   113\t| OpenClaw | `openclaw` |\n   114\t| DeepSeek Harness, built into the Strata app | `deepseek-harness` |\n   115\t\n   116\t`strata connect` lists adapters. `strata connect <identifier>` prints settings;\n   117\t`strata connect <identifier> --check --output json` sends a prompt and a tool call and reports their outcomes.\n   118\tDo not mistake printed configuration or an older tested version for a successful check of the current setup.\n   119\tSee [connection instructions and limits](TERMINAL_LAUNCH.md#connect-your-apps-strata-connect).\n   120\t\n   121\t## Evidence and publishing\n   122\t\n   123\t- Cite the release, commit and relevant guide behind each compatibility claim.\n   124\t- Performance claims need the model, quantization, hardware, workload, engine version and source report.\n   125\t  Keep prompt processing, decode throughput and latency separate. Do not turn a screenshot into a benchmark.\n   126\t- [Community benchmarks](COMMUNITY_BENCHMARKS.md) include historical reports.\n   127\t  The [standard benchmark protocol](BENCHMARK_PROTOCOL.md) remains subject to its documented activation status;\n   128\t  do not label legacy commands or results as implementing it.\n   129\t- Mark missing or unverified information explicitly. Never invent measurements, test results or download links.\n   130\t- For contribution evidence, follow [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md). For video assets and narration,\n   131\t  follow [MEDIA_KIT.md](MEDIA_KIT.md).\n     1\t# Strata engine and server reference\n     2\t\n     3\tThis page is the technical reference for the Qwen engine and its server: measured speed, the HTTP API, the settings in\n     4\t`strata-<model>.json`, memory modes and the speed switches. It is for people who run Strata from a source checkout, tune\n     5\tan installation or connect their own software. It describes the current release. Dated measurements are in\n     6\t[History / older measurements](#history--older-measurements) at the end.\n     7\t\n     8\tNew here? Start with the [README](../README.md). Installing is in [INSTALL.md](INSTALL.md), the models are in\n     9\t[MODELS.md](MODELS.md), common problems are in [TROUBLESHOOTING.md](TROUBLESHOOTING.md). DeepSeek V4.1-Flash has its own\n    10\tengine and is covered in [MODELS.md](MODELS.md); nothing below applies to it unless stated.\n    11\t\n    12\t> **On this page:** [Quick start](#quick-start) · [Speed](#speed-measured) · [Which model?](#which-model) ·\n    13\t> [Windows](#windows) · [Linux](#linux) · [Memory modes](#memory-modes) · [Sharing the GPU](#sharing-the-gpu-with-other-programs-optional) ·\n    14\t> [API](#using-it) · [MCP tools](#tools-from-mcp-servers) · [MCP server](#manage-strata-from-your-ai-assistant-mcp-server) ·\n    15\t> [Images](#images-vision) · [Speed settings](#speed-settings) · [CPU workers](#cpu-workers-and-thread-placement) ·\n    16\t> [Per-card defaults](#rtx-30-series-sm_86-and-tesla-p100-sm_60-bitwise-decode-switches-on-by-default) ·\n    17\t> [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) · [How it works](#how-it-works) · [Credits](#credits-and-licenses) ·\n    18\t> [History](#history--older-measurements)\n    19\t\n    20\t**Which flags go where.** `strata launch` has its own flags (`strata launch --help`). The engine and server flags below go\n    21\tin the `\"args\"` list of `strata-<model>.json` (source setup writes it) or on the `serve/server.py` command line.\n    22\t`START-HERE.bat`, `SETUP.bat`, `./setup.sh` and `run-<model>` exist only in a [source checkout](SOURCE_INSTALL.md). An\n    23\tinstalled app has none of them.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Quick start\n    26\t\n    27\t1. Install and start a model: [INSTALL.md](INSTALL.md) (app or Terminal) or [SOURCE_INSTALL.md](SOURCE_INSTALL.md).\n    28\t2. Check that the server answers. The default address is `http://127.0.0.1:8080`:\n    29\t\n    30\t   ```sh\n    31\t   curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/health\n    32\t   curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/chat/completions -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    33\t     -d '{\"model\": \"strata\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a haiku about GPUs.\"}], \"max_tokens\": 512}'\n    34\t   ```\n    35\t\n    36\t3. Point your app at `http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1` with any API key, or run `strata connect` to list the apps Strata can configure.\n    37\t4. Change a setting by editing `strata-<model>.json` and restarting. Setup rewrites the file when you run it again and\n    38\t   keeps the keys it does not manage.\n    39\t\n    40\t---\n    41\t\n    42\t<a id=\"speed-measured\"></a>\n    43\t## Speed (measured)\n    44\t\n    45\t**Measured on:** RTX 5070 12 GB, Ryzen 5 7600 (6 cores), 64 GB DDR5-5200, Windows, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next ISTA-DASLab packs,\n    46\tengine 0.1.26 with the settings setup writes (`--prefill auto`, 8-bit KV above 4K, KV streaming from 64K). One\n    47\tcode-agent prompt per length, 256 generated tokens, MTP speculative decoding on. \"262K\" is the full context window (a\n    48\t259,943-token prompt). The IQ2_XS row used Swift 1.5's IQ2_XS, which runs at the original's speed.\n    49\t\n    50\t### Prompt processing (tokens/s)\n    51\t\n    52\t| Model | 1K | 4K | 32K | 64K | 128K | 262K |\n    53\t| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n    54\t| **Q2_0** | 536 | 1,299 | 2,171 | 2,126 | 2,107 | 1,304† |\n    55\t| **IQ2_XS** | 534 | 1,256 | 2,092 | 1,754 | 1,752 | 1,181*† |\n    56\t| **IQ3_XXS** | 482 | 1,007 | 1,745 | 1,609 | 1,602 | - |\n    57\t| **IQ3_S** | 427 | 913 | 1,624 | 1,640 | 1,443 | - |\n    58\t| **Coder** | 656 | 1,583 | 2,177 | 2,236 | 2,208 | 1,034** |\n    59\t\n    60\t† not measured again since 0.1.22. \\* measured with images on (the image encoder's VRAM reserve leaves fewer experts\n    61\tcached). \\*\\* not measured again since 0.1.14.\n    62\t\n    63\t### Output (tokens/s)\n    64\t\n    65\t| Model | 1K | 4K | 32K | 64K | 128K | 262K |\n    66\t| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n    67\t| **Q2_0** | 87.3 | 93.0 | 81.8 | 76.2 | 73.7 | 60.3† |\n    68\t| **IQ2_XS** | 79.6 | 78.6 | 76.3 | 63.7 | 62.7 | 52.8† |\n    69\t| **IQ3_XXS** | 61.9 | 61.6 | 58.5 | 57.2 | 49.0 | - |\n    70\t| **IQ3_S** | 52.4 | 53.3 | 48.3 | 46.3 | 45.5 | - |\n    71\t| **Coder** | 58.9 | 55.1 | 54.9 | 53.2 | 43.0 | 42.8† |\n    72\t\n    73\t† not measured again since 0.1.14. Raw data: `bench/results/2026-09-29-speed-0126`.\n    74\t\n    75\t- Output speed depends on the text. Speculative decoding runs faster when more drafted tokens are accepted, so a different\n    76\t  answer to the same prompt moves it by several percent.\n    77\t- Time to first token is prompt length divided by prompt speed. With Q2_0 that is about 4 s at 4K, 25 s at 32K, under\n    78\t  2 minutes at 128K and 4.5 minutes at 262K.\n    79\t- IQ3_XXS and IQ3_S at 262K are not measured. With 43 and 50 GB of experts, a 260K-token context brings a 64 GB PC to its\n    80\t  memory limit by setup's estimate, so setup recommends up to 128K there. A longer context that you choose is kept, with a note.\n    81\t- Other cards, other CPUs and newer engines differ. The 0.1.36 fused prompt kernels and later changes raised these\n    82\t  numbers on RTX 30 and newer cards: see [History](#history--older-measurements). For your own PC, run the benchmark in\n    83\t  [BENCHMARK_PROTOCOL.md](BENCHMARK_PROTOCOL.md).\n    84\t\n    85\tMore VRAM matters more than a faster GPU. Every extra GB holds about 700 more experts, and every expert on the GPU is one\n    86\tthe CPU does not have to compute. `tools/make_profile.py` builds an expert profile from your own prompts: run the engine\n    87\tonce with `--dump-routing trace.bin`, then see the tool's help. The shipped profile ranks every expert pair, so a trace\n    88\tchanges the order only with `--reorder` (your trace's pairs first, then the base's, then the rest).\n    89\t\n    90\t## Which model?\n    91\t\n    92\t[MODELS.md](MODELS.md) is the page for choosing a model: sizes, memory, providers and what fits. The families are\n    93\tDeepSeek V4.1-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (with its Coder, Swift, Gyro, Unsloth and NVFP4 variants) and Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\n    94\t([QWEN36.md](QWEN36.md)). The ISTA-DASLab Qwen3.8-Flash-Next packs are:\n    95\t\n    96\t| Model | Download | RAM it uses | Speed | Quality |\n    97\t| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n    98\t| **Q2_0** | 66 GB | ~34 GB experts + ~6 GB | fastest | good |\n    99\t| **IQ2_XS** | 68 GB | ~36 GB experts + ~6 GB | close to Q2_0 | a bit better |\n   100\t| **IQ3_XXS** | 76 GB | ~43 GB experts + ~6 GB | slower (more CPU work) | best of the three |\n   101\t\n   102\tIQ3_S is a fourth, larger pack: see [MODELS.md](MODELS.md#pick-by-memory). With 64 GB of RAM these three fit (close the browser\n   103\tfor IQ3_XXS and keep its context at 128K or less). With 48 GB only Q2_0 and IQ2_XS may fit. With 32 GB use the Coder.\n   104\t\n   105\t### Or: the Coder (half the experts, for code)\n   106\t\n   107\t[ISTA-DASLab's expert-pruned release](https://huggingface.co/ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-Coder-GGUF) keeps 256 of\n   108\teach layer's 512 experts (10 still active per token), chosen on code, agent and vision data. Shard 1 is 29.6 GB (23 GB of\n   109\texperts in RAM), so it runs on 32 GB of RAM. Images work. Install it with `strata launch --family coder`\n   110\t(from a source checkout: `START-HERE.bat --setup --family coder`). Details: [MODELS.md](MODELS.md#coder).\n   111\t\n   112\t### Or: Swift 1.5 (a fine-tune that thinks shorter)\n   113\t\n   114\t[Swift 1.5](https://huggingface.co/ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-GGUF) is UkisAI's fine-tune. It has the same\n   115\tarchitecture and speed as the original, its own vision encoder, and thinks for fewer tokens. Its authors recommend IQ2_XS.\n   116\tThe license is the Swift Open License 1.0. Install it with `strata launch --family swift --model iq2_xs`\n   117\t(from a source checkout: `START-HERE.bat --setup --family swift --model IQ2_XS`). Details: [MODELS.md](MODELS.md#swift-15).\n   118\t\n   119\t<a id=\"experimental-unsloths-ud-q4_k_xl\"></a>\n   120\t\n   121\t### Unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL\n   122\t\n   123\tA 4-bit quantization of the same model (111 GB, 72 GiB of experts). Install it with `--family unsloth --model UD-Q4_K_XL`\n   124\t(RAM budget: your RAM less 24 GB, the rest read from the SSD). What it does and the manual workflow are in\n   125\t[UNSLOTH_Q4.md](UNSLOTH_Q4.md).\n   126\t\n   127\t---\n   128\t\n   129\t## Windows\n   130\t\n   131\tOn Windows the source wrapper is `START-HERE.bat` ([source installation](SOURCE_INSTALL.md#from-source-windows)). For a\n   132\treleased app, run its installer and open Strata from the Start menu. For the Terminal, use `strata launch`. Watch the\n   133\tsetup plan and the startup log for what is actually chosen on your machine, and see [TROUBLESHOOTING.md](TROUBLESHOOTING.md)\n   134\tif a component is missing.\n   135\t\n   136\tTerminal chat from a checkout: `.venv\\Scripts\\python chat.py`.\n   137\t\n   138\t### Tuning for your PC (`--calibrate`)\n   139\t\n   140\tSome engine settings depend on the PC more than on the model:\n   141\t\n   142\t| Setting | What it controls |\n   143\t| --- | --- |\n   144\t| `--pcie-frac` | Share of the experts missing from VRAM that are copied to the GPU instead of computed by the CPU. A fast PCIe link and a slow CPU want more; a laptop's narrow link wants less. |\n   145\t| `--spec-min-p` | How sure the draft layer must be to add another guess to a check. |\n   146\t| `--pool-workers` | How many CPU threads compute experts. On CPUs with efficiency cores, fewer can be faster. |\n   147\t| `--adapt-every`, `--adapt-swaps`, `--adapt-decay` | How busily the VRAM expert cache follows the conversation. A CPU that reads missed experts slowly (DDR3 or DDR4 in few channels) gains from more swaps. |\n   148\t| `--prefill-pipe` | On two or more cards (layer split), the prompt chunks that keep the cards reading at once. See [MULTI_GPU.md](MULTI_GPU.md). |\n   149\t\n   150\tThe defaults were measured on a Ryzen 5 7600 with an RTX 5070. From a source checkout, `START-HERE.bat --calibrate` (Linux: `./setup.sh --calibrate`)\n   151\tmeasures output speed with each setting on your PC and keeps one only when it is more than 3% faster. The result is\n   152\tremembered per PC and model, and updates keep it. Measuring the worker count restarts the model more than once, so the PC\n   153\tis busy and can stop responding for a minute or two at each restart.\n   154\t\n   155\t### Running it at startup (Task Scheduler)\n   156\t\n   157\tTo have the model up at logon, start the server from Task Scheduler or a service. Windows throttles such contexts by\n   158\tdefault, and the expert load then crawls.\n   159\t\n   160\t**Measured on:** RTX 5070 Ti, Ryzen 7 9800X3D, NVMe, a ~40 GB expert load, same binary, arguments and cache state.\n   161\t\n   162\t| How the server starts | Expert load |\n   163\t| --- | ---: |\n   164\t| Double-click, terminal or SSH | 1.42-1.52 GiB/s (~35 s) |\n   165\t| Task Scheduler with its defaults | 0.05 GiB/s (821-841 s) |\n   166\t| Task Scheduler with the two settings below | 1.42 GiB/s (35 s) |\n   167\t\n   168\tIn the task's properties set both of these (the defaults are the opposite):\n   169\t\n   170\t- **Priority level: Normal** (Options tab; the default is Below normal).\n   171\t- **Run with highest privileges** (General tab). Without it the task runs with a limited token, which also removes\n   172\t  `SeLockMemoryPrivilege`, the privilege Windows large pages need.\n   173\t\n   174\tBoth were changed at once, so the effect of each alone is not measured. If the model still starts slowly, the engine prints a\n   175\thint under its `loaded ... GiB at ...` line.\n   176\t\n   177\t**Large pages need a new logon.** Granting \"Lock pages in memory\" (`secpol.msc`, User Rights Assignment) to the account\n   178\tthat runs the engine takes effect at the next logon. Log off and on after granting it. Until then the startup log says\n   179\tlarge pages were refused. `tools\\windows\\enable_large_pages.ps1` (run as Administrator) grants it to the current account.\n   180\t\n   181\t---\n   182\t\n   183\t## Linux\n   184\t\n   185\tThese notes are for **source setup**. Normal installs use [the Terminal or AppImage](INSTALL.md).\n   186\t\n   187\t```bash\n   188\t./setup.sh\n   189\t```\n   190\t\n   191\tSource setup offers choices for the detected machine and can install missing Debian or Ubuntu dependencies with `sudo apt`.\n   192\tIt starts the server on `http://127.0.0.1:8080` unless you configure another address or port. Later runs of `./setup.sh`\n   193\tor `./run-<model>.sh` start the model directly. The options are the same as on Windows (`./setup.sh --setup`,\n   194\t`--model Q2_0 --yes`, `--gguf-dir /data/Q2_0`). Terminal chat: `.venv/bin/python chat.py`. Building from git and trying a pull\n   195\trequest: [SOURCE_INSTALL.md](SOURCE_INSTALL.md#build-from-git-on-linux-to-try-a-pull-request-or-change-the-code).\n   196\t\n   197\t- **Updating:** `git pull`, then `./setup.sh`. It compiles the engine again when its source changed. If the compile\n   198\t  fails, it says so and starts the engine you had.\n   199\t- **Other distributions** (Arch, Fedora and others): install the C++ compiler and the CUDA Toolkit 13 with your package\n   200\t  manager first (Arch: `sudo pacman -S base-devel cuda`). Setup finds `nvcc` on `PATH`, in `/usr/local/cuda*` and in\n   201\t  `/opt/cuda*`.\n   202\t- **WSL** works (Ubuntu 24.04 tested) with one limit. The NVIDIA driver pins only about 1 GB there, so KV streaming\n   203\t  (`--kv-resident`) is off, the KV cache stays in VRAM and experts are copied from unpinned RAM, which makes prompts\n   204\t  slower than on native Linux.\n   205\t\n   206\t---\n   207\t\n   208\t### Downloading the model files yourself\n   209\t\n   210\tSetup downloads the model files over 16 connections (`STRATA_DL_CONNECTIONS` 1 to 32), resumes a stopped download, and checks every file's SHA-256 against the\n   211\tpinned value. Measured from two computers (a home PC and a server in China), anonymous downloads from Hugging Face ran at 16 to 19 MB/s whatever the connection count (one\n   212\tstream 7 MB/s, 16 connections 17, 32 connections 19). Hugging Face's own `hf` command with its Xet backend gave 16 to 24 MB/s anonymously in the same test, so it is\n   213\tnot faster by itself. Hugging Face limits anonymous requests per IP address and says a login (a token of your own account) raises the limits, so if the download is slow\n   214\tyou can fetch the file with the `hf` command, optionally after `hf auth login`, and give the folder to setup:\n   215\t\n   216\t```bash\n   217\tuvx hf auth login            # optional: your own Hugging Face account; Strata does not read or send this token\n   218\tuvx hf download agentionai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Gyro-GGUF Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Gyro-S-TQ1_0.gguf \\\n   219\t    --revision 1ada08422e404aca51ab9df665f089f34c419c6b --local-dir /data/gyro-s\n   220\t./setup.sh --gguf-dir /data/gyro-s       # Windows: SETUP.bat --gguf-dir D:\\gyro-s\n   221\t```\n   222\t\n   223\tUse the file names and the revision setup shows for your model (the revision above is the one this version pins for Gyro-S: another version of Strata may pin another).\n   224\tKeep the file's original name. Setup finds the files in `--gguf-dir`, checks their SHA-256 and does not download them again; a file that does not match the published one is\n   225\treported and, for files in your own folder, never deleted. Without `uvx`, `pip install -U huggingface_hub` gives the same `hf` command.\n   226\t\n   227\t## Memory modes\n   228\t\n   229\t### KV cache\n   230\t\n   231\t| Option | What it does |\n   232\t| --- | --- |\n   233\t| `--kv int8` (default) | 8-bit KV cache. |\n   234\t| `--kv q4_0` | Half the KV memory with a Hadamard rotation before 4-bit rounding. About 4% faster at 128K, measurably less precise on long documents (perplexity +8-12%; needle tests still pass). Setup asks above 8K context. |\n   235\t| `--kv k8v4` | Keys at 8 bits, values as rotated 4-bit: 23% less KV memory than 8-bit, so more experts fit in VRAM. More precise than `q4_0` on long documents. Prompts are 2-5% slower. |\n   236\t| `--kv-streaming on\\|off\\|auto` | Keeps the context's KV cache in RAM and only the part attention reads in VRAM (`--kv-resident 32768`), so more experts fit on the GPU. Costs about 13.7 KB of RAM per context token (1.7 GB at 128K). Setup turns it on from 64K when the RAM has room; `on` overrides the RAM test with a note. Never under WSL. |\n   237\t| `--kv-grow` (or `STRATA_KV_GROW=1`; `--no-kv-grow` turns it off) | The KV takes VRAM only for the cells the requests reach; the expert cache holds the rest and gives slots back as the context grows. Needs one GPU, an expert profile, the whole KV in VRAM and every expert in RAM. Otherwise the engine says so and stays off. On HIP it uses virtual memory management (`STRATA_HIP_VMM=0` turns that off). It pays on cards of about 24 GB or less with contexts of 128K and more. |\n   238\t\n   239\t`STRATA_KV_HOST_REGISTER=1` forces the KV host copy to be pinned with `cudaHostRegister` (the order WSL2 uses\n   240\tautomatically); `0` forces `cudaHostAlloc` first.\n   241\t\n   242\t### Low-RAM modes (chosen by setup)\n   243\t\n   244\tNormally all of a model's experts are copied into RAM (23-50 GB, pinned) and the GPU holds a copy of the most-used ones.\n   245\tWhen the RAM cannot hold them beside the system (the experts plus about 10 GB), setup picks one of these:\n   246\t\n   247\t| Mode | Flag | What it does |\n   248\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   249\t| Mapped | `--mmap-experts` | Maps the experts from one file in the model's folder (the pack's `experts.bin`, +23-50 GB of disk), or from the GGUF files themselves for native packs that have no `experts.bin`. The OS file cache holds what the GPU does not and can give that memory back. With a small GPU most experts come from the SSD and it is much slower; setup says so. |\n   250\t| Resident | `--resident-experts` | When the experts the GPU does not hold fit the RAM, the engine copies exactly those into RAM at start. Nothing is read from the SSD while it answers. |\n   251\t| RAM budget | `--resident-budget-gib N` | The N GiB of experts the GPU does not hold that the profile ranks hottest are copied into RAM; the rest are read from the files. Implies `--mmap-experts` and leaves 4 GiB of headroom. On Windows the available commit limit also caps N, and a clamped budget is reported. This is what runs [Unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL](UNSLOTH_Q4.md) on a 64 GB PC. |\n   252\t\n   253\t- In a source checkout, `START-HERE.bat --setup --low-ram on|off` overrides the choice, and `--low-ram resident|mmap` forces one variant.\n   254\t- The resident variant keeps 4 GB of the RAM it finds free (`STRATA_RESIDENT_HEADROOM_GIB`). When even the missing experts do\n   255\t  not fit, it says so and runs the mapped mode. The server log shows per request how many expert reads went to the file\n   256\t  (`resident RAM: ... blob reads from the file`, 0 in steady use).\n   257\t- The adaptive tier (`--adapt-every`) keeps following the conversation in the resident mode. `--adapt-async 1` (opt-in,\n   258\t  `--serve`) moves the swaps between decode windows on a helper thread. With `--batch` slots it works between their\n   259\t  windows too, but not with `--batch-groups` or `--peer-device`. It is not bit-exact from run to run.\n   260\t- `STRATA_RAM_ADAPT=<n>` (opt-in, `--serve`, one GPU, needs `--adapt-every`): the RAM copy also follows the conversation.\n   261\t  A layer swaps up to 4 pairs per window: the experts the windows keep routing to (decayed count 2 or more) take the\n   262\t  RAM places of unused experts of the same layer (count below 0.5), largest difference first. The incoming expert was\n   263\t  usually read for the window before; when its blob still sits in that read's stage buffer it is copied from there and\n   264\t  no file read is made. `n` caps only the pairs that need a file read (`=1` is enough in practice). The log counts how\n   265\t  many experts moved and how many came out of a stage buffer.\n   266\t- Several GPUs: setup recommends one GPU in the low-RAM mode and asks. `--gpus 0,1` shares the model across both cards\n   267\t  ([MULTI_GPU.md](MULTI_GPU.md#using-it)). `--yes` keeps one GPU.\n   268\t- With a page-locked copy the GPU also takes its share of the misses over PCIe (`--pcie-frac`). `--pcie-frac 0` (or\n   269\t  `STRATA_RESIDENT_PIN=0`) gives the mapped mode's exact tokens.\n   270\t- Linux, opt-in: `STRATA_ARENA_MMAP=1` maps a native pack's expert arena read-only from `experts.bin` instead of reading\n   271\t  it into locked RAM (for example 2x 16 GB GPUs with 32 GB of RAM). Run it with `--pcie-frac 0`.\n   272\t- Windows, opt-in: `STRATA_FILE_RELEASE=1` trims the file-backed pages of experts after their GPU uploads. It is a\n   273\t  working-set hint, not an unmap, and it does not reduce committed memory or SSD reads\n   274\t  ([measurements and limits](../bench/results/2026-10-04-windows-mapped-release/README.md)).\n   275\t- Windows, opt-in: `STRATA_PIN_BY_NEED=1` (arena on one GPU) registers the profile's non-GPU-resident experts by rank up to\n   276\t  `STRATA_ARENA_PIN_GIB` (29 by default on Windows) instead of the first ~30 GiB.\n   277\t- Windows CUDA keeps 640 MiB then 160 MiB of page-lockable memory free while the arena pins (`STRATA_PIN_RESERVE_MIB=N`, `0`\n   278\t  turns it off, and it sets the reserve on any OS). If a required buffer is still refused, the error ends with\n   279\t  `[pinned-memory-exhausted]` and names the `STRATA_ARENA_PIN_GIB` to start with.\n   280\t- `STRATA_EXCHANGE_ROTATE=1` (opt-in): an adaptive swap hands buffer ownership over instead of copying the evicted blob into\n   281\t  the RAM copy. Same tokens, fewer host copies, about 1.4 GiB more page-locked RAM. See\n   282\t  [EXCHANGE_ROTATION.md](EXCHANGE_ROTATION.md).\n   283\t\n   284\t### Linux file tier\n   285\t\n   286\tWhen experts are read from GGUF files in place, Linux runs three things by default. Off switches are in brackets.\n   287\t\n   288\t| Part | Variable | What it does |\n   289\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   290\t| Zero-copy | `STRATA_COLD_OVERLAP=4` (`0` = off) | An expert whose three tensors' pages are all in the page cache is computed straight from the mapping, with no staging copy. |\n   291\t| io prefetch | `STRATA_IO_PREFETCH=1` (`0` = off) | I/O threads read the layer's uncached experts and the router-predicted ones of the next layers with whole-blob `pread`s into the page cache. Tunables: `STRATA_LOOKAHEAD_K`, `STRATA_IO_PREFETCH_DEPTH` (2), `STRATA_IO_PF_THREADS` (8), `STRATA_IO_PF_STAGE=1`, `STRATA_IO_PF_AHEAD=0`. |\n   292\t| Decode gate | `STRATA_IO_PF_GATE=1` (`0` = off) | Every 16 decode windows the read-ahead is judged. It runs only while the page cache has room for the experts (off below 0.55, on above 0.65: `STRATA_IO_PF_GATE_LO` and `_HI`) and while it pays (`STRATA_IO_PF_GATE_USEFUL`). |\n   293\t\n   294\tFor an `experts.bin` pack, io prefetch and the gate switch on when the page cache has room for at least 55% of the experts\n   295\t(the tightest cgroup limit or `MemAvailable`, asked once at start). Below that the pack reads as before and the log says so;\n   296\t`STRATA_IO_PREFETCH=1` forces it. The container RAM check counts a cgroup's clean page cache as available. The startup log\n   297\tnames what is on. Windows and macOS are unchanged. On a Radeon 780M iGPU, prefetch together with the adaptive tier under\n   298\tmemory pressure reset the GPU in most runs; with `--adapt-every 100000` there were none, and the cause is not found.\n   299\t\n   300\tThe weights and the MTP draft files are asked for ahead of their reads at start (madvise and `posix_fadvise` WILLNEED, 128 KiB\n   301\tsteps), which matters on a slow drive: `STRATA_READ_AHEAD=0` turns it off, `STRATA_FILL_AHEAD=N` sets how many fill pairs\n   302\tare requested ahead (default 256).\n   303\t\n   304\tThe prompt chunk follows the host's memory (Linux, file tier, on by default). When the page cache is smaller than the file\n   305\tset, a planner picks the chunk and ring with the fewest predicted bytes read per prompt from the RAM copy, the page cache that\n   306\tis left, the drive class and the PCIe link. It prints `chunk 8192 -> 3840, ring 96 -> 96: ...` when it changes the pick and\n   307\t`chunk 8192 kept: ...` when it does not. It does nothing when the GPU holds every expert, when the file set fits the cache,\n   308\tand on Windows. `STRATA_PREFILL_HOST_PLAN=0` turns it off; `STRATA_SSD_GBPS` overrides the drive speed;\n   309\t`--prefill N` and `STRATA_PREFILL_RING` always win.\n   310\t\n   311\t### Reading the tiers\n   312\t\n   313\tWith `--stats` the engine prints the tiers of the decode (`expert tiers`: blobs from the RAM copy, blobs and MB from the\n   314\tfiles, read time; `routing prefetch`: how many file reads had been warmed). The server log has the same per request, and\n   315\t`GET /metrics` lists `ram_blobs`, `file_blobs`, `file_mb`, `drafts_offered` and `drafts_accepted` per recent request, with\n   316\t`totals` since the start. `hit_rate` is the VRAM share of the experts looked up; experts the GPU reads over PCIe\n   317\t(`--pcie-frac`) are not in it, so a higher `--pcie-frac` raises it even when decoding gets slower. `pcie_share` is their\n   318\tshare of all routed experts. The Monitor tab shows both. The request list in the Monitor tab also shows the prompt's own speed: what the cache already held is not read\n   319\tagain, so it is the fresh tokens (`prompt_tokens` minus `reused`) over the prompt time (`prompt_ms`), and a request that read no\n   320\tnew token shows no number.\n   321\t\n   322\tProfiling: start the server with `STRATA_DECODE_TIMING=1` (and `STRATA_VERIFY_PROFILE=1` for the GPU side). After each request\n   323\tthe engine log has a `strata decode timing:` line and a `strata decode GPU stages (ms/window):` line. The GPU profile slows\n   324\tdecoding a little: use it to compare, not to measure speed. `STRATA_LOOKAHEAD_STATS=1` scores the next-layer expert\n   325\tprediction every 100 windows and costs CPU. `tools/replay_cache_policy.py` replays a `--dump-routing` trace through the\n   326\tadaptive tier's swap policy offline.\n   327\t\n   328\tIn the serial serve loop the timing line lists the wall-time parts in the order they run: `adapt wait + verify +\n   329\tcommit/emit + draft + adapt join + other`. `other` is the decode time minus the named parts, not a step of its own.\n   330\tThe times are host wall times, averaged over every decode window, and the bracketed parts of verify are already inside\n   331\tverify. The pipelined decode keeps the older line (`ms/window = verify ...`) and batch slots are not covered, so a script\n   332\tthat reads this line must accept `ms/window = adapt wait` for a serial run.\n   333\t\n   334\t### Draft layer\n   335\t\n   336\t| Option | Effect |\n   337\t| --- | --- |\n   338\t| `--mtp` | Use the model's MTP draft layer (setup passes it). The server also runs without it: drafts then come from the suffix and prompt-lookup drafter only, every token is still verified, and the draft layer's ~0.7-1 GiB of VRAM goes to the expert cache. |\n   339\t| `--spec N`, `--spec-min-p P` | Draft depth and the confidence needed to add a guess. Setup's default is `--spec 4 --spec-min-p 0.5`. |\n   340\t| `--draft-vocab en\\|cyrillic\\|fr` (setup) | The draft layer can only propose tokens from a subset of the vocabulary. The default subset includes every Chinese, Japanese and Korean token. `en` keeps the English and code subset (~110 MiB less VRAM, English 1-2% faster, CJK gets almost no drafts). `cyrillic` and `fr` add those scripts. `tools/draft_vocab.py` builds and inspects subsets. |\n   341\t| `--lookup-chain-min M`, `--mtp-hnorm stream`, `--mtp-draft-vocab FILE` | Finer controls: the shortest context match `--lookup-chain` extends (default 3), one norm per stream, and a token subset of your own. |\n   342\t\n   343\tWhen the start stops with \"the draft head does not fit\" (a 12 GB card with a long context), the engine says how much it needs\n   344\tand which smaller subset fits. Setup suggests `--draft-vocab en` on cards under 14 GB; nothing changes unless you pass it.\n   345\t\n   346\t**Reproducible output.** With the IQ models the CPU rounds one-token expert groups and multi-token groups differently, so the\n   347\tsame prompt at temperature 0 can end in a different, equally good answer when drafting, cache state or a resumed conversation\n   348\tdiffer. `STRATA_IQ_MT_MIN=1` uses the multi-token kernels for every group, so the answer no longer depends on drafting (IQ3_S\n   349\tdecode -1 to -3% on a Ryzen 7600). For byte-identical repeats also add `--prompt-cache 0 --adapt-swaps 0 --pcie-frac 0` to\n   350\tthe engine args. `--pcie-frac 0` costs decode speed, so keep it for A/B runs.\n   351\t\n   352\t---\n   353\t\n   354\t## Sharing the GPU with other programs (optional)\n   355\t\n   356\tBy default the model stays loaded until you close Strata. Three server options, all off by default and also keys in\n   357\t`strata-<model>.json`, give the VRAM back:\n   358\t\n   359\t| Option | Config key | What it does |\n   360\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   361\t| `--idle-unload 600` | `\"idle_unload_s\": 600` | Unload the model after 600 s without requests. The next request loads it again. |\n   362\t| `--min-free-vram-mib 11000` | `\"min_free_vram_mib\": 11000` | Load an unloaded model only when that much VRAM is free (it waits up to 15 s for memory being given back). Otherwise answer **503** \"the GPU is in use by another program\". With several GPUs it checks the first one. |\n   363\t| `--before-load \"cmd\"` | `\"before_load\": \"cmd\"` or `[\"cmd\", \"arg\"]` | A command run before the model is loaded again, for example one that unloads another server's model. |\n   364\t| | `\"before_load_timeout_s\": 120` | How long that command may run. When it fails or times out, the free VRAM decides: with `min_free_vram_mib` set and not enough free, the request gets **503** with `Retry-After`; otherwise the model loads with a warning. `\"before_load_strict\": true` always refuses on a failure; `\"before_load_ignore_failure\": true` always loads. |\n   365\t\n   366\t`POST /unload` unloads now (`409` while a request is running; `?wait=1` waits for the running turn, up to 300 s, or\n   367\t`?wait=<seconds>`). `POST /load` loads ahead of a request. Both need `Content-Type: application/json`:\n   368\t\n   369\t```sh\n   370\tcurl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" localhost:8080/unload\n   371\t```\n   372\t\n   373\t`/health` says `\"loaded\"`, `/v1/models` lists the model as `unloaded` while it is, `/props` sets `is_sleeping` and the\n   374\tMonitor shows the state. Unloading ends the engine process and the image encoder, so their VRAM and RAM return at once. The\n   375\tmodel files stay in the OS file cache, so loading again takes seconds while that RAM is not needed elsewhere.\n   376\t\n   377\t**Lazy start.** `serve/server.py --engine strata --config strata-<model>.json --lazy` (or `\"lazy_load\": true`) starts the HTTP\n   378\tAPI without the engine or the vision encoder. The first generation request or `POST /load` loads them, including\n   379\t`before_load` and `min_free_vram_mib`. `POST /v1/load` and `/v1/unload` are JSON control aliases (they need the API key,\n   380\t`application/json` and no foreign browser Origin, and return **409** while a request is active or queued). `/api/health`\n   381\taliases `/health`, and `/v1/status` exposes `loaded` and `auto_load`.\n   382\t\n   383\t**Giving part of the VRAM back while it keeps serving** (opt-in, one NVIDIA GPU, [VRAM_ELASTIC.md](VRAM_ELASTIC.md)). With\n   384\t`\"vram_elastic\": true` (engine flag `--vram-elastic`) the expert cache is allocated in 512 MiB segments\n   385\t(`\"vram_segment_mib\"`). `POST /v1/vram` with `{\"reserve_mib\": 8000}` shrinks it between requests until that much VRAM is free;\n   386\t`{\"reserve_mib\": null}` grows it back toward its full size, and `{\"reserve_mib\": 0}` takes all of it back. The experts of the\n   387\tsegments given back are computed on the CPU, so answers keep coming, slower. Nothing resizes on its own. Not with a layer\n   388\tsplit, the helper caches, `--peer-device` or the resident low-RAM mode.\n   389\t\n   390\t**Keep what the expert cache learned across restarts** (opt-in). `\"expert_profile_save\": \"expert-profile-learned.bin\"` saves\n   391\tthe experts in VRAM, then the counted routing, then the shipped order, on a clean exit and every 10 minutes between\n   392\trequests (`\"expert_profile_save_every\": 5` for another interval, `0` for exit only). The next start begins from it when it\n   393\tis a profile of the same model. A relative path is in the Strata folder. The file is a fingerprint of what you used the model\n   394\tfor and stays on your PC. A profile learned from one kind of text speeds that kind up and can slow others, so keep one file\n   395\tper kind of work. Setup rewrites the config when run again, so add the key again then.\n   396\t\n   397\t### The expert cache size is a budget\n   398\t\n   399\t`--expert-cache N` (`\"expert_cache\": N`) is `N` times the largest expert blob. The engine compares it only with the free VRAM\n   400\tit reads before the slots are written. `--expert-cache auto` (the default) checks again once the slots are written and\n   401\tshrinks if they do not fit. Under Windows an over-sized fixed cache does not fail. The driver pages the excess to system\n   402\tmemory, the start looks normal, and only the speed collapses (see the history table for one measurement).\n   403\t\n   404\t- The startup line `... MiB of VRAM free with everything loaded` is the tell. Below about 256 MiB it adds\n   405\t  `LOW: requests may stall; add --vram-reserve-mib N` with the value that would have left room.\n   406\t- Keep the cache on `auto`, or keep an explicit size and raise `--vram-reserve-mib`, which the engine deducts before it sizes\n   407\t  the cache.\n   408\t- That figure is read before the verify windows are captured, so it moves down by tens of MiB per captured window or `--batch`\n   409\t  layout. Each request re-reads the free VRAM and prints `WARNING: only N MiB of VRAM free at the start of this request` below\n   410\t  1 GiB on Windows (512 MiB elsewhere), with the `--vram-reserve-mib` that would leave room. The fix is a larger\n   411\t  `--vram-reserve-mib`, a smaller `--max-context` or `--batch`, or the NVIDIA setting `CUDA - Sysmem Fallback Policy` set to\n   412\t  `Prefer No Sysmem Fallback` for the engine's executable.\n   413\t- Performance Monitor's `GPU Process Memory` counters did not separate fast from slow runs because they count deliberately\n   414\t  pinned memory. Use the free line in the log.\n   415\t- A stall named \"request\" can be the OS paging the engine's own memory while it parks the outgoing conversation (the stage\n   416\t  reads `request: parking the previous conversation`). The cure is memory: a smaller `--resident-budget-gib`, a smaller\n   417\t  `--conversation-cache-mib`, or the pagefile on an SSD.\n   418\t\n   419\t---\n   420\t\n   421\t## Using it\n   422\t\n   423\tThe server listens on `http://127.0.0.1:8080` (`--port` in setup, or edit the run script).\n   424\t\n   425\t| API | Endpoint |\n   426\t| --- | --- |\n   427\t| OpenAI Chat Completions (stream and non-stream, tools) | `POST /v1/chat/completions` |\n   428\t| Anthropic Messages (stream and non-stream, tools) | `POST /v1/messages` |\n   429\t| OpenAI Responses (stream and non-stream, tools; stateless, [below](#the-responses-api-and-codex-cli)) | `POST /v1/responses` |\n   430\t| Model list and health | `GET /v1/models`, `GET /models`, `GET /health` |\n   431\t| Model properties | `GET /props` (also accepts `?model=<loaded-model-id>`) |\n   432\t| What the model is doing now | `GET /status`, `GET /slots` (busy or idle, with `n_prompt_tokens`; one entry per slot with `--batch`) |\n   433\t| Save or restore the conversation to a file | `POST /slots/0?action=save\\|restore` ([session files](#session-files-disk)) |\n   434\t| Everything the Monitor tab shows | `GET /metrics` (JSON) |\n   435\t| The same for Prometheus with vLLM's metric names | `GET /metrics` with `Accept: text/plain` or `?format=prometheus` |\n   436\t| Request totals in llama-server's metric names | `GET /metrics/prometheus` |\n   437\t| The MCP servers, their state and tools ([below](#tools-from-mcp-servers)) | `GET /mcp` |\n   438\t| API request monitor ([below](#api-request-monitor)) | `GET /api-monitor`, `GET /api/requests` |\n   439\t\n   440\t```python\n   441\tfrom openai import OpenAI\n   442\tclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\", api_key=\"none\")\n   443\tr = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"strata\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}])\n   444\tprint(r.choices[0].message.content)\n   445\t```\n   446\t\n   447\t- **Prometheus.** With `Accept: text/plain` the metrics use vLLM's names (`vllm:num_requests_running`, `vllm:kv_cache_usage_perc`,\n   448\t  token and request counters, `vllm:prefix_cache_queries_total` and `_hits_total`, `vllm:spec_decode_num_draft_tokens_total`\n   449\t  and `_accepted_tokens_total`, and the time-to-first-token, inter-token and end-to-end histograms), so dashboards written\n   450\t  for vLLM read this server. Strata's own facts come under `strata:` and are named after their JSON key (for example\n   451\t  `strata:live_tok_s`, `strata:last_hit_rate`, `strata:gpu_util`, `strata:ram_used_bytes`). A value the server does not have\n   452\t  has no sample. With an API key, the scraper sends it as a bearer token. Ready-made files are in `docs/monitoring/`:\n   453\t  `prometheus.yml`, `servicemonitor.yaml` and `grafana-strata.json`.\n   454\t- **Metadata.** `/models` and `/v1/models` list only the loaded model, with its context limit and input modalities. `/props` exposes\n   455\t  the chat template, context limit, configured generation defaults, model path and engine version. `n_predict: -1` means no\n   456\t  fixed output cap. `/slots` uses llama.cpp's names (`n_ctx`, `n_prompt_tokens`). `total_slots` is the number of `--batch` slots\n   457\t  (1 without). These endpoints require the API key when one is configured and never load, unload or restart models.\n   458\t- **`chat_template_caps`** (llama.cpp's field names) is checked by rendering small requests when the template loads. A rejected\n   459\t  feature is `false`. `supports_tools`, `supports_tool_calls`, `supports_parallel_tool_calls`, `supports_system_role` and\n   460\t  `supports_preserve_reasoning` are hints for clients, not a promise that the model follows a request. Zed's llama.cpp provider\n   461\t  reads this object to offer tools.\n   462\t\n   463\t### Thinking levels: none, low, medium, high\n   464\t\n   465\tThe model thinks before it answers (streamed as `reasoning_content`; Anthropic: `thinking` blocks). Choose how much per request\n   466\tin the chat page, in `chat.py` (`/think low`) or over the API:\n   467\t\n   468\t| API | How |\n   469\t| --- | --- |\n   470\t| OpenAI | `\"reasoning_effort\": \"none\" \\| \"low\" \\| \"medium\" \\| \"high\"` (also `\"reasoning\": {\"effort\": ...}` or `\"chat_template_kwargs\": {\"enable_thinking\": false}`) |\n   471\t| Anthropic | `\"output_config\": {\"effort\": \"low\" \\| \"medium\" \\| \"high\"}`, `\"thinking\": {\"type\": \"disabled\"}`, or `\"thinking\": {\"type\": \"enabled\", \"budget_tokens\": N}` (under 2K = low, under 8K = medium, more = high; also the hard budget) |\n   472\t\n   473\tWithout a setting the model uses its own default, **high**. `none` answers at once. The levels are instructions the model was\n   474\ttrained with, not a hard token limit.\n   475\t\n   476\t### Config keys (`strata-<model>.json`, outside `\"args\"`)\n   477\t\n   478\tAll are optional. Setup rewrites the config when run again and keeps keys it does not manage. The About tab's Model settings\n   479\tcard edits some of them (the sampling defaults, `reasoning_budget_tokens`, `fit_max_tokens`, `anthropic_thinking`,\n   480\t`effort_position`, `aliases`, `idle_unload_s`, `lazy_load`, `engine_silence_s`, `api_monitor`, `open_browser` and\n   481\t`--vram-reserve-mib`). An empty field removes the key, the earlier file is kept as `strata-<model>.json.bak`, and the change\n   482\tapplies at the next start. Network, key, MCP and program keys are not editable there.\n   483\t\n   484\t| Key | Default | Effect |\n   485\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   486\t| `reasoning_effort` | model default (high) | Level for requests that name none. The engine has no such option; `--reasoning-effort` in `\"args\"` is moved here with a note. A request's level and the web page's shared Chat settings win. |\n   487\t| `reasoning_budget_tokens` | `0` (no budget) | Caps the thinking at N tokens: the server ends it with a short wrap-up line and `</think>` and the model answers from there. The wrap-up counts as output tokens. A request's own `reasoning_budget_tokens` (or llama.cpp's `thinking_budget_tokens`) wins. |\n   488\t| `answer_room` | on | A request with thinking on, no budget and `max_tokens` of 1,024 or more gets one: thinking is wrapped up when a quarter of the cap (at least 512 tokens) is left. Requests without `max_tokens` get three quarters of the context. `false` turns it off. Clients should not cap the reply far below what the context allows. |\n   489\t| `reasoning_close_retry` | off | Closes the thinking once and continues when a reply ended inside its thinking with no answer and no tool call (#1053). |\n   490\t| `reasoning_loop_recovery` | off | `\"stop\"` ends, `\"recover\"` (or `true`) restarts once, a reply whose reasoning repeats whole passages. Checked every 512 output tokens. It can mistake repeated useful code for a loop, so keep the answer quality alongside the completion rate when testing it (#728). |\n   491\t| `repeat_stop_tokens` | `256` | A reply that repeats one token this many times in a row ends as `\"length\"`. `0` turns it off. |\n   492\t| `effort_position` | `\"start\"` | `\"end\"` puts a `low` or `medium` effort in a short system turn right before the answer, so a request that only changes the effort reuses the conversation cache. How well the model follows a level in that place is not measured. |\n   493\t| `anthropic_thinking` | as the template | `\"on_request\"` renders a `/v1/messages` request that asks for no thinking without thinking, as Anthropic does. Claude Code's short helper calls need it. |\n   494\t| `tool_call_recovery` | on (since 0.2.8; off in 0.2.7 and before) | Also reads a declared tool's call written as `<parameter=NAME>`, as JSON inside `<tool_call>`, as a `<function=NAME>` block without `<tool_call>`, or as a second call inside one `<tool_call>`. On 1,462 agent turns 98.3% read as intended with it and 92.9% without; the 49 specimens of the test corpus read the same with it on or off. Only a declared tool's call is ever recovered; anything else stays text. `\"tool_call_recovery\": false` turns it off. |\n   495\t| `env_to_user` | off | Moves opencode's `<env>` block (working directory, date) to the first user message so the system turn is the same for every session and the disk prefix cache restores it. |\n   496\t| `return_progress` | off | Per request `\"return_progress\": true` adds llama.cpp's `prompt_progress` (`total`, `cache`, `processed`, `time_ms`) to the stream, one update per `--prefill` chunk. A prompt shorter than one chunk sends nothing. |\n   497\t| `prevent_sleep` | off | Windows only: keeps the PC awake while a request runs or waits (`SetThreadExecutionState`). On Linux and macOS one startup line says it does nothing. |\n   498\t| `sampling` | the model card | Defaults for requests that leave sampling fields out, for example `{\"temperature\": 1.0, \"top_p\": 0.95, \"top_k\": 20}`. |\n   499\t| `aliases` | none | Extra model names listed in `/v1/models`, like llama-server's `--alias`. Any other name is still served. |\n   500\t| `fit_max_tokens` | `true` | A request whose `max_tokens` runs past the context gets it shortened to the room left. `false` (or `--no-fit-max-tokens`) returns 400 instead. A prompt that leaves no room is always refused. |\n   501\t| `engine_silence_s` | `300` | Ends the engine after this many seconds without a line during a request (`0` = wait forever). While a prompt is read, each chunk may take three times the previous one's time. |\n   502\t| `parallel` | `1` | Up to N requests decoded together in batch slots ([BATCHING.md](BATCHING.md)). |\n   503\t| `api_monitor` | off | Records requests for `/api-monitor`. |\n   504\t| `lazy_load` | off | Start without loading the model. |\n   505\t| `slot_save_path` | none | Enables session files. |\n   506\t| `host`, `api_key`, `cors_origins`, `trusted_origins`, `allowed_hosts` | local only | See [Network access](#network-access). |\n   507\t| `codex_thread_titles`, `codex_compaction_cache` | off | See [Codex CLI](#the-responses-api-and-codex-cli). |\n   508\t| `mcp_servers`, `mcp` | none | See [Tools from MCP servers](#tools-from-mcp-servers). |\n   509\t\n   510\t### Streaming and tool calls\n   511\t\n   512\t- With `\"stream\": true` everything arrives as it is made: thinking, answer and tool calls (the tool's name first, then its\n   513\t  arguments piece by piece). While the model reads a long prompt the stream sends keep-alives. The server window prints progress\n   514\t  every 15 s and `GET /status` says what it is doing. Closing the connection or pressing stop in your app stops the model.\n   515\t- A tool call counts only when it names a tool the request declared. A namespaced tool (`mcp__filesystem.write_file`) is\n   516\t  that call when the model writes `mcp__filesystem__write_file`, `mcp__filesystem=write_file`, `mcp__filesystem_write_file`,\n   517\t  or the bare member (`write_file`) and exactly one declared tool has that member. A name with `__` in it (`mcp__playwright__browser_navigate`)\n   518\t  is also that call when the model writes every `__` as `_` (`mcp_playwright_browser_navigate`), unless two declared tools collapse to the same\n   519\t  spelling. Any other call, or any call in a request that declared no tools, comes back as the text it was written as. Names are never\n   520\t  guessed: `bash` is not `shell`, and a reordered name (`server1_label_values` for `label_values_mcp_server1`) is not the declared one.\n   521\t  A call whose envelope drifted by a token (`<=NAME>`,\n   522\t  `<function:NAME>` or `<NAME>` instead of `<function=NAME>`) is read as the call when NAME is a declared tool.\n   523\t- **What the parser repairs, and what it leaves.** A string argument that quotes a whole tool call, or repeats its own\n   524\t  `</parameter><parameter=NAME>`, stays exact (#4078). A call whose `</function>` was left out is read as the call (#4427), in the\n   525\t  answer and inside the thinking. An argument the tool declares as an array or an object, or declares nothing for, is read as\n   526\t  written when it has a slip in it: a trailing or doubled comma, single quotes or `True`/`False`/`None`, a bare string between two\n   527\t  members of an object (`\"status\": \"pending\", \"in_progress\", \"pending\": false`), brackets left open at the end. The server window\n   528\t  says `N tool call argument(s) repaired` and `/metrics/prometheus` counts them in `strata:tool_args_repaired_total`. Text that\n   529\t  is not an array or an object, a string cut short and a string-typed parameter are never touched. After the thinking has ended at\n   530\t  a closing think tag, a second one at the start of a line in the answer is dropped (the model quoted the tag while thinking, #4077):\n   531\t  the call that follows is kept. The reasoning between the two went out as the answer already, because a stream cannot take text\n   532\t  back.\n   533\t- **A call that came back as text because its name was not declared (#3796).** The text stays exactly as the model wrote it and\n   534\t  `finish_reason` stays `stop` (or `end_turn` on `/v1/messages`). Next to it the reply carries a list, only when there is something in it:\n   535\t  `\"strata_rejected_tool_calls\": [{\"name\": \"bash\", \"reason\": \"undeclared\"}]` (at the top level of a Chat Completions reply, in\n   536\t  its last streamed chunk, and in the `message_delta` event and the message of `/v1/messages`; up to five names). A client that\n   537\t  wants to recover can answer the model with the tool names it does have and ask again; Strata never runs or forwards a call for a\n   538\t  tool the request did not declare. The server window prints the same names (`N <tool_call> block(s) ... went back as text`), and\n   539\t  `/metrics/prometheus` counts the blocks in `strata:tool_calls_unread_total`. A block that is malformed (not an undeclared name) is counted\n   540\t  there and in the window, but has no name to list.\n   541\t- **Anthropic's tool search on `/v1/messages` (#4663).** A tool with `\"defer_loading\": true` is declared but not listed in the\n   542\t  prompt; a `tool_reference` block in a `tool_result` (a tool search's result) puts that tool's definition into the prompt where it\n   543\t  stands, in the form the tool list uses, so loading a tool leaves the prompt's start, and the prompt cache, unchanged. Any other\n   544\t  reference is left out, as before (a tool whose MCP server is gone must not fail the session). A request with a deferred tool holds back more tools than\n   545\t  it declares (Claude Code declares ToolSearch and a `DeferredToolPlaceholder` and names its MCP tools in the conversation), so\n   546\t  there a call for an undeclared name is returned as a `tool_use`: Claude Code runs it, where text ended the turn.\n   547\t- JSON output: `response_format: {\"type\":\"json_object\"}` or `{\"type\":\"json_schema\", ...}` on `/v1/chat/completions`. The schema\n   548\t  must accept only JSON objects at its root (`\"type\":\"object\"`, or `anyOf`/`oneOf` of object schemas). Local `#` references\n   549\t  work and remote ones are refused. This is schema prompting followed by server validation, not grammar-constrained\n   550\t  decoding, with one generation per request. `jsonschema` is used when installed (`python -m pip install \"jsonschema>=4.23,<5\"`);\n   551\t  without it the answer is only checked to be one JSON object. Malformed output returns **502** with\n   552\t  `error.code: structured_output_failed`, an invalid schema **400**. JSON formats combined with tools or MCP are refused.\n   553\t  Streaming buffers the answer and emits content only after validation. Math answers often hold LaTeX such as `\\alpha`, which is\n   554\t  not a valid JSON escape. Add `\"repair_latex_escapes\": true` to the `response_format` (or to `text.format` on `/v1/responses`) and,\n   555\t  only after the strict parse failed, the server doubles the backslashes that are not valid escapes and checks the answer again.\n   556\t  Valid output is never touched. `\\b`, `\\f`, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t` and `\\uXXXX` are valid JSON, so they stay: a `\\beta` beside a `\\alpha`\n   557\t  comes back with a backspace. Off by default.\n   558\t- `logit_bias` and token/bias pair lists work on CUDA, HIP and Metal engines, also with continuous batching (`--batch` / `--parallel`: each slot keeps its own bias) ([LOGIT_BIAS.md](LOGIT_BIAS.md)).\n   559\t- Stop strings (`stop`, `stop_sequences`), `temperature`, `top_p`, `top_k`, `min_p` and `seed` are honored per request.\n   560\t  `top_k` keeps at most 64 candidates. Penalties (`presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`, `repetition_penalty`,\n   561\t  `penalty_last_n`; llama.cpp's `repeat_penalty` and `repeat_last_n` work too) apply to every token the speculative check\n   562\t  verifies, as if decoded one at a time, and make a request 1-11% slower because more drafts are rejected.\n   563\t  `penalty_last_n` (64 when a penalty is set and the request gives none) is a window over the tokens of the current answer: the\n   564\t  prompt, including earlier assistant turns in it, is not counted, so the numbers or a quoted earlier answer in a new task are not\n   565\t  penalised. `STRATA_PENALTY_PROMPT=1` counts the last `penalty_last_n` tokens of prompt and answer together, the old window.\n   566\t  This holds for the Qwen3.8 and Qwen3.6-35B-A3B engines on CUDA, HIP, Metal and SYCL.\n   567\t- Sampling defaults, in order of precedence: the request, the web page's shared Chat settings, the config's `sampling` block,\n   568\t  then the model card. Without a block, thinking requests get temperature 1.0, top_p 0.95, top_k 20, and non-thinking\n   569\t  requests get 0.7, 0.8, 20 with presence_penalty 1.5 (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B uses presence_penalty 1.5 in both). Greedy is only for\n   570\t  whoever asks: `temperature: 0` in the request or `\"sampling\": {\"temperature\": 0}` in the config. With the default adaptive\n   571\t  tier a sampled result is not reproducible from run to run. For seed-reproducible output add `--adapt-every 100000`.\n   572\t- Sampled drafting is opt-in and not faster in a way that holds up. `STRATA_SPEC_COUPLED=1` (or `--coupled-draft`) and\n   573\t  `STRATA_SPEC_GUMBEL=1` raise the accepted-draft rate on some machines; `STRATA_SPEC_PROB=1` is speculative rejection\n   574\t  sampling. `STRATA_SPEC_DEPTH=1` prints acceptance by draft depth and `STRATA_SPEC_MIN_TEMP=0.9` limits sampled drafting\n   575\t  to hotter requests. Try them on your own card before relying on them.\n   576\t- **logprobs: how likely each token was (CUDA/HIP).** `\"logprobs\": true` with `\"top_logprobs\": 0..20` on\n   577\t  `/v1/chat/completions`, streamed or not, adds `choices[0].logprobs.content[]` to the reply (`token`, `logprob`,\n   578\t  `bytes`, `top_logprobs`); with thinking on, the thinking's entries come under `reasoning_content` instead. The\n   579\t  tokens, joined, spell the reply exactly (a character split across two tokens lands in the one that finishes it).\n   580\t  A reply is bounded by `max_tokens` -- or by the rest of the context when it is not sent -- and by no cap of our\n   581\t  own, as on OpenAI and vLLM, neither of which ties a length limit to logprobs. A reply that is not streamed, or\n   582\t  that answers a `json_schema` (buffered to be validated), does hold every entry until it ends, so a long one holds\n   583\t  a lot of them; a streamed reply hands each entry out with its chunk and keeps none. `\"allowed_token_ids\": [ids]`\n   584\t  keeps only those ids in `top_logprobs`, most likely first, renormalised to sum to 1; the chosen token is one of\n   585\t  them when it was named (vLLM's field, but vLLM masks generation with it, while here it filters what is reported,\n   586\t  so a token outside the named ids keeps its own `logprob` and an empty list). An id past the model's vocabulary is\n   587\t  a 400. A token whose probability could not be read gets no entry, and the reply goes on. On a server started with\n   588\t  `--parallel`, only a `max_tokens` 1 request gets them: it is the one a batch slot decodes through the single-token\n   589\t  path, which writes an LP line. A longer reply there is a 400, since the slot's `BT` lines carry none -- entries for\n   590\t  a prefix only, which a client cannot tell from the model's own answer.\n   591\t- `STRATA_SPEC_PROB=1` with the default `--spec-min-p` gate: the gate tests the token the draft head drew from its distribution\n   592\t  q. A draft that fails the gate still counts as a proposal. The verifier keeps q's mass on the tokens that pass the gate\n   593\t  (not renormalised; call it mu), accepts an offered token D with probability min(1, p(D)/mu(D)), and draws the replacement\n   594\t  for a rejection or a failed gate from the positive part of p - mu. The older rule checked against the unfiltered q and\n   595\t  treated a failed gate as a plain sample, which shifts the output: with p = (0.5, 0.5), q = (0.875, 0.125) and a threshold\n   596\t  of 0.5 it gave (0.5625, 0.4375). `STRATA_SPEC_PROB_GATE=top` gates on q's largest probability before the draw instead and\n   597\t  keeps the normalised-q rule. The gated rule needs serial decode on one device with `--lookup-chain 0 --adapt-swaps 0\n   598\t  --pcie-frac 0` and without `STRATA_SPEC_COST`; otherwise the engine says so once and keeps the older rule. This is a\n   599\t  correctness change, not a speed change. `spec_admission_test` enumerates the rule exactly for a two-token vocabulary,\n   600\t  `gated_proposal_test` checks the proposal packet and `spec_proposal_parity` checks the GPU verifier against a CPU\n   601\t  reference. A model-level distribution run is not published yet.\n   602\t- With `STRATA_SPEC_PROB=1` the CPU experts of a native (IQ) pack compute a token's rows the same way whatever the window\n   603\t  width: the grouped kernels that round differently from the one-token result are replaced by the one-token arithmetic,\n   604\t  and the grouped kernels that already match it stay. Prefill and every other decode keep their arithmetic.\n   605\t  `native_arithmetic_parity` checks every group width from 1 to 8 for the supported formats, bit for bit. Comparing other\n   606\t  decode modes against each other still needs one target evaluator; `STRATA_IQ_MT_MIN=1` selects the grouped IQ arithmetic\n   607\t  at every width and also changes prefill, so keep it the same in both arms.\n   608\t- **Non-finite logits end the request and drop the cached state (on by default).** If a verify window's logits are NaN, the\n   609\t  window emits no tokens, the request ends, and the next request drops the live session, checkpoints and parked\n   610\t  conversations and reads its prompt from token 0. The server retries a request that had sent nothing once, transparently.\n   611\t  A started reply ends as `\"length\"`, and a second hit on the retry returns an error. `STRATA_NAN_GUARD=0` turns the check off;\n   612\t  `STRATA_NAN_INJECT=<n>[,<n>...]` flags the n-th checked window to test the path. The one-shot command line and the\n   613\t  Intel (SYCL) port do not check.\n   614\t\n   615\t### Context and limits\n   616\t\n   617\t- Context is chosen in setup (8K-262K). Requests longer than that are refused, never cut silently.\n   618\t- `max_tokens`, `max_completion_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` of `-1`, `0` or left out mean no cap: the answer may use the room\n   619\t  left in the context.\n   620\t- The `model` field is not matched against the loaded model, because one server serves one model and OpenAI clients often\n   621\t  send names of their own. Only `GET /v1/models` says what is loaded.\n   622\t- One request at a time unless `\"parallel\": N` is set. Images only when set up with them. No video.\n   623\t\n   624\t### Context beyond 262K (rope scaling, experimental, off unless you pick it)\n   625\t\n   626\tThe model was trained on 262,144 positions. Rope scaling rescales the rotation angles so that longer contexts stay usable,\n   627\twith llama.cpp's types and flag names: `linear` (Position Interpolation) and `yarn`. Use **yarn**. Without the flags\n   628\tnothing changes. Scaled contexts need proportionally more VRAM and RAM (~13 KB for the KV cache and ~0.26 KB for the rope\n   629\ttables per token). The scaling is fixed for the whole run, because the KV cache stores keys after rotating them.\n   630\t\n   631\t- Source setup: `START-HERE.bat --setup --context 393216` picks yarn and derives the factor (final context / 262,144, at least 1).\n   632\t  `--rope-scaling` and `--rope-scale` override; an explicit `--rope-scaling none` for a context past 262,144 is refused.\n   633\t- Engine flags: `--rope-scaling none|linear|yarn`, `--rope-scale F`, `--rope-freq-base`, `--rope-freq-scale`,\n   634\t  `--yarn-orig-ctx` (262,144), `--yarn-ext-factor`, `--yarn-attn-factor`, `--yarn-beta-fast` (32), `--yarn-beta-slow` (1).\n   635\t  A fine-tune's `rope.scaling.*` keys are the defaults the flags override.\n   636\t- Within the trained range a scaled run is a slightly different model, so setup turns scaling on only past 262,144.\n   637\t  Pictures read the same scaled table; that is unmeasured, and the evidence in History is all text.\n   638\t\n   639\t### Network access\n   640\t\n   641\t- **Local only by default** (`127.0.0.1`). For other devices on your network run setup with\n   642\t  `--host 0.0.0.0 --api-key some-long-secret`, or add `\"host\": \"0.0.0.0\"` and `\"api_key\": \"...\"` to the config. A key is\n   643\t  recommended, not required: without one the server starts and warns that anyone who can reach the port can use the model\n   644\t  and its tools. `--no-api-key` (or `\"api_key\": \"none\"`) says it is on purpose. The server window prints this PC's addresses.\n   645\t  For IPv6 use `--host ::` (or `\"host\": \"::\"`, all IPv6 interfaces) and put the address in brackets in URLs, for example\n   646\t  `http://[2001:db8::7]:8080/v1`.\n   647\t  On Windows allow the port in the firewall for private networks:\n   648\t  `New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName \"Strata 8080\" -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 8080 -Action Allow -Profile Private`\n   649\t  (admin PowerShell).\n   650\t- **From the internet:** put a tunnel in front, for example `cloudflared tunnel --url http://127.0.0.1:8080`. Set a key first.\n   651\t  Several keys (one per client, so one can be withdrawn): `--api-key key1,key2`, `\"api_key\": \"key1,key2\"` or\n   652\t  `\"api_key\": [\"key1\", \"key2\"]`. `STRATA_API_KEY` sets one too. Streamed answers carry `X-Accel-Buffering: no`. When you\n   653\t  open the web page through a proxy or tunnel whose address differs, add it: `\"trusted_origins\": [\"https://strata.example.com\"]`.\n   654\t- **Browser pages (CORS):** off by default. `\"cors_origins\": [\"https://chat.example.com\"]` lets those origins call `/v1/*`;\n   655\t  `[\"*\"]` lets any page and is only sensible with an API key. It never opens `/settings`, `/unload` or the MCP tools. It\n   656\t  belongs to the engine's own server; the desktop app answers browser pages on its own API address and already allows\n   657\t  pages of any site there. When you share with other devices or run a tunnel, the shared address answers them too, with an\n   658\t  API key: the key decides, not the site. The browser's preflight (`OPTIONS`) is answered without a key, since a browser never\n   659\t  sends one with it; the request that follows still needs the key. Without an API key the shared address sends no CORS\n   660\t  headers, so a page of another site cannot read your model through it - only devices on your network can.\n   661\t- **Host names (DNS rebinding):** without an API key the server answers only requests whose `Host` is `localhost`, an IP\n   662\t  address, the address it listens on, or (when it listens beyond this PC) this PC's name and `host.docker.internal`. Others\n   663\t  get **403** naming the setting. Add a name with `\"allowed_hosts\": [\"strata.example.com\"]` or\n   664\t  `STRATA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=strata.example.com` (comma-separated). `\".example.com\"` allows every name below it and `[\"*\"]`\n   665\t  turns the check off, as does setting `api_key`.\n   666\t- **Web pages without a key:** a `POST` to `/v1/*` carrying an `Origin` header is answered only for Strata's own page, pages on\n   667\t  `localhost` or an allowed host, origins in `trusted_origins` or `cors_origins`, and browser extensions and desktop apps, and only with\n   668\t  a JSON body. Other pages and `Origin: null` get **403**. Clients without an `Origin` (curl, the SDKs) are not affected. With\n   669\t  an API key, the key decides. `POST /unload`, `/load` and `/slots/0?action=...` also take only JSON from no `Origin`,\n   670\t  Strata's own page or a trusted origin.\n   671\t\n   672\t### Connecting apps\n   673\t\n   674\t- **Any \"OpenAI-compatible\" app:** base URL `http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1`, any API key. Zed's llama.cpp provider reads `/props`\n   675\t  and offers tools from `chat_template_caps`. Zed's generic OpenAI-compatible provider does not; add\n   676\t  `\"capabilities\": {\"tools\": true, \"chat_completions\": true}` on that model in Zed's settings.\n   677\t- **Claude Code:** set `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080` and `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` to a Claude model name it knows\n   678\t  (Strata ignores the name), plus any `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` (or your `api_key`).\n   679\t- **OpenCode:** a starting point for `opencode.jsonc`. The field names are OpenCode's, so check its config docs if your version\n   680\t  differs.\n   681\t\n   682\t  ```jsonc\n   683\t  {\n   684\t    \"$schema\": \"https://opencode.ai/config.json\",\n   685\t    \"provider\": {\n   686\t      \"strata\": {\n   687\t        \"npm\": \"@ai-sdk/openai-compatible\",\n   688\t        \"name\": \"Strata (local)\",\n   689\t        \"options\": { \"baseURL\": \"http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\", \"apiKey\": \"none\" },  // or your api_key\n   690\t        \"models\": {\n   691\t          \"strata\": {\n   692\t            \"name\": \"Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (Strata)\",\n   693\t            // context: what you chose in setup; output: what one reply may use (prompt + output must fit)\n   694\t            \"limit\": { \"context\": 262144, \"output\": 32768 },\n   695\t            \"options\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\" },\n   696\t            \"variants\": {\n   697\t              \"low\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"low\" },\n   698\t              \"medium\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"medium\" },\n   699\t              \"none\": { \"reasoningEffort\": \"none\" }\n   700\t            }\n   701\t          }\n   702\t        }\n   703\t      }\n   704\t    },\n   705\t    \"model\": \"strata/strata\"\n   706\t  }\n   707\t  ```\n   708\t\n   709\t  Set `limit.context` to the context you chose in setup and keep `limit.output` well under it. `strata connect` writes\n   710\t  configurations for these apps for you.\n   711\t- **Codex CLI:** [below](#the-responses-api-and-codex-cli).\n   712\t\n   713\t### Conversation cache\n   714\t\n   715\tA request that continues a chat reads only the part after what the engine already holds: the live session, or one of the\n   716\tcheckpoints in RAM (up to 6, about 118 MB each, taken at the start of each new assistant turn and every 16K prompt tokens). A\n   717\tcheckpoint is used only when the prompt starts with exactly its tokens and pictures. The oldest checkpoint, in practice\n   718\tthe end of the system prompt that every chat of a client shares, is kept for good, so a new chat that shares the prefix\n   719\tstarts after it. A prompt of 2,048 tokens or more is also checkpointed at the end of its system prompt. Claude Code stamps its\n   720\tsystem prompt with a billing header that changes on every request; the server pins both stamps so the prompt stays the same.\n   721\t\n   722\t| Option | Effect |\n   723\t| --- | --- |\n   724\t| `--prompt-cache N` | Number of checkpoints (`0` = off, which also disables parking). |\n   725\t| `--prompt-cache-every N`, `--prompt-cache-root N`, `--turn-token ID` | Checkpoint interval, the system-prompt checkpoint (`0` = none) and the turn token. The interval is not a piece-size setting: [what it does](PROMPT_CACHE_TAIL.md#prompt-cache-every-does-not-set-the-size-of-the-pieces-a-prompt-is-read-in). |\n   726\t| `--prompt-cache-spread` (opt-in) | The checkpoint that leaves is the one whose removal leaves the smallest gap, so the kept points thin out geometrically back from the end. An agent that rewrites its history a few thousand tokens back then resumes near the edit instead of at the system prompt. Same RAM. |\n   727\t| `\"strata_checkpoint\": false` (per request) | A one-shot request that no later request continues saves no checkpoints and keeps no session. |\n   728\t| `\"strata_prefix\": {\"messages\": 1}` (per request, opt-in) | One long document, many questions. Marks where the document ends: `{\"messages\": 1}`, `{\"message\": 0, \"chars\": 210000}` or `{\"tokens\": 12345}`. The checkpoint at the boundary is pinned, so 20 questions cost one read of the document. One pinned prefix at a time per engine; needs `--prompt-cache 3` or more; a malformed field is a 400. See [RESEARCH_RUNS.md](RESEARCH_RUNS.md). |\n   729\t| `--conversation-cache-mib N`, `--conversation-cache-slots N` | Park conversations in a bounded host-RAM cache so alternating requests (controller and worker histories, sub-agents) reuse their prompts. Without `--conversation-cache-mib` the budget is 40% of the RAM left above `--conversation-cache-min-free-mib` once the model is loaded, at most 8192 MiB in steps of 256, off when under 1024; the engine log says which. `0` turns it off. It preserves histories; it does not run requests at once. |\n   730\t| `--conversation-cache-min-tokens N` | Park only conversations of at least N tokens, so short side requests do not evict a long conversation. Default `0` (park all). |\n   731\t| `--conversation-cache-park-gain N` (opt-in) | Park a conversation again only after it advanced N tokens past the copy it would replace. |\n   732\t| `--conversation-cache-min-free-mib N` | RAM floor, default 2560. Parking and session files are skipped when less than the snapshot plus the floor is available. |\n   733\t| `--memory-limit-mib N` (or `STRATA_MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB`) | Total RAM this engine's container may use, for when a container cannot see its own limit. Config `args`: `\"--memory-limit-mib\", \"102400\"`. `0` = none. |\n   734\t\n   735\tThe RAM figure the floor is checked against is the smallest of `MemAvailable`, the room under each cgroup limit visible to\n   736\tthe engine (v2 `memory.max` and `memory.high`, v1 `memory.limit_in_bytes`, less clean inactive file cache) and the\n   737\t`--memory-limit-mib` cap. The startup line `strata generate: memory guard: limit X GiB (source: flag|cgroup|meminfo) ...` names\n   738\tthe source. It covers parking and session files only, not the startup sizing of the arena, the resident budget or the Windows commit check.\n   739\t\n   740\t**When the arena does not fit in the RAM that is free (#4051).** The engine checks the RAM before it reads the experts into the\n   741\tarena. A memory limit below the arena (a container) stops the start with the numbers. Less free RAM than the arena (an engine\n   742\tthat was still exiting, another program) is waited for up to 20 s, and the log says so (`STRATA_ARENA_RAM_WAIT_S` changes the\n   743\tseconds, `0` loads at once); if it is still short the load goes on with a warning that says how much, never a refusal. Below half\n   744\tof the arena the warning adds that the whole PC can stall in memory reclaim, and that a systemd `MemoryHigh=` below the RAM keeps\n   745\tone program from taking all of it. The server, before it starts an engine, ends one that an earlier run left behind and now waits up\n   746\tto 120 s for it to be gone (it used to wait 10 s): an engine with tens of GB of pinned memory needs longer to give it back, and the\n   747\tnext one started beside it ran the PC out of RAM (#4051).\n   748\t\n   749\t**When it is still short after the wait: the RAM copy, and `--ram-keep-gib` (R5070).** The arena is used only when it fits. When it does\n   750\tnot (#4846), the engine does not read every expert through the file any more: with a static expert profile it keeps the hottest experts\n   751\tin a RAM copy, as much as the RAM available at the start allows after a headroom, and reads the rest from the model files (the\n   752\t`--resident-budget-gib` form). On a 12 GB card with a 63 GB PC and 44.6 GiB available this took IQ3_S at 128K from 9.4 to 25-28 tok/s.\n   753\t\n   754\t- `--ram-keep-gib W` (default 0; `STRATA_RAM_KEEP_GIB` is the same for a program that cannot pass the flag) is the RAM you want left free\n   755\t  for your own work. It covers both decisions: the arena is used only if W GiB are still available after it, and the RAM copy that\n   756\t  replaces it leaves `max(W, the safety headroom)` free. The arena needs `arena + one blob + W` available; the log says so\n   757\t  (\"the expert arena needs ... with the W GiB --ram-keep-gib asks to leave free\").\n   758\t- The safety headroom is 8 GiB on Windows and 4 GiB elsewhere. It is not a preference: on Windows the copy is read through a mapped view\n   759\t  whose pages count against the process until the engine gives them back (every 4 layers), and with less than 8 GiB of room available RAM\n   760\t  fell to 0.9 GiB during the copy. `STRATA_RESIDENT_HEADROOM_GIB`, when set, decides the headroom instead of both (testers).\n   761\t- With W = 0 nothing about the fallback changes: the full system is used, the arena whenever it fits, with no margin, and the copy leaves\n   762\t  the safety headroom. The explicit modes (`--resident-budget-gib N`, `--resident-experts`) leave `max(W, their own 4 GiB)`.\n   763\t- The app's \"Leave 20% for my work\" passes W = clamp(ceil(0.2 x RAM in GB), 5, 8) as `STRATA_RAM_KEEP_GIB` in the run config's env (not as the flag, so an older engine chosen in the Engine tab, which ignores an env variable, still starts); `strata serve` and full performance pass nothing.\n   764\t- `STRATA_ARENA_FALLBACK_RESIDENT=0` keeps the plain file source for the fallback (testers).\n   765\t\n   766\tSnapshots hold running state, checkpoints, used K/V pages and draft-layer K/V. They add host RAM, not VRAM. Oldest parked entries\n   767\tare evicted first. A new conversation that starts at a checkpoint inside a parked one (sub-agents that share a system prompt)\n   768\trestores that prefix and leaves the parked conversation in place (the log says \"borrowed\"). Oversized snapshots or host\n   769\tallocation failures fall back to ordinary prompt processing. With `--layer-split` a parked conversation holds one image per\n   770\tstage. Parked snapshots are not kept across restarts; session files are. `STRATA_SNAPSHOT_FULL_CAPTURE=1` disables K/V\n   771\tretention for diagnostics. The Monitor tab's **Conversation cache** card shows parked, restored and evicted counts and the\n   772\tprompt tokens the cache gave back; `/metrics` has the same under `\"conversation_cache\"`.\n   773\t\n   774\t### Session files (disk)\n   775\t\n   776\tThe conversation the engine holds can be saved to a file and restored later, also after a restart of the same engine version,\n   777\tso a long prompt is not read again. Start the server with `--slot-save-path DIR` (or `\"slot_save_path\"` in the config). The\n   778\tfile format is Strata's own, not llama.cpp's, and there is no erase action.\n   779\t\n   780\t```bash\n   781\tcurl -X POST \"http://127.0.0.1:8080/slots/0?action=save\"    -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"filename\": \"chat1.bin\"}'\n   782\tcurl -X POST \"http://127.0.0.1:8080/slots/0?action=restore\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"filename\": \"chat1.bin\"}'\n   783\t```\n   784\t\n   785\t- `DIR` becomes an absolute path at start and is created with mode 0700. Files are created with mode 0600 on Linux (on Windows\n   786\t  they inherit the folder's permissions), nothing deletes them, and they are large (about 1.2 GB per 63K-token conversation).\n   787\t  Keep `DIR` private to the user that runs Strata.\n   788\t- `NAME` must be a plain file name inside `DIR`: no path, drive, stream (`:`), Windows device name, control character, leading\n   789\t  dot or trailing dot or space. A save checks that the disk has room for the whole file plus `--session-min-free-mib` (engine\n   790\t  argument, default 4096, `0` = no check).\n   791\t- `--session-save-reclaim` (engine argument, off by default) lets a save that fails its RAM check first release reconstructible\n   792\t  host caches (retained K/V, oldest unpinned parked conversations, unpinned checkpoints), and the server waits up to 30 s for\n   793\t  RAM accounting to catch up. It trades later prefix-cache hits for room to save and cannot guarantee a save.\n   794\t- Clients still send their messages and images; the file holds engine state and token identity, not a chat export. A follow-up\n   795\t  that continues the restored conversation reuses its state; a short tail may be read again. Only the deepest checkpoint is saved.\n   796\t- A file is bound to the model files (sampled by size and first and last MiB, not by path) and to the settings that change what\n   797\t  the cells mean (engine version, CUDA or HIP, `--kv`, `STRATA_KV_ROT`, `--kv-resident`, `--max-context`, `--mtp-window`, the\n   798\t  rope configuration, a loaded control vector and the arithmetic switches). Sampling, seeds, draft tuning and the expert\n   799\t  tier are not in it. Do not change model files while their sessions are kept. The hash detects accidental corruption and does\n   800\t  not authenticate a file: restore only files this engine wrote.\n   801\t- Errors: `501` without `--slot-save-path` or with parallel requests; `400` for a slot other than 0, an unknown action, a refused\n   802\t  file name or an invalid file; `404` for a missing file; `503` when the model is not loaded or RAM is short; `507` when the disk has\n   803\t  no room; `500` for other I/O failures or an engine that ended. `error.published: true` means a save failed after its new\n   804\t  file had replaced the old one. Requests must be `application/json` (else `415`) and come from no browser page, Strata's own\n   805\t  page or a trusted origin.\n   806\t- Not supported with `--layer-split`, `--peer-device`, `--batch` or `--prompt-cache 0`. The engine has been run on Linux and CUDA\n   807\t  only for this feature. Exact token replay across restarts is not guaranteed: expert residency and CPU/GPU rounding can\n   808\t  change later output.\n   809\t\n   810\t### The web page (Chat, Images, Monitor, About)\n   811\t\n   812\tOpen the server's address in a browser. Everything in it runs from the server's own files: no CDN, no outside address. Chats,\n   813\tsettings and the API key stay in the browser.\n   814\t\n   815\t**Chat settings** (the button next to the message box) holds the sampling and thinking settings and the switches below. Every\n   816\tswitch below is **off by default**; with all of them off the page behaves as it did before.\n   817\t\n   818\t- **Render math.** Equations written as `\\( ... \\)`, `\\[ ... \\]`, `$x$` or `$$ ... $$` are drawn as formulas by KaTeX 0.19.0, which\n   819\t  ships in `serve/web/vendor/katex` (MIT; `PROVENANCE.json` there lists the package and a hash of every file). The text of a\n   820\t  message is never changed: copying, the Markdown export and the next request all carry what the model wrote. Code, prices\n   821\t  (`$5 and $10`) and a formula still being written stay text. A formula KaTeX cannot read stays as its source. A formula can only\n   822\t  draw: it cannot make a link, load a picture or run anything (`\\href`, `\\url`, `\\includegraphics` and `\\htmlClass` are shown as\n   823\t  their own names).\n   824\t- **Saved chats list.** Keeps every chat in a list on the left, with search over titles and text, rename, delete (with Undo) and\n   825\t  days as headings. The chats live in this browser's IndexedDB; a picture or file is kept by name only. Nothing goes to the server.\n   826\t  In a private window, or when the browser's storage is full, the list stays in memory and the page says so once. Turning it on\n   827\t  keeps the chat on the screen as the first one in the list; turning it off keeps that chat as the single chat.\n   828\t- **Edit and rewind messages.** Under each message: **Regenerate** (a new answer to the same question), **Edit answer** (correct its\n   829\t  text; the old thinking and tool calls go and no request is sent) and **Rewind to here**. Rewind asks what you want. On your\n   830\t  message, **Edit and send again here** hides it and the messages after it and puts it in the box; on an answer, **Continue from\n   831\t  here** hides the messages after it. **Branch from here** (needs the saved chats list) starts a new chat from the messages up to\n   832\t  that point and leaves this chat as it is; the branch keeps a name such as \"Branch of ...\" and links back to the original. An\n   833\t  **Undo** bar above the box brings back what was hidden, until your next question, another chat or a reload. A picture or file\n   834\t  that was only kept by name is not sent again: the page asks you to attach it again.\n   835\t- **When you send while it is answering.** *Wait* (the default): Send is off until the answer is done. *Send after*: your message\n   836\t  waits and goes when the answer is done. *Interrupt*: the answer stops where it is, what was written stays and is marked\n   837\t  interrupted, and your message goes at once; a tool that is running finishes first. Only one message waits, and **Take back**\n   838\t  returns it to the box. Stop keeps a waiting message for you to send yourself.\n   839\t\n   840\tAn answer's ` ```svg ` or ` ```html ` block gets a **Preview** button. The code is drawn in a frame that is cut off from the page:\n   841\tan empty sandbox (no scripts, forms, popups or downloads, and no access to the page, its storage or the server), a policy inside\n   842\tthe frame that allows no address at all, and a page header (`Content-Security-Policy: frame-src 'none'`) that keeps a link or a\n   843\tredirect in the model's HTML from loading a web page into it. An svg is shown as a picture. For an HTML page there is also a\n   844\twarning button, **Run scripts**: after you confirm, that one block runs with scripts, still with no way to the page and no network,\n   845\tuntil you close the preview. It can still use your CPU, and it is code the model wrote.\n   846\t\n   847\t**Load / Unload** in the header loads the model or frees the GPU (`POST /load`, `POST /unload`). It asks before unloading and is off\n   848\twhile an answer is being written or an image is made.\n   849\t\n   850\t**Language.** About > Settings lists the page's languages: English (the default), German, Spanish, Japanese, Korean, Portuguese,\n   851\tRussian and Chinese. The Chat view, its settings and the header are translated; Monitor, Images and About are still English. A\n   852\tlanguage is a file `serve/web/locales/<lang>.json`; the English text lives in the code next to its key, and\n   853\t`python tools/web_i18n.py` checks every file for the same keys and placeholders (`--write` rewrites `en.json`). `/?lang=de` shows a\n   854\tlanguage for one visit.\n   855\t\n   856\tTests: `serve/test_web_*.py` and `serve/test_web_*.js` (Node), and a browser run against a stand-in server with no model:\n   857\t`WEBTEST_MODULES=<folder with puppeteer-core> node tools/webtest/browser_test.mjs`. `tools/webtest/shots.mjs` takes the page's\n   858\tscreenshots.\n   859\t\n   860\t### API request monitor\n   861\t\n   862\tOff by default, because it keeps prompts and answers in memory. Turn it on with `\"api_monitor\": true` or\n   863\t`serve/server.py --api-monitor`; otherwise the two endpoints answer 404. `/api-monitor` shows the model state, load and unload\n   864\tcontrols, active and queued requests, request bodies, output, separate reasoning and response bodies, with load, queue,\n   865\tfirst-token and decode timings. `GET /api/requests` returns summaries and `GET /api/requests?id=<id>` one request. The\n   866\tmonitor keeps the newest 100 requests in memory until restart, 262,144 characters per field, with visible truncation flags.\n   867\tHeaders are not recorded. Treat the history as sensitive when Strata is reachable from a network, and set an API key.\n   868\t\n   869\t---\n   870\t\n   871\t### The Responses API and Codex CLI\n   872\t\n   873\t`POST /v1/responses` speaks OpenAI's newer Responses API, which Codex CLI uses. It shares the chat path, so thinking levels,\n   874\tbudgets, the conversation cache and the API key, Host and Origin checks apply. It is **stateless**: the client sends the whole\n   875\tconversation in `input` every time. `previous_response_id`, `conversation`, `background` and the retrieve, delete and cancel\n   876\tendpoints are refused with an error that says so.\n   877\t\n   878\t| Request | What Strata does |\n   879\t| --- | --- |\n   880\t| `input` as a string or items | `message` items (`user`, `assistant`, `system`, `developer`; text and images), `reasoning`, `function_call`, `function_call_output`, `custom_tool_call(_output)` |\n   881\t| `instructions` | The system message (with leading `developer` messages) |\n   882\t| `tools` | `function`, `namespace` and `custom` tools. Hosted tools (`web_search`, `file_search`, ...) are left out because the model cannot run them |\n   883\t| `tool_choice` | `\"none\"` hides the tools; anything else lets the model choose (it cannot be forced) |\n   884\t| `reasoning.effort` | `none`/`minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`/`xhigh`; without it the config's `reasoning_effort`, else the model's default |\n   885\t| `max_output_tokens` | The output cap, thinking included. Running out ends the response `incomplete`. `0` and `-1` (or leaving it out) mean no cap, as for `max_tokens`; anything below `-1` is a 400 |\n   886\t| `text.format` | `json_schema` and `json_object` use the JSON formats above (checked, not constrained) |\n   887\t| `client_metadata[\"x-codex-turn-metadata\"]` thread-title request | Opt-in `\"codex_thread_titles\": true`: answered without the engine as `{\"title\": \"<the user's line, cut to 36 characters>\"}` so the conversation keeps the prompt cache |\n   888\t\n   889\tThe final event (`response.completed` or `response.incomplete`) and a non-streaming reply carry the same top-level `timings` object as\n   890\t`/v1/chat/completions`.\n   891\t\n   892\tThe thinking comes back as a `reasoning` output item with `reasoning_text` content. The model writes no summaries, so `summary` is\n   893\tempty. With `\"include\": [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\"]` the item also carries an opaque string (base64, not encrypted).\n   894\tSend reasoning items back with the conversation, as Codex does, so the prompt matches and the cache is reused. Streaming\n   895\tevents are the official ones, in order, ending in `response.completed`, `response.incomplete` or `response.failed`. A\n   896\t`response.in_progress` event goes out every 15 s while a long prompt is read.\n   897\t\n   898\t**Codex CLI.** In `~/.codex/config.toml` (Windows: `%USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml`):\n   899\t\n   900\t```toml\n   901\tmodel = \"strata\"                        # any name; Strata answers with its model\n   902\tmodel_provider = \"strata\"\n   903\tmodel_context_window = 32768            # the context you chose in setup: Codex compacts before it gets there\n   904\tshow_raw_agent_reasoning = true         # show the model's thinking (it writes no summaries)\n   905\t# model_reasoning_effort = \"medium\"     # none, low, medium or high; default: the model's (high)\n   906\t\n   907\t[model_providers.strata]\n   908\tname = \"Strata (local)\"\n   909\tbase_url = \"http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\"\n   910\twire_api = \"responses\"\n   911\tstream_idle_timeout_ms = 600000         # a first, long prompt can take minutes to read\n   912\t# env_key = \"STRATA_API_KEY\"            # only if the server has an api_key: the variable holding it\n   913\t```\n   914\t\n   915\tRun `codex` (or `codex exec \"...\"`). Codex warns `Model metadata for ... not found` for a local model name; that is expected.\n   916\tOn Windows, Codex's own sandbox rejected shell commands until it was started with `-c 'windows.sandbox=\"unelevated\"'` (a\n   917\tCodex setting, not Strata's).\n   918\t\n   919\t**Codex's compaction** (opt-in: `\"codex_compaction_cache\": true`). When the context fills up (or on `/compact`), Codex sends the\n   920\tconversation once more with a summarize request and `tools: []`. The template writes tools at the top of the prompt, so that\n   921\tprompt would be read again from the start. With the option on, Strata remembers which Codex conversation sent the last\n   922\tnon-compaction prompt (`session_id` and `thread_id`) and the tools it was rendered with, and renders a compaction request from\n   923\tthat same conversation with them. It is one entry, replaced by each such prompt. A compaction of another conversation, a\n   924\trequest without that metadata or a restart renders the request as sent. The prompt of a compaction then differs from the one\n   925\tthe client sent, which is why it is off by default.\n   926\t\n   927\t---\n   928\t\n   929\t## Tools from MCP servers\n   930\t\n   931\tThe chat page can give the model tools from [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) servers, as LM Studio and Claude Desktop do.\n   932\tList the servers in `strata-<model>.json` under `\"mcp_servers\"`, the same shape as Claude Desktop's `mcpServers` block (key\n   933\t`\"mcpServers\"` also works when pasted as is). Merge these fields into the existing config:\n   934\t\n   935\t```json\n   936\t{\n   937\t  \"mcp_servers\": {\n   938\t    \"files\": {\"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem\", \"C:\\\\Users\\\\me\\\\Documents\\\\notes\"]},\n   939\t    \"search\": {\"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:3000/mcp\", \"headers\": {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer ...\"}}\n   940\t  },\n   941\t  \"mcp\": {\"timeout_s\": 60, \"max_result_chars\": 20000, \"max_rounds\": 8}\n   942\t}\n   943\t```\n   944\t\n   945\tOr keep them in their own file and start the server with `--mcp-config path\\to\\claude_desktop_config.json`. Restart Strata\n   946\tafter a change.\n   947\t\n   948\t- A **program** (`command`, `args`, optional `env` and `cwd`) is started by Strata and spoken to over stdin and stdout. Node.js is\n   949\t  needed for `npx` servers. An **address** (`url`, optional `headers`) uses MCP's Streamable HTTP transport (the older SSE-only\n   950\t  transport is not supported). `\"disabled\": true` leaves an entry out.\n   951\t- Servers start with Strata. The server window says what each offers or why it did not start. The Monitor tab lists them, and\n   952\t  Chat settings has **Use tools from MCP servers** (on by default). A server that stops later is started again at its next call.\n   953\t- In the chat each call shows as a small block. The model reads the result and goes on, up to `max_rounds` rounds of calls (one round can hold several calls). At the limit\n   954\t  the model gets one final reply with no tools, using the results already found, so the answer is not cut off. A tool\n   955\t  that fails or takes longer than `timeout_s` gives the model an `error: ...` result. Results longer than `max_result_chars` are\n   956\t  cut with a note. Stop stops a running tool too.\n   957\t- Only the chat page uses them. API clients see the API exactly as before and keep their own tools. A request to\n   958\t  `/v1/chat/completions` opts in with `\"strata_mcp\": true`.\n   959\t\n   960\t**Security.** MCP tools run on your PC with your user's rights, and the model decides when to call them, also because of what\n   961\tit reads (a web page or file can contain instructions). Give a filesystem server only the folders it needs, prefer read-only\n   962\ttools and add only servers you trust. If Strata is reachable from other devices, set an API key.\n   963\t\n   964\t## Manage Strata from your AI assistant (MCP server)\n   965\t\n   966\t`tools/strata_mcp.py` is an MCP server for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, Codex and other assistants. Once it is\n   967\tadded you can ask your assistant to install Strata, start it or check whether it is running. In Claude Code:\n   968\t\n   969\t```bash\n   970\tclaude mcp add strata -- python C:\\Users\\you\\Strata\\tools\\strata_mcp.py\n   971\t```\n   972\t\n   973\tIt has tools for status, the model list, install, start, stop, logs, a speed test and connection settings. Install runs\n   974\t`setup.py` with `--yes` in the background and shows the plan first. The MCP server only ends processes it started itself and\n   975\tuses only Python's standard library. Config snippets for every client, the tool arguments and the safety rules are in\n   976\t[MCP_SERVER.md](MCP_SERVER.md). This is the opposite direction from [Tools from MCP servers](#tools-from-mcp-servers) above.\n   977\t\n   978\t---\n   979\t\n   980\t## Images (vision)\n   981\t\n   982\tQwen3.8-Flash-Next has a vision encoder (`mmproj-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-BF16.gguf`, 0.9 GB). Qwen3.6-35B-A3B reads pictures the\n   983\tsame way with its own `mmproj-F16.gguf` ([QWEN36.md](QWEN36.md)). Pictures are **on by default** wherever a model can read\n   984\tthem. The app, `strata launch` and setup use one rule for the reader:\n   985\t\n   986\t| Graphics memory | Default reader | Cost |\n   987\t| --- | --- | --- |\n   988\t| Under 24 GB (NVIDIA and AMD) | CPU reader | Nothing from the card, so the experts keep their room. About 3 s for a 300-token picture on 8 cores; more tokens take longer in proportion. |\n   989\t| 24 GB or more | The card's reader, started by the first picture and stopped after 120 s without one (NVIDIA, one GPU) | 0.1-0.5 s per picture (up to 1,024 image tokens) once it runs, 1.2-1.4 s more for the first picture after a stop. It holds about 1.3 GB of VRAM only while pictures are being read; a start with no room is read on the CPU. On AMD, Intel and a Mac the reader stays on the card as before. |\n   990\t| Mac | The GPU (Metal) | Whatever its memory. |\n   991\t\n   992\tSwitch pictures off with `--vision no` (or \"No\" in the app and `strata launch`), or pick the reader with `--vision gpu` or\n   993\t`--vision cpu`. Setup downloads the encoder and puts a small helper (`strata-vision`, from llama.cpp's `mtmd` library) beside\n   994\tthe engine. Where no reader exists, pictures stay off and the screen says so (the Intel Arc engine has none yet). On AMD the\n   995\tcard's reader is `strata-vision-hip`; if it cannot start, the server starts the CPU reader and prints one line ([AMD_HIP.md](AMD_HIP.md)).\n   996\t\n   997\tA picture becomes up to 1,024 tokens of the context (a 640x480 photo: 300). The same picture sent again, as chat apps do on\n   998\tevery turn, is encoded once.\n   999\t\n  1000\t### Vision settings (the `\"vision\"` section of `strata-<model>.json`)\n  1001\t\n  1002\t| Setting | Effect |\n  1003\t| --- | --- |\n  1004\t| `--vision-tokens N` at setup, or `\"max_tokens\"` | The most tokens a picture becomes. More keep more detail (small text, charts) and take longer, on the CPU most of all. A setup run again keeps the value. |\n  1005\t| `\"min_tokens\": N` | Scales a small picture up to at least N tokens (passed to the encoder as `--min-tokens`). Qwen-VL grounding tasks want at least 1,024. A larger minimum changes image answers and costs encode time. |\n  1006\t| `\"mmproj\"` | Points to another mmproj file of this model, for example a Q8_0 one (`llama-quantize mmproj-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-BF16.gguf mmproj-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Q8_0.gguf Q8_0`): 590 MiB against 865 MiB, about 280 MiB less encoder RAM, and it can encode faster on the CPU. **Recommended for `--vision cpu`.** Embedding cosine against BF16 is 0.997-0.999. Setup downloads the BF16 file and keeps a file of your own that still exists. |\n  1007\t| `\"cuda_device\": N` | Runs the encoder on a spare card (numbered like `nvidia-smi`). Lower `--vram-reserve-mib` in `\"args\"` to 700 as well. The card needs code in the ready-made encoder (RTX 20/30/40/50). |\n  1008\t| `\"elastic\": false` | Keeps the GPU encoder on the card from the start (the NVIDIA one-GPU default is to start it with the first picture and stop it after `\"elastic_idle_s\"`, default 120, without one). `\"elastic\": true` asks for the on-demand start on any card; with `\"vram_elastic\": true` too the expert cache gives the room (`\"elastic_mib\"`, default 2048), which turns `--kv-grow` off ([VRAM_ELASTIC.md](VRAM_ELASTIC.md#the-image-encoder-on-demand)). |\n  1009\t| `--flash-attn on\\|off\\|auto` (`strata-vision`) | Flash attention is turned off on the CPU when ggml has AVX-512 (a build from source), because ggml's fallback kernel is several times slower and accumulates in FP16. Release builds use AVX2 and leave it on. |\n  1010\t\n  1011\tOn an NVIDIA card under 24 GB the CPU encoder is the default, because the GPU encoder takes ~850 expert slots of a 16 GB card's ~4,500\n  1012\t(about 5% of decode). `--vision gpu` is kept with a note.\n  1013\t\n  1014\t### Sending a picture\n  1015\t\n  1016\tTerminal chat: type `/image <path>`, press Enter, then type your question. OpenAI API: an `image_url` part (a `data:` URL, an\n  1017\t`http(s)://` URL or a local file path).\n  1018\t\n  1019\t```python\n  1020\timport base64\n  1021\tfrom openai import OpenAI\n  1022\tclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\", api_key=\"none\")\n  1023\timg = base64.b64encode(open(\"photo.jpg\", \"rb\").read()).decode()\n  1024\tr = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"strata\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n  1025\t    {\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": f\"data:image/jpeg;base64,{img}\"}},\n  1026\t    {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"What is in this picture?\"}]}])\n  1027\tprint(r.choices[0].message.content)\n  1028\t```\n  1029\t\n  1030\tAnthropic API: an `image` block with a `base64` (or `url`) source. JPEG, PNG, BMP, GIF, WebP, TIFF and AVIF work (the last\n  1031\tones are converted to PNG first). Chat apps with image upload work the same way.\n  1032\t\n  1033\t**How it works inside.** The encoder turns the picture into rows of the width of the model's word embeddings. Strata puts them\n  1034\twhere the prompt has `<|image_pad|>` tokens and gives each its 2-D position (row and column; interleaved M-RoPE). Answers\n  1035\tmatch llama.cpp's multimodal implementation token for token on our test images.\n  1036\t\n  1037\t---\n  1038\t\n  1039\t## Speed settings\n  1040\t\n  1041\tDefaults first, then switches you can try. A switch you set yourself, to anything, is never overridden. \"Output\" means the\n  1042\tbits of the answer: a switch that changes rounding can change a greedy answer at a near tie.\n  1043\t\n  1044\t### On by default\n  1045\t\n  1046\t| Setting | Applies to | Off switch | What it does |\n  1047\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n  1048\t| `STRATA_PREFILL_CPU_SHARE` (`auto`, cap `STRATA_PREFILL_CPU_SHARE_MAX=1024`) | CUDA, one GPU, no `--batch`, prompt chunks under 1,024 tokens | `STRATA_PREFILL_CPU_SHARE=0` | The CPU pool computes the experts few of the chunk's tokens route to, while the GPU works on the rest. `auto` measures per layer how long each side takes and checks that sharing pays. Skipped with one log line when RAM or commit is short. Output changes in the last bits. Layer split, batch slots and HIP keep it off unless you set it. |\n  1049\t| `STRATA_ROUTE_TAIL_SKIP` (7) | CUDA, and HIP gfx1200/gfx1201; one GPU or a layer split | `STRATA_ROUTE_TAIL_SKIP=0` | A missed expert that every token of a verify window routes at rank 7 or lower is neither copied nor computed. Not applied when every expert is in VRAM, with `--batch` slots, with a peer tier or helper GPU, on other HIP and SYCL, or when 90% or more of the experts are cached (`STRATA_TAIL_SKIP_MAX_CACHED=<percent>` moves that line; it is 60% on an AMD layer split). Answers differ slightly from a run without it. |\n  1050\t| PCIe share from the CPU's speed | CUDA, one GPU, no `--pcie-frac` | `--pcie-frac N` always wins; `STRATA_PCIE_FRAC_DEFAULT=old` keeps the probe-only rule | The share starts at the probe's value and is refined in the first decode windows from the CPU pool's time per missed expert: `L / (L + 1.85 R)`, rounded to 0.05 and kept between 0.10 and 0.60, changed at most three times. Each change prints a `PCIe share:` line. On HIP it is the default for gfx1200/gfx1201; `STRATA_PCIE_FRAC_DEFAULT=auto` turns it on for other HIP parts. `STRATA_PCIE_AUTO_SPLIT=1` (HIP, opt-in) refines a two-stage layer split. |\n  1051\t| Chunk follows the host's memory | Linux file tier | `STRATA_PREFILL_HOST_PLAN=0` | See [Linux file tier](#linux-file-tier). |\n  1052\t| Fused int8 prompt experts | gfx12 (R9700): IQ3_S and IQ3_XXS packs. 12.0 NVIDIA cards with 80 or more SMs: IQ2_S, IQ3_XXS, IQ3_S, IQ4_XS gate/up | `STRATA_PF_FUSED=0` | Replaces the MMQ prompt kernels. `STRATA_PF_FUSED=1` opts in every covered format on other cards (on HIP gfx11 and gfx1200/gfx1201 too; see [AMD_HIP.md](AMD_HIP.md)). |\n  1053\t| Bare `--prefill auto` takes 16384-token chunks | gfx12, Linux | `STRATA_GFX12_CHUNK=0` or `STRATA_GFX12_DEFAULTS=0` | An explicit `--prefill N`, `auto:N` or `STRATA_PREFILL_AUTO_MAX` is untouched. |\n  1054\t| Per-architecture bitwise sets | sm_86, sm_60, sm_120 | See [below](#rtx-30-series-sm_86-and-tesla-p100-sm_60-bitwise-decode-switches-on-by-default) | |\n  1055\t| `STRATA_ADAPT_CALLBACK_WARMUP` | Linux | `=0` | Starts the GPU host-callback thread before the CPU pool pins the host thread. |\n  1056\t| `STRATA_ADAPT_SIGNAL` | CUDA | `host` / `copy` | The overlap's per-layer \"landed\" signal is a stream-ordered copy into pinned memory on Linux and a host function on Windows. |\n  1057\t| `--ple-io direct` | all | `--ple-io ram` or `mmap` | The n-gram table is read unbuffered from the SSD. A startup probe warns when direct reads are slow (under 15K rows/s) but never switches by itself. `STRATA_PLE_AUTO_MMAP=1` brings back the automatic switch to `mmap`. On a rotational disk setup adds `--ple-io ram` when the RAM holds the table. |\n  1058\t| `STRATA_STAGER_SSD` | Linux | `STRATA_STAGER_SSD=1`/`0` forces a profile | A GGUF read in place copies experts with 4 threads when its pages are in the page cache (checked with `mincore` at the first prompt) and with 32 threads and a 128-deep ring when they are on an SSD. `STRATA_STAGER_THREADS` wins. |\n  1059\t\n  1060\t`STRATA_STAGER_SLEEP=1` makes the prompt-staging threads sleep while they wait on Windows too (they sleep on Linux and spin on\n  1061\tWindows by default, which is a little faster). Use it when sharing the machine matters more than speed. `=0` forces spinning on Linux.\n  1062\t\n  1063\t### Opt-in switches\n  1064\t\n  1065\tNone changes the default answers unless the table says so. Each was measured against the default with interleaved off/on\n  1066\tpairs of whole engine runs; the numbers are in [History](#history--older-measurements), on the cards named there. Try\n  1067\tthem on your own card.\n  1068\t\n  1069\t| Switch | Applies to | What it does | Verdict |\n  1070\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n  1071\t| `STRATA_ADAPT_OVERLAP=1` | One GPU, the whole arena in RAM, no `--adapt-async`, `--pipeline-windows`, `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG`, `STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT`, `STRATA_DMA_BATCH`; never on a layer split | The adaptive tier's copies land while the next window runs, with the same tokens. The startup log names the state and why it cannot run. | Gains on RTX A4000 and Tesla P100 (+4 to +8%), loses on an RTX 5070 under Windows and an RTX 5090 Laptop. Opt-in on every backend. With the per-card defaults set `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG=1 STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT=0` beside it. |\n  1072\t| `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG=2` | CUDA | The window takes the adaptive tier's swaps once they are two windows old. | Gains on a Tesla P100; on by default on sm_86 and sm_60. |\n  1073\t| `STRATA_STAGE_PIN=1` | GGUF read in place | Page-locks the stage buffers the cache fill copies from. Unset, it stays off: with it on, IQ3_S decode was corrupted after a 4096-token prompt in an earlier release gate. | Keep off until that is fixed. |\n  1074\t| `STRATA_PCIE_BALANCE=1` | CUDA, one GPU, no `--batch`, no `--pcie-frac` | Each verify layer reads over PCIe the number of its missed experts that balances the two sides. | Helps an RTX A4000 arena (+5.6%), hurts others slightly. |\n  1075\t| `STRATA_GDN_CHUNKED=1` | CUDA, 128 or more SMs by itself (`=2` forces it); HIP gfx1100-gfx1102, gfx1150, gfx1151 | The DeltaNet recurrence of the prompt in chunks of 32 tokens (CUDA) or 16-token WMMA chunks (HIP). Changes rounding. | 1.8x on the recurrence on an RTX 5090; slower on smaller cards. See [AMD_HIP.md](AMD_HIP.md#opt-in-chunked-prompt-gdn-on-gfx11). |\n  1076\t| `STRATA_FS_SLOTS=N` | CUDA | Foresight swap space: N VRAM slots per layer refilled with missed and predicted experts. | The smaller cache cost more than the slots returned in every box measured. Left off. |\n  1077\t| `STRATA_MMQ_DIRECT=1` | CUDA, native packs; needs a build with `-DSTRATA_MMQ_TAB=ON` (off by default because it costs 36-38 MiB of VRAM per GPU) | The prompt's MMQ reads resident experts in their cache slots instead of copying them. Same bits. | Faster \"read in\" time with every expert resident. |\n  1078\t| `--prefill-head-alias` | `--serve`, one GPU | The output head's VRAM holds the prompt buffers while a prompt is read ([PREFILL_HEAD_ALIAS.md](PREFILL_HEAD_ALIAS.md)). | For 8 GB cards. |\n  1079\t| `STRATA_EMB_REUSE_ACCOUNT=1` | CUDA | The planner counts the VRAM saved because the half-output buffer `bo` reuses the embedding buffer `emb`, so the auto chunk can grow. Changes prompt rounding. | Prompt about +3.9% on an RTX 3060, no KL measurement yet. |\n  1080\t| `STRATA_VERIFY_ASYNC_LAUNCH=1` | CUDA, single-request verifier | Runs the CUDA graph launch of a verify window on a separate host thread so the caller keeps serving CPU expert doorbells. A diagnostic for Compute Sanitizer runs. | |\n  1081\t| `STRATA_CPU_IQ_ALL_EXACT1=1` | AVX2 CPUs without AVX-512 | Strata's AVX-2 decode for one-token i-quant gate/up rows, with ggml's float order, so the output does not change. On by default on sm_86. | End to end +0.4 to +2.5%, so left off elsewhere. |\n  1082\t| `STRATA_Q2_BITPLANE=1` | AVX2 CPUs without AVX-512 | A bit-plane row kernel for Q2_0. Changes the last bits. | |\n  1083\t| `STRATA_NO_AVXVNNI=1` | CPUs with AVX-VNNI | Turns the AVX-VNNI row forms off (they give the same bits, 4-25% faster rows). | A diagnostic. |\n  1084\t| `STRATA_PREFILL_EQUAL=1` | CUDA | A prompt segment is read in chunks of equal size, not full chunks and a short last one. Changes rounding. | |\n  1085\t| `STRATA_OWNED_PRICE=exact` | CUDA | The cache sizing prices the prompt path's own buffers by their real allocation. | |\n  1086\t| `--lookup-max-t N` (up to 16), `--lookup-sizes A,B` | CUDA, one conversation on one GPU (a layer split, batch and pipelined windows stay at 8) | Prompt-lookup windows up to N tokens while the MTP keeps `--spec`. A window past 8 tokens runs as two groups of 8; each size is a CUDA graph of its own (about 20-35 MiB), a size that finds no VRAM is dropped. Same answers on file rewrites. On by default at 12 in the serve engine of the 8-row CUDA build on one GPU (no batch, DFlash2 or layer split); `--lookup-max-t 0` or `STRATA_LOOKUP_MAX_T=0` turns it off. | File rewrites and the edit turns of an agent loop decode +11 to +14% (RTX A6000 IQ3_S, greedy and sampled), +13 to +18% on a Tesla P100 IQ3_XXS; ordinary prose and code are within 1% at 12. 16 gains 1-3 points more on rewrites and costs 3-4% on some prose. A wide-window build (`-DSTRATA_WINDOW_MAX_T=12` or 16) gains less (+7 to +9%) and is not needed. |\n  1087\t| `STRATA_PF_GROUP_SCRATCH=1`, `STRATA_PF_HC_REGION=1` | CUDA, prompt path on MMQ (not the fused int8 experts) | The prompt path's gate/up output, SwiGLU and q8_1 copy hold one expert group's rows, and the hyper-connection read's scratch shares the attention/MoE region. Same bits at an equal chunk. A line at start says when they took effect or why GROUP_SCRATCH is ignored. | Frees 0.3-0.5 GiB of borrowed cache slots so `--prefill auto` can pick a bigger chunk; the speed gain showed only with `STRATA_PREFILL_CPU_SHARE=0` (+7 to +11% on an emulated 8 GB card), because the bigger chunk leaves the CPU-share range. |\n  1088\t| `STRATA_PW_WITH_BATCH=1` | CUDA/HIP layer split with `--pipeline-windows 2` and `--batch` slots | A request that is alone keeps the pipelined windows beside the batch slots; batch windows run without them. | 2x RTX A6000 IQ3_S: lone request +14% on code, +9% on a file rewrite, +3% on a long decode; two requests at once not slower. The second verifier takes about 270 MiB of the first card. |\n  1089\t| `STRATA_MTP_ROOM_MIN=TOKENS` | CUDA, `--serve` with `--mtp`, no `--batch` | A request that reads at least TOKENS prompt tokens uses the draft layer's 512 decode-only experts (about 675 MiB) as extra ring slots while it is read, then gets them back. | The author measured +17 to +23% prompt on an 8 GB RTX 2060 SUPER; on a P100 limited to 8 GB (ring 8 to 311 slots) it gave +0.5% (CPU share off) and nothing with defaults. Off by default. |\n  1090\t| `STRATA_IQ_MT_MIN=1`, `STRATA_IQ3S_MT1=1` | CPU | See [Draft layer](#draft-layer) (reproducible output; the second gives IQ3_S the multi-token kernel for one token on Alder Lake and later without AVX-512). | |\n  1091\t| `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR`, `STRATA_ROUTE_RESIDENT`, `STRATA_ROUTE_RESIDENT_MTP` | CUDA | Routing changes that add speed and also add KL. | Off by default. `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR=1.5` makes the router prefer experts that are already on the card; it is the one worth trying, on a discrete NVIDIA card of 13 GiB or less (an RTX 3060 12 GB, IQ3_XXS: story +15%, code +37% decode against off). It is an opt-in because it can repeat itself and give weaker results in long agent sessions (#4402; our own check on that card: out of 10 agent tasks 4.5 passed on average over 4 runs at 1.5, against 5.6 over 7 runs with the prior off, where the off runs alone ranged from 4 to 6; a stronger setting, 2.0, showed copy and code defects). Recommended for a 12 GB card used mostly for chat and short answers, not for long agent runs. 0.2.7 turned 1.5 on by itself on those cards and 0.2.8 took that back (owner decision of 2026-10-29): with nothing set, the engine routes exactly as 0.2.6 did (the same as `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR=0` and as `STRATA_DEFAULTS=026`). Turn it on: the Expert-settings row \"Faster answers on 12 GB NVIDIA cards\" (it writes `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR=1.5`; shown only for a discrete NVIDIA card of 13 GiB or less), `strata launch --set STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR=1.5`, or the variable itself. Any value you set wins on every card; it is never on by itself. |\n  1092\t| `STRATA_HOST_CORE=last\\|sibling` (or `--host-core`) | Windows | `last` puts the host thread on the last physical core, away from the core that gets the GPU's interrupts. `sibling` keeps it on the first core's other hardware thread, so the workers keep every core. The default `first` leaves a hybrid CPU as it is. | One layer-split box went from a ±6% swing to steady and 8% faster. |\n  1093\t| `--pool-affinity measure` | Hybrid CPUs, native packs | Times the `all` and `p-cores` layouts at start (about a second) and takes the faster. The measurement is taken once; where the E-cores' speed changes while the engine runs, `--pool-affinity p-cores` is the steady choice. | |\n  1094\t| `STRATA_KEEP_EMPTY_TURNS=1`, `STRATA_TOPK_STREAM=0` | | See [TROUBLESHOOTING.md](TROUBLESHOOTING.md). | |\n  1095\t| HIP `STRATA_DENSE_MMQ=1`, `STRATA_HIP_ADAPT_KERNEL_COPY=1` | HIP | See [AMD_HIP.md](AMD_HIP.md#model-and-serving-configuration). | |\n  1096\t\n  1097\t### CUDA opt-in kernels (sm_80 and newer)\n  1098\t\n  1099\tEach was measured as an A/B on an RTX 3060 and an RTX A4000; the gain is small there and larger on the Turing cards the authors\n  1100\tmeasured. \"Exact\" means the 10-prompt and 4K/20K identity gate with the switch on equals the gate with it off.\n  1101\t\n  1102\t| Switch | What | Answers |\n  1103\t| --- | --- | --- |\n  1104\t| `STRATA_ATTN_DAILY=1` | Decode attention, two cells per step | Exact; on by default on sm_86 and 12.0 |\n  1105\t| `STRATA_QSA_ROWS=1` ([QSAROWS.md](QSAROWS.md)) | Block scores of a verify window with queries in registers | Exact; on by default on sm_86 |\n  1106\t| `STRATA_EXPDEC=1` ([EXPERTDEC.md](EXPERTDEC.md)) | Gate/up of the window's resident experts with the q8_1 rows in shared memory | Exact |\n  1107\t| `STRATA_SIDE_MIXER=1/2` | The mixer's input-only work on graph branches | Exact; on by default on sm_86, sm_60 and 12.0 |\n  1108\t| `STRATA_B6_MMVQ_ROWS=1` | Exact multi-column MMVQ reusing the activation fragment across rows | Exact; on by default on sm_86 and sm_60 |\n  1109\t| `STRATA_QSA_SPLIT_ATTN=1` | Decode attention split across blocks on the int8 mma path (sm_80+, int8 KV) | Rounding level |\n  1110\t| `STRATA_HC_UPMIX=1` | Hyper-connection up projection and `gr_mix_r` in one kernel (sm_80+) | Exact |\n  1111\t| `STRATA_PROMPT_ATTN_IMMA` | Int8-KV prompt attention on INT8 tensor cores | Rounding level. On by default since 0.2.7 on NVIDIA Ampere and newer (+1.1% to +1.8% prompt at 8K/16K on an RTX PRO 5000 and 2x RTX A4000); `=0` brings the 0.2.6 kernel back. |\n  1112\t| `STRATA_GR_V3` | The hyper-connection read split over two kernels | Rounding level. On by default since 0.2.7 on a discrete compute capability 12.0 card (RTX 50, RTX PRO Blackwell) with more than 13 GiB (RTX PRO 5000: +1.2% decode with identical tokens); slower on sm_86, so off there. `=0` / `=1` forces it off / on. |\n  1113\t| `STRATA_HC_REQ8=1` | Hyper-connection projections as int8 plus a scale per 32 (more expert-cache slots) | Rounding level |\n  1114\t| `STRATA_QUANT_GATHER=1`, `STRATA_PREFILL_GROUP_GATHER=0` | Per-row gather for the MoE input quantization; the short-prompt grouped gather off | No gain measured; keep the defaults |\n  1115\t\n  1116\t`STRATA_DEQUANT_WIDE` (wide dequantization of Q4_K, Q5_K, Q6_K and IQ4_XS for the prompt's dense weights) is on by default on\n  1117\tsm_86 and sm_61, bit-exact. `STRATA_DEQUANT_WIDE=0` turns it off.\n  1118\t\n  1119\t`STRATA_PREFILL_ROUTED_STREAM=1` (opt-in; the streamed prompt walk of a chunk of 1,024 tokens or more, one GPU, the\n  1120\tissuer thread): the issuer keeps at most `STRATA_PREFILL_ROUTED_STREAM_DEPTH` copies (default 16) queued ahead of the\n  1121\tPCIe link, each layer's routing is handed to it as soon as the layer's router has run, and an expert not yet copied that\n  1122\tno token of the chunk routes to is not copied at all. The routed experts go into the same ring slots as without the\n  1123\tswitch, so the prompt's results are the same bits, and the slots the prompt borrowed are refilled from RAM as before.\n  1124\tWith `STRATA_TRACE=1` each chunk prints how many ring entries it copied and how many it dropped. With\n  1125\t`STRATA_PREFILL_COPY_EVENT_EVERY` above 1 or `STRATA_PREFILL_COPY_WAIT_DEDUPE=1` the switch stays off: those keep the\n  1126\tdefault issuer, whose copy records the routed walk does not write. When the switch is set but a streamed chunk keeps the\n  1127\tdefault walk (these two, `STRATA_PREFILL_ISSUER=0` or the peer prompt ring), the engine prints the reason once. On an\n  1128\tRTX 3090 (PCIe 3.0 x16, 250 W) with the Swift IQ3_XXS pack at the v0.2.6 release defaults, 23% fewer experts were\n  1129\tstreamed for a 2K prompt and 4% fewer at 32K (8,192-token chunks); a 1,790-token follow-up read on an 18.8K-token\n  1130\tconversation took 18% less time and fresh 2K, 4K and 32K reads ran 20%, 8% and 5% faster (4 pairs), with byte-identical\n  1131\treplies in a deterministic lane at 1K to 32K.\n  1132\t\n  1133\t### RTX 20 (Turing, sm_75) kernels (opt-in)\n  1134\t\n  1135\tKernels by rafatxf (#2069-#2072, #2093, #2220-#2222), imanu86 (#2081) and xrip (#1821) for compute capability 7.5 are bitwise\n  1136\tequal to the ones they replace. Nobody on the team has an sm_75 card to confirm them, so they are off until asked for.\n  1137\t`STRATA_SM75_PERF=1` turns on every bitwise Turing kernel on a 7.5 card (no other card is affected). Each has its own variable\n  1138\tthat wins in both directions: `STRATA_SM75_IL`, `STRATA_SM75_EXP`, `STRATA_GR_UP75` / `STRATA_GR_DOWN_TG`, `STRATA_SCORES_HALVE`,\n  1139\t`STRATA_TOPK_SPLIT`, `STRATA_TOPK_SAMPLE`, `STRATA_ATTN_V3`, `STRATA_Q2_WARP`, `STRATA_FUSE_WAIT=2`, `STRATA_QIL`,\n  1140\t`STRATA_TC_SM75`, `STRATA_PA_V1_PREFETCH`, `STRATA_SCORES_THREAD` (a thread-per-head block scorer; `=1` works on any CUDA card).\n  1141\tOptions that change answers stay separate: `STRATA_SELECT_SIMT=1|2`, `STRATA_PA_V2T=1`, `STRATA_PF_FUSED=1 STRATA_PF_FUSED_SM75=1`\n  1142\t(Q2_0 pack, 7.5 card only), `STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1` (the native Q2_0 pack's prompt experts on sm_80+, below) and\n  1143\t`STRATA_BF16_TC=1` with `STRATA_XN_F16`.\n  1144\t\n  1145\t### The native Q2_0 pack's prompt experts on the fused int8 kernels (`STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1`, opt-in, sm_80+)\n  1146\t\n  1147\tA PC whose CPU lacks the AVX-512 set that setup looks for gets the native Q2_0 pack. On a CUDA card of sm_80 or newer its\n  1148\tQ2_0 / Q2_0 layers read prompts through MMQ's Q2_0 kernel; the fused int8 kernel for that format (#2145) is set up only on a\n  1149\t7.5 card. `STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1` sets it up on any sm_80+ card and takes it for those layers. It has its own variable, so\n  1150\t`STRATA_PF_FUSED=1` keeps its bits, and `STRATA_PF_FUSED=0` or `STRATA_PF_FUSED_NATIVE=0` still turn it off. Unset, the engine\n  1151\tis unchanged (first-token logits byte-identical to v0.2.2 on 40 prompts of 1-5K tokens, the author's check). With the switch\n  1152\tthe prompt arithmetic changes and the generated text can change.\n  1153\t\n  1154\tThe author measured it on an RTX 5080 16 GB (sm_120, Linux, CUDA 13.0), i9-14900K, 2,100 experts in VRAM, CPU prompt share off,\n  1155\tagainst the same build unset. At the same 4,864-token chunk (4 interleaved rounds) the 4K, 16K and 23K prompts read 2-3.5%\n  1156\tfaster (4/4 rounds each). With `--prefill auto` (5 rounds) the fused path's smaller buffers make the chunk 5,632 tokens instead of\n  1157\t4,864 and the 16K prompt reads 32-33% faster (2,592 -> 3,438 tok/s), the 23K one 14%, a 4K one 0-2%; so most of the gain is the\n  1158\tlarger chunk, not the kernel. One layer (`prefill_fused_iq_test`, Q2_0 / Q2_0) runs at 1.92x / 1.37x / 1.30x the MMQ path at\n  1159\t2,048 / 4,864 / 8,192 tokens. First-token KL against the FP16 prompt path over 30 prompts of 1.5-4.6K tokens: 0.0110 (MMQ\n  1160\t0.0112), top-1 agreement 28/30 (MMQ 29/30). Not measured: a card that holds every expert, other sm_80+ cards, Windows, the\n  1161\tgenerated text. On an RTX A4000 the same kernel in #2145 stopped matching the old output and read 4K prompts 4.5% slower, so\n  1162\tAmpere is not verified. The ctest entry `prefill_fused_iq_test_q2n` runs the Q2_0 / Q2_0 layer with the switch alone (it skips\n  1163\tbelow sm_80 and without a card).\n  1164\t\n  1165\t---\n  1166\t\n  1167\t## CPU workers and thread placement\n  1168\t\n  1169\t### How many CPU threads (`--pool-workers`)\n  1170\t\n  1171\tWithout the setting the engine picks the number of CPU expert workers from the CPU, not from the count of logical threads: one\n  1172\tworker per physical core (never an SMT sibling) except the core of the main thread, one core less on a CPU with 17 or more\n  1173\tcores, only the cores of the main thread's socket on a multi-socket machine, at most 32, no more than a container's CPU limit\n  1174\tallows, and on a hybrid CPU with more efficiency than performance cores the performance cores but one plus half of the\n  1175\tefficiency cores. The start line says what bounded the number (`CPU pool: 31 workers by default (32 physical cores, ...)`).\n  1176\tWorkers wait by spinning, so a worker without a core of its own takes time from the others. A count in the config is always\n  1177\tused as given; when it is above what the CPU uses well, the engine prints a warning with the recommended number\n  1178\t(`strata cpu pool: warning: ...`). Setup writes no worker count except the hybrid CPU's, and gives the CPU vision encoder at most 16\n  1179\tthreads (`--threads` in the `vision` entry changes that).\n  1180\t\n  1181\t`--pool-tasks N` sets the target total number of row tasks in each batched CPU expert gate/up and down phase (the default `0` keeps\n  1182\tthree per participating thread; values `1..4096` are capped by each phase's row count). More tasks can reduce imbalance between\n  1183\tcores and add scheduling overhead, so compare against `0` with the same worker count. Add `\"--pool-tasks\", \"192\"` to the `args`\n  1184\tlist and restart. Setup's `--calibrate` does not tune it.\n  1185\t\n  1186\t### Two sockets and NUMA\n  1187\t\n  1188\tThe default uses the host's socket only. With 2 or more NUMA nodes the pool splits itself by node on Linux: one slice per\n  1189\tnode, each with its own task counter, and each node's half of every CPU-side expert's rows is placed in that node's memory. The\n  1190\tdefault worker count under the split is the physical cores of both sockets minus the host core (minus one more from 16 workers\n  1191\ton, at most 32). An explicit `--pool-workers N` is spread alternately across the sockets.\n  1192\t\n  1193\t- It runs when Linux has exactly 2 nodes holding the pool's threads, balanced slices, pinned threads and no\n  1194\t  `STRATA_EXCHANGE_ROTATE=1`; otherwise the log says `NUMA pool off: <reason>` and the pool runs as before. One node is exactly\n  1195\t  the pool as before and logs nothing. Windows reads the node count but does not split yet.\n  1196\t- `STRATA_NUMA_POOL=0` turns it off (also `off`, `false`, `no`) and wins over `STRATA_NUMA_SPLIT=1`. `STRATA_NUMA_SPLIT=1` asks for\n  1197\t  the split on any machine; `=0` is off; `=test` builds the slices on any machine for scheduling tests, not for speed.\n  1198\t- Only native expert layers are split. If a node lacks free memory for its half, the log warns `NUMA placement at risk` and\n  1199\t  after the load prints FAILED when a node holds under 95% of its slice.\n  1200\t- Do not wrap the server in `numactl --cpunodebind` when testing workers across sockets: it pins them to one node.\n  1201\t- Workers on both sockets paid off on one box and cost on another, so measure on yours: `--pool-workers N` with N above one\n  1202\t  socket's cores, a fresh server per value, the same prompt, decode tok/s from the log.\n  1203\t- Check the IOMMU. On a distro kernel where it translates every DMA (`cat /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*/type | sort | uniq -c`\n  1204\t  shows `DMA-FQ`), pinned copies ran slower than with `iommu=pt` on the kernel command line.\n  1205\t\n  1206\t### Which logical processors (`--pool-cores`, experimental)\n  1207\t\n  1208\tThe engine starts one worker per physical core and keeps the first core for the main thread (the one that waits for the GPU).\n  1209\tThe start-up log says where everything sits:\n  1210\t\n  1211\t```\n  1212\tstrata generate: CPU workers on logical processors 2,4,...,30 (15 workers, one per physical core; host on 0)\n  1213\t```\n  1214\t\n  1215\tWindows numbers the two threads of a core next to each other, so the workers sit on the even numbers; Linux usually numbers the first\n  1216\tthread of every core first. The engine reads the pairing from the system (Windows `GetLogicalProcessorInformationEx`, Linux\n  1217\t`/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/topology`) and says so when the OS does not tell it. Only workers and the main thread are pinned\n  1218\t(thread names: `strata-pool-N`, `strata-host`, and `strata-stage`, `strata-adapt`, `strata-io` for helpers), so a task manager\n  1219\tshowing only the first logical processors busy does not mean the pool is misplaced. The CPU picture encoder is a separate process\n  1220\tthat the OS places.\n  1221\t\n  1222\t`--pool-cores LIST` (or `STRATA_POOL_CORES=LIST`, `\"pool_cores\": \"2,4,6,8\"` in the config, or `strata launch --pool-cores 2,4,6,8`)\n  1223\tpins the workers to exactly these logical processors, in this order. Use it to leave cores for other programs, use one chiplet\n  1224\tor test a layout. The list is numbers, ranges and `first-last:step` (`0,2,4,6`, `2-14:2`), as Task Manager and `lscpu` show them.\n  1225\tThe worker count is the length of the list; `--pool-workers N` takes the first N entries. Nothing is refused. The engine warns\n  1226\twhen two entries are the hardware threads of one core, when an entry is the host thread's processor, and when one is not a\n  1227\tprocessor the process may use (it is left out). The option moves threads only and never changes a result. The Qwen3.6 engine\n  1228\t(`strata-q36`) keeps its own `--pool-workers` and default placement.\n  1229\t\n  1230\t### Other threads (`--aux-cpus`, Linux, off by default)\n  1231\t\n  1232\tA thread the engine starts after the host thread is pinned inherits the host's one CPU: the adaptive tier's job thread, prefill\n  1233\thelpers, the router look-ahead. `--aux-cpus` puts every thread that is neither a pool worker nor the host on spare CPUs. It\n  1234\tmoves threads only and never changes a result.\n  1235\t\n  1236\t- `--aux-cpus auto` (or `STRATA_AUX_CPUS=auto`): the SMT siblings of the host's core first, then the CPUs of physical cores\n  1237\t  that have no pool worker. A list (`--aux-cpus 24,26` or `24-27`) is used as given, minus the host's and the workers' CPUs.\n  1238\t  `--aux-cpus off` is the default. When no CPU is spare the feature does nothing and the start-up line says so.\n  1239\t- Threads the engine starts pin themselves first; threads started by someone else (the CUDA driver's) are moved by a sweep over\n  1240\t  `/proc/self/task` once the pool exists and again at the start of each request. An explicit `STRATA_ADAPT_JOB_CPU` wins for the\n  1241\t  adaptive tier's job thread. `STRATA_AUX_STAGER=0` leaves the prefill copy threads where they started.\n  1242\t- Per request: `\"strata_tune\": {\"aux_cpus\": 1}` moves the threads to the set; `0` puts them back.\n  1243\t- It prints `strata aux cpus: threads that are neither pool workers nor the host go to CPUs 24 (on; N threads placed so far)`.\n  1244\t- Not covered: other processes and the engine's host thread. The measurement is one machine: see [History](#history--older-measurements).\n  1245\t\n  1246\t---\n  1247\t\n  1248\t<a id=\"experimental-speed-projection\"></a>\n  1249\t## Experimental speed projection (experimental, off by default)\n  1250\t\n  1251\tThis is an experiment. It ships with Strata and stays off unless you turn it on. It is a 480 KB control vector for the original\n  1252\tQwen3.8-Flash-Next (`data/experimental-speed-projection/`, not Swift 1.5). After each of layers 4-44 the engine removes one\n  1253\tdirection from every hyper-connection stream: `h -= (h . v) v`.\n  1254\t\n  1255\t**What it changes.** The vector's package describes it as a **refusal-direction projection**. With it the model declines far fewer\n  1256\trequests, and removing refusals removes a safety behavior, so you are responsible for what the model writes with it on. It also\n  1257\tshifts ordinary answers a little and costs 0.2-0.4% per token. What a chat's tokens/s does depends on the text, so measure it on\n  1258\tyour own prompts. The Monitor marks every request ESP or stock.\n  1259\t\n  1260\t**Turning it on.** In a source checkout, `START-HERE.bat --setup` asks \"Turn on the experimental speed projection?\" (default no), or pass\n  1261\t`--experimental-speed-projection on` (`off`, or a path to another vector GGUF). It writes these engine flags into `strata-<model>.json`:\n  1262\t\n  1263\t```\n  1264\t--control-vector-scaled <Strata>\\data\\experimental-speed-projection\\Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-experimental-speed-projection.gguf:1.0\n  1265\t--control-vector-layer-range 4 44 --cvec-mode project --cvec-dir per-layer\n  1266\t```\n  1267\t\n  1268\tThe engine log then says `control vector mode = project, dir = per-layer, layers 4..44 (41 steered)`. A loaded vector is on for\n  1269\tevery request unless it says otherwise: Chat settings has a switch, and the API takes `\"experimental_speed_projection\": false`\n  1270\t(config default: `\"sampling\": {\"experimental_speed_projection\": false}`). Switching drops the conversation cache once. Switched\n  1271\toff, the output is token for token the stock model's.\n  1272\t\n  1273\t---\n  1274\t\n  1275\t## RTX 30 series (sm_86) and Tesla P100 (sm_60): bitwise decode switches on by default\n  1276\t\n  1277\tSwitches that are bit-exact on every card are on by default per architecture (`include/strata/core/nv_defaults.hpp`, set before\n  1278\tthe engine reads its switches; a switch you set yourself, to anything, is kept). The table is used when every visible card has\n  1279\tthat one compute capability. The identity gate (10 prompts plus exact_pp 4K/20K, fixed flags) is identical with the sets on.\n  1280\t\n  1281\t| Architecture | On by default | Off switch |\n  1282\t| --- | --- | --- |\n  1283\t| sm_86 (RTX 3050-3090, RTX A2000-A6000) | `STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT=1`, `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG=2`, `STRATA_CPU_IQ_ALL_EXACT1=1`, `STRATA_SIDE_MIXER=2`, `STRATA_ATTN_DAILY=1`, `STRATA_GR_UP75=1`, `STRATA_GU_SWIGLU=1`, `STRATA_QSA_ROWS=1`, `STRATA_B6_MMVQ_ROWS=1` | `STRATA_SM86_PERF=0` |\n  1284\t| sm_60 (Tesla P100) | `STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT=1`, `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG=2`, `STRATA_SIDE_MIXER=2`, `STRATA_FUSE_WAIT=2`, `STRATA_B6_MMVQ_ROWS=1`, `STRATA_FUSE_DECODE=1`, `STRATA_GR_UP75=1` | `STRATA_SM60_PERF=0` |\n  1285\t| sm_120 (RTX 50 and RTX PRO Blackwell) | `STRATA_SIDE_MIXER=2`, `STRATA_ATTN_DAILY=1`, `STRATA_GR_UP75=1`, `STRATA_GR_DOWN_TG=1`, `STRATA_GU_SWIGLU=1` | `STRATA_SM120_PERF=0` |\n  1286\t\n  1287\t`STRATA_ADAPT_OVERLAP=1` does not run beside `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG` / `STRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT`: set `STRATA_ADAPT_LAG=1\n  1288\tSTRATA_ADAPT_NOWAIT=0` with it. Switches that did not pay or were mixed are left out of every set: `STRATA_EXPDEC`,\n  1289\t`STRATA_GR_NORM_FAST`, `STRATA_RAM_ADAPT`, `STRATA_KV_HOST_REGISTER`, `STRATA_HOST_CORE`, `STRATA_GR_DOWN_TG` (sm_86),\n  1290\t`STRATA_DEQUANT_WIDE` beyond its own default. `STRATA_EXCHANGE_ROTATE` never reached READY on the RTX 3060 and\n  1291\t`STRATA_PCIE_BALANCE` is slower on a single card.\n  1292\t\n  1293\tOn 12.0 cards (sm_120) these also apply:\n  1294\t\n  1295\t- The interleaved small-batch matvec (`native_mmvq_il`, [MMVQ_IL_TABLE.md](MMVQ_IL_TABLE.md)) runs 5 to 8 tokens, with a 12.0 table\n  1296\t  and, on cards with 80 or more SMs, its own 2-4 token table. `STRATA_MMVQ_IL_WIDE=0` and `STRATA_MMVQ_IL_120=0` turn them off.\n  1297\t  RTX 40 cards (compute capability 8.9) have their own 2-4 token tables and a one-token Q3_K row, on by default in 0.2.7 and not yet measured\n  1298\t  on an RTX 40 card by us (same output bit for bit): `STRATA_MMVQ_IL_89=0` or `STRATA_DEFAULTS=026` turns them off.\n  1299\t- With 80 or more SMs the native packs' prompt experts run on the fused int8 kernels (`STRATA_PF_FUSED`) for IQ2_S, IQ3_XXS,\n  1300\t  IQ3_S and IQ4_XS gate/up. IQ2_XXS and IQ2_XS keep MMQ. Smaller 12.0 cards (an RTX 5070 with 48 SMs) keep MMQ because the fused\n  1301\t  kernels were 4.7% slower on 20K prompts there. `STRATA_PF_FUSED=0` keeps MMQ.\n  1302\t- `--spec 4 --spec-min-p 0.5` stays the default depth.\n  1303\t- Setup (Linux, not WSL): when the RAM covers the model's need plus the n-gram table plus 16 GB (Q2_0 93 GB, IQ3_S 107 GB), the table\n  1304\t  is kept in RAM (`--ple-io ram`), so a new prompt does not wait for its rows from the SSD. With RAM short, ram mode costs\n  1305\t  prompt time and start-up time, which is why smaller PCs keep the SSD reads.\n  1306\t\n  1307\tA prompt chunk above 8192 (`--prefill auto:16384` or `auto:32768`) is not bit-exact on CUDA, so the default stays 8192 on\n  1308\tNVIDIA. It is exact on gfx12, where 16384 is the default. `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR` raises KL and is an opt-in again in 0.2.8 (0.2.7 set 1.5 by itself on a discrete NVIDIA card of 13 GiB or less, and the owner took that back on 2026-10-29 because of repetition loops and weaker results in long agent sessions, #4402). `STRATA_ROUTE_PRIOR=1.5` on an RTX 3060 12 GB: story +15%, code +37%, 80 tasks with no copy or code errors, but 4.5 of 10 agent tasks against 5.6 with it off.\n  1309\t\n  1310\t---\n  1311\t\n  1312\t## Troubleshooting\n  1313\t\n  1314\t[TROUBLESHOOTING.md](TROUBLESHOOTING.md) is the main page. These are the cases specific to the server and engine.\n  1315\t\n  1316\t| Symptom | What to do |\n  1317\t| --- | --- |\n  1318\t| `the NVIDIA driver is too old` | Update the driver, restart, run setup again. |\n  1319\t| Python or the build tools could not be installed | Install what it names (links are printed), then run it again. Everything already done is kept. |\n  1320\t| `port 8080 is already in use` | Strata is already running, or another program uses the port: `strata launch --port 8081` (source checkout: `START-HERE.bat --port 8081`). |\n  1321\t| `cudaHostRegister ... out of memory` in the log | Normal on Windows: the engine pins the experts in per-layer slices instead. A problem only if the load then fails. |\n  1322\t| `ExpertCache: cudaMalloc(...) failed: out of memory` although VRAM is free | Windows' page file is off or tiny. Every allocation on the card is also charged to Windows' commit (RAM + page file). Set a fixed page file size, the same initial and maximum (for example 65536 MB), and restart. The engine retries with a smaller cache, and setup warns about a page file under 4 GB. |\n  1323\t| The first start takes minutes, or the PC freezes for a few minutes | The engine is reading 34-55 GB into RAM, pinning part of it and sizing the expert cache. Wait. The second start is faster while the files are in the OS cache. Still frozen after 10 minutes: restart, close other programs, or pick a smaller size. |\n  1324\t| `the engine stopped unexpectedly (exit code ...)` | The engine ended mid-answer, usually out of RAM (Linux ends the biggest program: `sudo dmesg \\| grep -i -E 'killed process\\|out of memory'`). The next request starts it again. If it repeats, close other programs or pick a smaller size. The server warns at start when the experts leave less than ~6 GB of RAM. |\n  1325\t| Slow output, disk light busy | Not enough free RAM: close other programs, or choose Q2_0 or IQ2_XS. |\n  1326\t| `prompt ... exceeds the context` | The request is longer than the context you chose: run setup again with a bigger `--context`. |\n  1327\t| Slower than the tables | A monitor plugged into the GPU and other GPU programs take VRAM from the expert cache. RAM below its rated speed (enable EXPO or XMP in the BIOS) slows the CPU half. |\n  1328\t| `this server was started without the vision encoder` | The model was set up for text only: run setup again with `--vision gpu` or `--vision cpu`. |\n  1329\t| Setup puts the model on an old drive after you moved or reinstalled Strata | Setup remembers the data folder in its settings file (`%APPDATA%\\Strata\\settings.json` on Windows, `~/.config/strata/settings.json` on Linux; the `data_dir` entry). Choose another place with `--data-dir DIR` (model files, packs, MTP layer) or `--models-dir DIR` (the GGUF files only), or delete the `data_dir` line. `--gguf-dir DIR` uses GGUF files you already have without copying. |\n  1330\t| A picture is refused or `cannot read the image` | The file is not a picture Pillow can open (JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF, BMP, TIFF, AVIF work). |\n  1331\t| Pictures are slow (3-30 s) | The encoder runs on the CPU, the default under 24 GB of graphics memory. Run setup again with `--vision gpu` (needs ~1.4 GB of VRAM, taken from the expert cache). |\n  1332\t| Output much slower than the tables, only with a large `--context`, and the start shows a small `... MiB of VRAM free with everything loaded` | The expert cache does not fit the card at that context. Use `--expert-cache auto`, a smaller number, or a larger `--vram-reserve-mib`. See [The expert cache size is a budget](#the-expert-cache-size-is-a-budget). |\n  1333\t| A request ends with `no progress for ... s ... (issue #29)`, with a stall report under it | A request that stops moving ends with an error after 2 minutes, and the next request starts the engine again. `STRATA_WATCHDOG_S` sets the time in seconds (`0` turns it off). Open an issue with the line, the stall report and, on Windows, the `strata-stall-<pid>.dmp` file. |\n  1334\t| `no progress for 60 s ... reading the prompt` on Linux with `threads waiting on the disk (state D)` in the stall report | The engine waits for the drive: the n-gram table is read at random (`--ple-io direct`), which a rotational disk cannot keep up with. `--ple-io ram` (needs RAM for the table) or the model on an SSD fixes it. Setup adds `--ple-io ram` itself on a rotational disk when the RAM holds the table. |\n  1335\t| `the engine said nothing for ... s during the request` | The engine and the server lost step. The server ends the engine after `engine_silence_s` (300 by default), the request ends with an error and the next request starts the engine again. If it repeats, add the end of the engine log to issue #481. |\n  1336\t| `the engine said nothing for ... s and used no CPU or disk in that time (frozen ...)` | A quicker version of the check above for an engine that stopped, not slowed: after 90 s the server ends it. `STRATA_ENGINE_STALL_S=180` sets the time, `0` turns it off (needs `psutil`, which setup installs). |\n  1337\t| The desktop app or the Terminal could not find an engine, or a Mac says an app is \"damaged\" | [TROUBLESHOOTING.md](TROUBLESHOOTING.md#the-desktop-app-and-the-terminal). |\n  1338\t| Anything else | The engine log is `strata-<model>.log` in this folder. In the app: Settings, Engine, Diagnostics, View logs, Save logs. In the Terminal: `strata doctor`. |\n  1339\t\n  1340\t---\n  1341\t\n  1342\t## How it works\n  1343\t\n  1344\t<p align=\"center\"><img src=\"paper/tiers.svg\" width=\"760\" alt=\"memory tiers\"></p>\n  1345\t\n  1346\t- **GPU (VRAM):** attention and DeltaNet mixers, the gated-residual weights, routers, shared experts, the output head, the MTP draft\n  1347\t  layer, the KV cache (from 64K only its most-read part; the rest streams from RAM) and an **expert cache** that fills the rest of\n  1348\t  VRAM with the most-used experts and adapts to the conversation.\n  1349\t- **RAM:** all 24,576 experts, pinned. The CPU computes the experts that are not on the GPU in place, at the same time as the GPU\n  1350\t  works on the cached ones (AVX-512 and AVX2 kernels, ggml's for the i-quants).\n  1351\t- **SSD:** the 28.8 GB n-gram table, a few rows per token, read unbuffered past the OS cache (`--ple-io direct`). The engine reads the\n  1352\t  table in the format the GGUF has it: IQ4_NL (the default table), Q4_0, Q5_0, Q5_1, Q8_0, FP8 (E4M3 with a scale) or BF16. Setup does not\n  1353\t  offer another table.\n  1354\t- **Speculation:** the model's own MTP layer drafts up to 3 tokens and one pass over all 48 layers checks them, for 2.4-3.2 tokens\n  1355\t  per pass on average. When the reply repeats the context (code edits, quoted text), prompt lookup drafts up to 5 tokens from the\n  1356\t  earlier copy, but only where its measured acceptance and cost say it pays. Drafts are checked like the MTP's, so the output is the\n  1357\t  same.\n  1358\t- **Prompts** are processed in chunks of up to 8,192 tokens (`--prefill auto`; 32,768 opt-in) with the experts streamed to the GPU\n  1359\t  over PCIe. The streamed ring is sized in bytes for the pack, and `--prefill auto` picks the largest chunk that keeps that ring\n  1360\t  full. `STRATA_RING_BYTES=0` restores the earlier ring, loan and chunk choice.\n  1361\t\n  1362\tThe full story, with measurements, bottlenecks and what comes next: **[docs/paper/Strata-Paper.pdf](paper/Strata-Paper.pdf)**.\n  1363\t\n  1364\t---\n  1365\t\n  1366\t## Credits and licenses\n  1367\t\n  1368\tStrata itself: [MIT](../LICENSE). The model files are not part of it; their licenses apply to them.\n  1369\t\n  1370\t- Model: [Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next) by the Qwen team; quantizations:\n  1371\t  [ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-GGUF). The Coder:\n  1372\t  [ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-Coder-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/ISTA-DASLab/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-Coder-GGUF)\n  1373\t  (Apache-2.0 per its card); its support in Strata came from @pjgmobile's PR #54. Swift 1.5:\n  1374\t  [ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GSQ-RCO-GGUF) by UkisAI.\n  1375\t  Their licenses apply to the weights.\n  1376\t- [llama.cpp / ggml](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp) (MIT): the i-quant formats, the GPU dot products and dequantizers\n  1377\t  transcribed in `src/kernels/cuda/iq_kernels.cu`, the CPU backend linked for the i-quant experts, the `mtmd` library behind the\n  1378\t  image encoder (`tools/vision/`), and `gguf-py` used by the tools. See `third_party/ggml/LICENSE`.\n  1379\t- [antirez/ds4](https://github.com/antirez/ds4) (MIT, commit 890898cf): the Metal vision-tower kernels of the Mac DeepSeek engine (`metal/src/engine/msl/ds4_vision.metal`, `ds4_vision_metal.cpp`) follow its `metal/deepseek4_vision.metal` and the tower's host code. See `third_party/antirez-ds4/NOTICE.md`.\n  1380\t- Ideas from [Splash](https://github.com/incoai/splash), [ninfer](https://github.com/Neroued/ninfer) and\n  1381\t  [HyperQwen](https://github.com/syv-ai/HyperQwen); references in the paper.\n  1382\t- The DFlash2 drafter's Q4_0 selector (174 MiB less graphics memory, same drafts and same text) is nazeshinjite's idea:\n  1383\t  [nazeshinjite/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-DFlash2-GGUF](https://huggingface.co/nazeshinjite/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-DFlash2-GGUF) (Apache-2.0) stores the drafter's\n  1384\t  two selector tables as Q4_0; Strata converts the checked pack on your computer (`tools/dflash2_pack.py --convert-selector`) and checks the bytes\n  1385\t  against the same pin.\n  1386\t- The web app's font: [Outfit](https://github.com/Outfitio/Outfit-Fonts) (SIL Open Font License 1.1, see `serve/web/fonts/OFL.txt`). Its\n  1387\t  Monitor tab started from @code-martin's dashboard idea (PR #22).\n  1388\t  Math is drawn by [KaTeX](https://katex.org) (MIT, vendored in `serve/web/vendor/katex`). The chat page's wide-window layout, svg/html preview, math, Load/Unload, saved\n  1389\t  chats, edit and rewind came from PRs #3176 (@mnott), #1628 (@thoives), #2972 and #2974 (@CC-David-CC), #3550 (@sakuumomo), #1787 (@Scotty-Hudson) and\n  1390\t  #2615 (@uppet), and the rewind choice and sending while answering from issues #3823 and #4004 (@xvcezrqpbxtpzblrt).\n  1391\t- The experimental speed projection's vector (`data/experimental-speed-projection/`): Qwen Community License 1.0, made from the model's\n  1392\t  activations (see its README).\n  1393\t\n  1394\t---\n  1395\t\n  1396\t## History / older measurements\n  1397\t\n  1398\tDated results. They were measured on the engine version and hardware named in each row. They are evidence for the defaults\n  1399\tabove, not claims about the current release or other machines. Where a row says \"default\", the setting is now on or off as\n  1400\tdescribed in the sections above. Raw data is in [`bench/results/`](../bench/results/).\n  1401\t\n  1402\t### Engine speed tables and tuning\n  1403\t\n  1404\t| Engine | Measured on | Result |\n  1405\t| --- | --- | --- |\n  1406\t| 0.1.5 | RTX 5070 12 GB, Q2_0, 262K | KV streaming: 50.9 -> 62.6 tok/s output (1,589 -> 3,872 experts in VRAM); about +6% at 128K |\n  1407\t| 0.1.8 | Setup, `--kv q4_0` | 4-bit KV (Hadamard-rotated): about 4% faster at 128K, perplexity +8-12%. [`bench/results/2026-09-27-kv-q4`](../bench/results/2026-09-27-kv-q4/README.md) |\n  1408\t| 0.1.13 | RTX 5070 12 GB, 64 GB RAM, 32K prompt | Prompt chunks of 8,192, quantized MMQ kernels: Q2_0 572 -> 1,290 tok/s, IQ3_S 383 -> 1,208. Through the server (Q2_0, 128K context): 999 tokens 353 -> 438 tok/s, 6,927 tokens 529 -> 1,077, 28,584 tokens 584 -> 1,249. Output speed unchanged. [`bench/results/2026-09-28-prefill-speed`](../bench/results/2026-09-28-prefill-speed/README.md) |\n  1409\t| 0.1.14 | RTX 5070, 4K prompt, back to back | Output 88.5 tok/s (0.1.12: 85.7). Tables before 0.1.13: [`bench/results/2026-09-24-final`](../bench/results/2026-09-24-final/matrix.md); 0.1.14: [`bench/results/2026-09-28-speed-0114`](../bench/results/2026-09-28-speed-0114/README.md) |\n  1410\t| 0.1.25 | RTX 3090, Coder, 198K context, `--kv k8v4` | 99 instead of 85 tok/s output, same needle results, prompts 2-5% slower. On an RTX 2060 SUPER 8 GB at 128K: +780 expert slots over resident K8V4 |\n  1411\t| 0.1.36 (#136) | RTX 5070, Q2_0, fused int8 kernels (RTX 30 and newer) | Prompt 4K 1,294 -> 1,570, 32K 2,170 -> 2,653, 128K 2,123 -> 2,468 tok/s (+16-22%), teacher-forced KL 0.009 at 32K vs 0.012 before. Decode on thread-block clusters (RTX 50, sm_90+): Q2_0 output 4K 89 -> 93.5, 128K 64.5 -> 76.4 tok/s. `STRATA_PF_FUSED=0