ocument re-reads up to 16K tokens (55 s at 262K) without `strata_prefix` |\n  1536\t| not stated | Codex CLI 0.160.0, Q2_0, RTX 5070 | First prompt 9,443 tokens in 10 s; later tool-loop turns reused about 96% from the cache and read the new part in 1-2 s. Compaction after an 85,000-token conversation reused 84,895 of 84,997 tokens with `codex_compaction_cache` and 41 of 80,683 without |\n  1537\t| not stated | Signalnine/q27's drift corpus, 1,462 agent turns (Qwen3.8 under Claude Code) | `tool_call_recovery`: 98.3% read as intended, 92.9% without (on by default from 0.2.8) |\n  1538\t| not stated | RTX 3080, RVN IQ3_S config, 8-turn chat replay on a 16K-token system prompt | NaN guard on and off: byte-identical answers in 3 pairs; cost 4.8 us of GPU time a window (7.8 us at most), 0.009% of decode time |\n  1539\t\n  1540\t### Context beyond 262K (contributors' runs, RTX 5080, IQ3_S, native path, PR #84)\n  1541\t\n  1542\tPer-position perplexity on the same tokens, only the scaling flag changed. At 293K tokens (1.12x the trained length) `yarn` with\n  1543\tfactor 2 lowered the NLL by 0.18 nats against `none` and `linear` 2 (2.04 vs 2.22 and 2.22), 3-4x the path noise. `linear` 2 was\n  1544\tindistinguishable from `none`, and at 2.7K and 32K no arm separated from the noise. Needle tests do not tell the arms apart (the unscaled\n  1545\tmodel also finds a needle at 413K). Long real-document Q&A worked with `yarn` 2 at 421K and `yarn` 4 at 714K (8/8 each), and a\n  1546\t1M-token `yarn` 4 run read end to end. One machine and one quant.\n  1547\t\n  1548\t### n-gram table formats and the prompt ring\n  1549\t\n  1550\t**Measured on:** 4,000 random rows against the checkpoint's own BF16 table, and 700 teacher-forced tokens on the Q2_0 model. Mean per-row\n  1551\terror: Q8_0 0.53%, FP8 2.64%, Q5_1 3.78%, Q5_0 4.25%, IQ4_NL 7.60%, Q4_1 7.80%, Q4_0 8.55% (a Q8_0 table is 54 GB, an IQ4_NL one\n  1552\t28.8 GB). Mean KL against the BF16 table: 0.0117 (Q5_0), 0.0121 (Q5_1), 0.0125 (Q8_0), 0.0128 (FP8), 0.0140 (Q4_0), 0.0146 (IQ4_NL), perplexity within\n  1553\t1% of BF16's either way.\n  1554\t\n  1555\tRTX 5070, 32K prompts against `STRATA_RING_BYTES=0`, 2-3 interleaved rounds (engine 0.1.39b, #583): IQ3_XXS +18.5% at a 1,500-slot cache and\n  1556\t+9% in another memory state, the Coder +3%, IQ3_S unchanged; 4K prompts unchanged. Teacher-forced over 2,001 tokens of a long document\n  1557\tagainst the FP16 prompt path (IQ3_XXS): 8K prompt KL 0.042 (0.1.39: 0.054), top-1 agreement 93.5% (92.2%); 32K prompt KL 0.020 (0.019),\n  1558\ttop-1 95.3% (95.0%).\n  1559\t\n  1560\t### Experimental speed projection\n  1561\t\n  1562\t**Measured on:** Q2_0, fixed experts, 2,557 teacher-forced tokens of code, a document and a chat. The top-1 token changes at 10% of positions,\n  1563\tmean KL from the stock model 0.063 nats (max 4.1), perplexity +15% on code, +2.3% on the document, +0.4% on the chat. Details:\n  1564\t`bench/results/2026-09-27-esp/`.\n  1565\t\n  1566\t### Older runs of the quality checks\n  1567\t\n  1568\t- Our small Swift 1.5 check (8 reasoning questions, default thinking, IQ2_XS): both models got 8/8; Swift used 1,234 output tokens in 28 s,\n  1569\t  the original 2,682 in 46 s. Speed: 4K IQ2_XS 465 prompt / 78.7 output tokens/s against 467 / 78.3 for the original.\n  1570\t- UD-Q4_K_XL on a 64 GB PC with a 12 GB RTX 5070: 7-8.5 tok/s, picks the same tokens as llama.cpp on the same file at 97.5-99% of the\n  1571\t  positions of short greedy answers and 90-91% after a 16K prompt ([UNSLOTH_Q4.md](UNSLOTH_Q4.md#quality-against-llamacpp-on-the-same-file)).\n  1572\t\n  1573\t## Conversation cache copies on a slow host link (CUDA, opt-in)\n  1574\t\n  1575\tWhere the card sits behind a slow host link, the conversation cache's copies are on the critical path of the next request. A turn checkpoint copies the DeltaNet state (118 MB on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next) from the card to the host. Parking a conversation copies its K/V and its checkpoints before the next prompt can start. These switches move the copies card-to-card in VRAM or onto background threads. All are off unless you set them. On HIP, gfx906 and Metal `STRATA_CKPT_ASYNC`, `STRATA_CKPT_GATHER` and `STRATA_PARK_ASYNC` stay off, and so do the two slot switches that need them. The answers and the parked images are the same bytes.\n  1576\t\n  1577\tThe times below were measured by the author of the change (linxuhao, PR #3779) on one CMP 170HX (GA100, sm_80) on a PCIe Gen2 x4 link. We have no sm_80 card and did not measure them. Some of their park figures were taken with other opt-in switches set.\n  1578\t\n  1579\t| Switch | What | Reporter's figure (CMP 170HX) |\n  1580\t|---|---|---|\n  1581\t| `STRATA_CKPT_ASYNC=1` | A turn checkpoint's state is copied card-to-card into a VRAM stage and moved to the host by a background thread. A reader waits only for the copy it needs. The stage is one buffer the size of the state, allocated at the first save; if the allocation fails the save copies as before. | checkpoint 104-105 -> 34-35 ms (fresh vectors), 71.4 -> 0.9 ms (reused vectors) |\n  1582\t| `STRATA_CKPT_VRAM_SLOTS=N` (0-16, with `STRATA_CKPT_ASYNC=1`) | The last N saved states also stay in VRAM (N x 118 MB, only while 1.5 GB stay free). A restore from one copies card-to-card. | restore 114-126 -> 28-47 ms |\n  1583\t| `STRATA_PARK_ASYNC=1` | A park copies the K/V card-to-card into a VRAM stage (only while 1.5 GB stay free) and moves it to the host in the background. The checkpoints the request drops move into the parked image instead of being copied. | park of a 3.3K / 13K-token conversation 212-217 / 329-334 -> 64-75 / 68 ms |\n  1584\t| `STRATA_CKPT_POOL_READY=N` (1-16, with `STRATA_CKPT_POOL=1`) | The checkpoint pool keeps N ready checkpoints, sized at start and topped up in the background, so a save never faults in a fresh vector. It holds N x 118 MB of host memory from start-up. `STRATA_CKPT_POOL=1` alone is unchanged: it reuses dropped checkpoints and allocates nothing ahead of time. | with `STRATA_PARK_ASYNC=1` and `STRATA_CKPT_GATHER=1`: park 21.5-40.1 / 26.9 ms |\n  1585\t| `STRATA_CKPT_GATHER=1` | A checkpoint's 37 small card-to-host copies are gathered into one buffer and fetched with one copy. Not tested with a layer split across cards. | (in the row above) |\n  1586\t| `STRATA_PARK_VRAM_MIB=N` (with `STRATA_PARK_ASYNC=1`) | A park also keeps the K/V in VRAM slots (64 MiB granules, up to N MiB, only while 1.5 GB stay free). Switching back restores it card-to-card. | second and later restores of a run 33-51 -> 7-13 ms; the first is not faster |\n  1587\t| `STRATA_PARK_TRACE=1` | Diagnostic: the park's joins and timers on stderr. | - |\n  1588\t\n  1589\tAn independent user (bitaccesscomau, one RTX 3090 on PCIe 3.0 x16) tried the `STRATA_CKPT_ASYNC=1` commit on its own on 0.2.6: the checkpoint save went from 73.2 to 62.4 ms, the first token of a short prompt came about 13 ms of 350 ms sooner (median of 4 pairs), and the answers were byte-identical. On a fast link the gain is small.\n  1590\t\n  1591\tThe serve loop's `strata trace:` lines (`STRATA_TRACE`) now end with `@<ms>`, a steady-clock timestamp.\n  1592\t\n  1593\t## CUDA opt-in decode switches measured on a CMP 170HX (sm_80; off unless you set them)\n  1594\t\n  1595\tThe author of the change (linxuhao, PR #3779) measured these on one CMP 170HX (GA100, 70 SMs) with the Qwen3.8-Flash-Next IQ3_S native pack, `--spec 3`, decode after a prompt of about 3,000 words, a fresh engine per run, reference and switch runs interleaved. We have no sm_80 card and did not measure them. Both switches keep every output bit by construction (the greedy answers to 11 prompts were the same with the switch on and off, on their card). Neither does anything on HIP, gfx906 or Metal.\n  1596\t\n  1597\t| Switch | What | Answers | Reporter's figure (CMP 170HX) |\n  1598\t|---|---|---|---|\n  1599\t| `STRATA_GRAPH_PRIO=1` | The verify window graph's kernel nodes run at their capturing stream's priority (`cudaGraphInstantiateFlagUseNodePriority`): the GDN gates and the QSA indexer branch at the highest, the shared expert at the lowest. Nothing changes on a card whose priority range is 0..0. | bit-exact | decode +1.8 % with `STRATA_QIL=1` (both runs above every reference run) |\n  1600\t| `STRATA_MMVQ_IL_PF=1` | Hoisted-load, exact-integer kernels for the interleaved small-batch matvec where the sm_80 table picks them (Q6_K only so far, [MMVQ_IL_TABLE.md](MMVQ_IL_TABLE.md)). On a card that is not sm_80 the value 1 does nothing. `=2` to `=6` force one variant everywhere (A/B only). | bit-exact | decode +1.4 % (two reference / on / reference / on sets) |\n  1601\t\n  1602\tTwo sm_80 defaults come with the same change and are on without a switch, only on a compute capability 8.0 card: the wide dense dequant kernels (`STRATA_DEQUANT_WIDE=0` turns them off) and the sm_80 rows tables of the interleaved matvec (`STRATA_MMVQ_IL_SM80=0` turns them off). Both are bitwise. The reporter's figures: prompt GPU time of a 3,259-token prompt 857 -> 792 ms for the dequant, decode +2.7..+3.2 % for the tables.\n  1603\t\n  1604\t## CUDA opt-in prompt switches for sm_80 cards (off unless you set them)\n  1605\t\n  1606\tAlso from PR #3779, measured by its author on the same CMP 170HX; none of the figures was measured here. `STRATA_PF_FUSED_NT=256` and `STRATA_PF_BATCH=N` keep every output bit. `STRATA_PF_FUSED_F16=1`, `STRATA_PF_FUSED_WS=1` and `STRATA_GDN_TC=1` change the output at rounding level: the author's teacher-forced KL against the bitwise base on 20 prompts is mean 0.0062 (F16 / WS) and 0.0157 (GDN_TC), top-1 agreement 100 %.\n  1607\t\n  1608\t| Switch | What | Reporter's figure (CMP 170HX) |\n  1609\t|---|---|---|\n  1610\t| `STRATA_PF_FUSED_NT=256` | The fused prompt-expert kernels with 256 threads and two blocks a multiprocessor (bitwise). Adds device code to every CUDA build. | one MoE layer of 3,256 tokens 8.27 ms |\n  1611\t| `STRATA_PF_BATCH=N` | Up to N experts per launch (above 128 only with the 256-thread kernels; bitwise). | with NT=256: prompts +7.6 % (4K), +4.9 % (16K), +4.3 % (52K) |\n  1612\t| `STRATA_PF_FUSED_F16=1` | f16 mma instead of the int8 products in the 256-thread kernels (rounding level). | 7.90 ms for the same layer |\n  1613\t| `STRATA_PF_FUSED_WS=1` | Warp-specialized kernel with the same F16 products; needs about 101 KB of shared memory, so sm_80 and sm_90 only (it says \"not available\" elsewhere). | 7.65 ms for the same layer |\n  1614\t| `STRATA_GDN_TC=1` | The DeltaNet recurrence of a prompt as a chunked scan on the tensor cores; needs 122 KB of shared memory a block (sm_80, sm_90). | recurrence 105 -> 34 ms at 3.3K tokens; prompts +3.9 % (4K), +6.0 % (16K), +6.7 % (52K) |\n  1615\t| `STRATA_PLE_COMPACT=1`, `STRATA_PLE_COPY_STREAM=1`, `STRATA_EMBED_VRAM=1`, `STRATA_DEQUANT_SIDE=1` | The PLE rows go up raw and are dequantized on the card, on their own stream; the token-embedding table in VRAM; the dense dequant on a side stream (CUDA only). All bitwise. | prompts +2.2 % to +2.6 % for the four together |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
