top's narrow link wants less. |\n146:| `--pool-workers` | How many CPU threads compute experts. On CPUs with efficiency cores, fewer can be faster. |\n147:| `--adapt-every`, `--adapt-swaps`, `--adapt-decay` | How busily the VRAM expert cache follows the conversation. A CPU that reads missed experts slowly (DDR3 or DDR4 in few channels) gains from more swaps. |\n150:The defaults were measured on a Ryzen 5 7600 with an RTX 5070. From a source checkout, `START-HERE.bat --calibrate` (Linux: `./setup.sh --calibrate`)\n158:default, and the expert load then crawls.\n160:**Measured on:** RTX 5070 Ti, Ryzen 7 9800X3D, NVMe, a ~40 GB expert load, same binary, arguments and cache state.\n203:  (`--kv-resident`) is off, the KV cache stays in VRAM and experts are copied from unpinned RAM, which makes prompts\n229:### KV cache\n233:| `--kv int8` (default) | 8-bit KV cache. |\n235:| `--kv k8v4` | Keys at 8 bits, values as rotated 4-bit: 23% less KV memory than 8-bit, so more experts fit in VRAM. More precise than `q4_0` on long documents. Prompts are 2-5% slower. |\n236:| `--kv-streaming on\\|off\\|auto` | Keeps the context's KV cache in RAM and only the part attention reads in VRAM (`--kv-resident 32768`), so more experts fit on the GPU. Costs about 13.7 KB of RAM per context token (1.7 GB at 128K). Setup turns it on from 64K when the RAM has room; `on` overrides the RAM test with a note. Never under WSL. |\n237:| `--kv-grow` (or `STRATA_KV_GROW=1`; `--no-kv-grow` turns it off) | The KV takes VRAM only for the cells the requests reach; the expert cache holds the rest and gives slots back as the context grows. Needs one GPU, an expert profile, the whole KV in VRAM and every expert in RAM. Otherwise the engine says so and stays off. On HIP it uses virtual memory management (`STRATA_HIP_VMM=0` turns that off). It pays on cards of about 24 GB or less with contexts of 128K and more. |\n244:Normally all of a model's experts are copied into RAM (23-50 GB, pinned) and the GPU holds a copy of the most-used ones.\n245:When the RAM cannot hold them beside the system (the experts plus about 10 GB), setup picks one of these:\n249:| Mapped | `--mmap-experts` | Maps the experts from one file in the model's folder (the pack's `experts.bin`, +23-50 GB of disk), or from the GGUF files themselves for native packs that have no `experts.bin`. The OS file cache holds what the GPU does not and can give that memory back. With a small GPU most experts come from the SSD and it is much slower; setup says so. |\n250:| Resident | `--resident-experts` | When the experts the GPU does not hold fit the RAM, the engine copies exactly those into RAM at start. Nothing is read from the SSD while it answers. |\n251:| RAM budget | `--resident-budget-gib N` | The N GiB of experts the GPU does not hold that the profile ranks hottest are copied into RAM; the rest are read from the files. Implies `--mmap-experts` and leaves 4 GiB of headroom. On Windows the available commit limit also caps N, and a clamped budget is reported. This is what runs [Unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL](UNSLOTH_Q4.md) on a 64 GB PC. |\n254:- The resident variant keeps 4 GB of the RAM it finds free (`STRATA_RESIDENT_HEADROOM_GIB`). When even the missing experts do\n255:  not fit, it says so and runs the mapped mode. The server log shows per request how many expert reads went to the file\n257:- The adaptive tier (`--adapt-every`) keeps following the conversation in the resident mode. `--adapt-async 1` (opt-in,\n261:  A layer swaps up to 4 pairs per window: the experts the windows keep routing to (decayed count 2 or more) take the\n262:  RAM places of unused experts of the same layer (count below 0.5), largest difference first. The incoming expert was\n265:  many experts moved and how many came out of a stage buffer.\n270:- Linux, opt-in: `STRATA_ARENA_MMAP=1` maps a native pack's expert arena read-only from `experts.bin` instead of reading\n272:- Windows, opt-in: `STRATA_FILE_RELEASE=1` trims the file-backed pages of experts after their GPU uploads. It is a\n275:- Windows, opt-in: `STRATA_PIN_BY_NEED=1` (arena on one GPU) registers the profile's non-GPU-resident experts by rank up to\n286:When experts are read from GGUF files in place, Linux runs three things by default. Off switches are in brackets.\n290:| Zero-copy | `STRATA_COLD_OVERLAP=4` (`0` = off) | An expert whose three tensors' pages are all in the page cache is computed straight from the mapping, with no staging copy. |\n291:| io prefetch | `STRATA_IO_PREFETCH=1` (`0` = off) | I/O threads read the layer's uncached experts and the router-predicted ones of the next layers with whole-blob `pread`s into the page cache. Tunables: `STRATA_LOOKAHEAD_K`, `STRATA_IO_PREFETCH_DEPTH` (2), `STRATA_IO_PF_THREADS` (8), `STRATA_IO_PF_STAGE=1`, `STRATA_IO_PF_AHEAD=0`. |\n292:| Decode gate | `STRATA_IO_PF_GATE=1` (`0` = off) | Every 16 decode windows the read-ahead is judged. It runs only while the page cache has room for the experts (off below 0.55, on above 0.65: `STRATA_IO_PF_GATE_LO` and `_HI`) and while it pays (`STRATA_IO_PF_GATE_USEFUL`). |\n294:For an `experts.bin` pack, io prefetch and the gate switch on when the page cache has room for at least 55% of the experts\n296:`STRATA_IO_PREFETCH=1` forces it. The container RAM check counts a cgroup's clean page cache as available. The startup log\n300:The weights and the MTP draft files are asked for ahead of their reads at start (madvise and `posix_fadvise` WILLNEED, 128 KiB\n304:The prompt chunk follows the host's memory (Linux, file tier, on by default). When the page cache is smaller than the file\n305:set, a planner picks the chunk and ring with the fewest predicted bytes read per prompt from the RAM copy, the page cache that\n307:`chunk 8192 kept: ...` when it does not. It does nothing when the GPU holds every expert, when the file set fits the cache,\n313:With `--stats` the engine prints the tiers of the decode (`expert tiers`: blobs from the RAM copy, blobs and MB from the\n316:`