nutes between\n392:requests (`\"expert_profile_save_every\": 5` for another interval, `0` for exit only). The next start begins from it when it\n397:### The expert cache size is a budget\n399:`--expert-cache N` (`\"expert_cache\": N`) is `N` times the largest expert blob. The engine compares it only with the free VRAM\n400:it reads before the slots are written. `--expert-cache auto` (the default) checks again once the slots are written and\n401:shrinks if they do not fit. Under Windows an over-sized fixed cache does not fail. The driver pages the excess to system\n406:- Keep the cache on `auto`, or keep an explicit size and raise `--vram-reserve-mib`, which the engine deducts before it sizes\n407:  the cache.\n417:  `--conversation-cache-mib`, or the pagefile on an SSD.\n447:- **Prometheus.** With `Accept: text/plain` the metrics use vLLM's names (`vllm:num_requests_running`, `vllm:kv_cache_usage_perc`,\n448:  token and request counters, `vllm:prefix_cache_queries_total` and `_hits_total`, `vllm:spec_decode_num_draft_tokens_total`\n492:| `effort_position` | `\"start\"` | `\"end\"` puts a `low` or `medium` effort in a short system turn right before the answer, so a request that only changes the effort reuses the conversation cache. How well the model follows a level in that place is not measured. |\n495:| `env_to_user` | off | Moves opencode's `<env>` block (working directory, date) to the first user message so the system turn is the same for every session and the disk prefix cache restores it. |\n496:| `return_progress` | off | Per request `\"return_progress\": true` adds llama.cpp's `prompt_progress` (`total`, `cache`, `processed`, `time_ms`) to the stream, one update per `--prefill` chunk. A prompt shorter than one chunk sends nothing. |\n507:| `codex_thread_titles`, `codex_compaction_cache` | off | See [Codex CLI](#the-responses-api-and-codex-cli). |\n526:  written when it has a slip in it: a trailing or doubled comma, single quotes or `True`/`False`/`None`, a bare string between two\n543:  stands, in the form the tool list uses, so loading a tool leaves the prompt's start, and the prompt cache, unchanged. Any other\n555:  only after the strict parse failed, the server doubles the backslashes that are not valid escapes and checks the answer again.\n602:- With `STRATA_SPEC_PROB=1` the CPU experts of a native (IQ) pack compute a token's rows the same way whatever the window\n608:- **Non-finite logits end the request and drop the cached state (on by default).** If a verify window's logits are NaN, the\n628:nothing changes. Scaled contexts need proportionally more VRAM and RAM (~13 KB for the KV cache and ~0.26 KB for the rope\n629:tables per token). The scaling is fixed for the whole run, because the KV cache stores keys after rotating them.\n713:### Conversation cache\n724:| `--prompt-cache N` | Number of checkpoints (`0` = off, which also disables parking). |\n725:| `--prompt-cache-every N`, `--prompt-cache-root N`, `--turn-token ID` | Checkpoint interval, the system-prompt checkpoint (`0` = none) and the turn token. The interval is not a piece-size setting: [what it does](PROMPT_CACHE_TAIL.md#prompt-cache-every-does-not-set-the-size-of-the-pieces-a-prompt-is-read-in). |\n726:| `--prompt-cache-spread` (opt-in) | The checkpoint that leaves is the one whose removal leaves the smallest gap, so the kept points thin out geometrically back from the end. An agent that rewrites its history a few thousand tokens back then resumes near the edit instead of at the system prompt. Same RAM. |\n728:| `\"strata_prefix\": {\"messages\": 1}` (per request, opt-in) | One long document, many questions. Marks where the document ends: `{\"messages\": 1}`, `{\"message\": 0, \"chars\": 210000}` or `{\"tokens\": 12345}`. The checkpoint at the boundary is pinned, so 20 questions cost one read of the document. One pinned prefix at a time per engine; needs `--prompt-cache 3` or more; a malformed field is a 400. See [RESEARCH_RUNS.md](RESEARCH_RUNS.md). 