| ---: |\n    67\t| **Q2_0** | 87.3 | 93.0 | 81.8 | 76.2 | 73.7 | 60.3† |\n    68\t| **IQ2_XS** | 79.6 | 78.6 | 76.3 | 63.7 | 62.7 | 52.8† |\n    69\t| **IQ3_XXS** | 61.9 | 61.6 | 58.5 | 57.2 | 49.0 | - |\n    70\t| **IQ3_S** | 52.4 | 53.3 | 48.3 | 46.3 | 45.5 | - |\n    71\t| **Coder** | 58.9 | 55.1 | 54.9 | 53.2 | 43.0 | 42.8† |\n    72\t\n    73\t† not measured again since 0.1.14. Raw data: `bench/results/2026-09-29-speed-0126`.\n    74\t\n    75\t- Output speed depends on the text. Speculative decoding runs faster when more drafted tokens are accepted, so a different\n    76\t  answer to the same prompt moves it by several percent.\n    77\t- Time to first token is prompt length divided by prompt speed. With Q2_0 that is about 4 s at 4K, 25 s at 32K, under\n    78\t  2 minutes at 128K and 4.5 minutes at 262K.\n    79\t- IQ3_XXS and IQ3_S at 262K are not measured. With 43 and 50 GB of experts, a 260K-token context brings a 64 GB PC to its\n    80\t  memory limit by setup's estimate, so setup recommends up to 128K there. A longer context that you choose is kept, with a note.\n    81\t- Other cards, other CPUs and newer engines differ. The 0.1.36 fused prompt kernels and later changes raised these\n    82\t  numbers on RTX 30 and newer cards: see [History](#history--older-measurements). For your own PC, run the benchmark in\n    83\t  [BENCHMARK_PROTOCOL.md](BENCHMARK_PROTOCOL.md).\n    84\t\n    85\tMore VRAM matters more than a faster GPU. Every extra GB holds about 700 more experts, and every expert on the GPU is one\n    86\tthe CPU does not have to compute. `tools/make_profile.py` builds an expert profile from your own prompts: run the engine\n    87\tonce with `--dump-routing trace.bin`, then see the tool's help. The shipped profile ranks every expert pair, so a trace\n    88\tchanges the order only with `--reorder` (your trace's pairs first, then the base's, then the rest).\n    89\t\n    90\t## Which model?\n   250\t| Resident | `--resident-experts` | When the experts the GPU does not hold fit the RAM, the engine copies exactly those into RAM at start. Nothing is read from the SSD while it answers. |\n   251\t| RAM budget | `--resident-budget-gib N` | The N GiB of experts the GPU does not hold that the profile ranks hottest are copied into RAM; the rest are read from the files. Implies `--mmap-experts` and leaves 4 GiB of headroom. On Windows the available commit limit also caps N, and a clamped budget is reported. This is what runs [Unsloth's UD-Q4_K_XL](UNSLOTH_Q4.md) on a 64 GB PC. |\n   252\t\n   253\t- In a source checkout, `START-HERE.bat --setup --low-ram on|off` overrides the choice, and `--low-ram resident|mmap` forces one variant.\n   254\t- The resident variant keeps 4 GB of the RAM it finds free (`STRATA_RESIDENT_HEADROOM_GIB`). When even the missing experts do\n   255\t  not fit, it says so and runs the mapped mode. The server log shows per request how many expert reads went to the file\n   256\t  (`resident RAM: ... blob reads from the file`, 0 in steady use).\n   257\t- The adaptive tier (`--adapt-every`) keeps following the conversation in the resident mode. `--adapt-async 1` (opt-in,\n   258\t  `--serve`) moves the swaps between decode windows on a helper thread. With `--batch` slots it works between their\n   259\t  windows too, but not with `--batch-groups` or `--peer-device`. It is not bit-exact from run to run.\n   260\t- `STRATA_RAM_ADAPT=<n>` (opt-in, `--serve`, one GPU, needs `--adapt-every`): the RAM copy also follows the conversation.\n   261\t  A layer swaps up to 4 pairs per window: the experts the windows keep routing to (decayed count 2 or more) take the\n   262\t  RAM places of unused experts of the same layer (count below 0.5), largest difference first. The incoming expert was\n   263\t  usually read for the window before; when its blob still sits in that read's stage buffer it is copied from there and\n   264\t  no file read is made. `n` caps only the pairs that need a file read (`=1` is enough in practice). The log counts how\n   265\t  many experts moved and how many came out of a stage buffer.\n   341\t| `--lookup-chain-min M`, `--mtp-hnorm stream`, `--mtp-draft-vocab FILE` | Finer controls: the shortest context match `--lookup-chain` extends (default 3), one norm per stream, and a token subset of your own. |\n   342\t\n   343\tWhen the start stops with \"the draft head does not fit\" (a 12 GB card with a long context), the engine says how much it needs\n   344\tand which smaller subset fits. Setup suggests `--draft-vocab en` on cards under 14 GB; nothing changes unless you pass it.\n   345\t\n   346\t**Reproducible output.** With the IQ models the CPU rounds one-token expert groups and multi-token groups differently, so the\n   347\tsame prompt at temperature 0 can end in a different, equally good answer when drafting, cache state or a resumed conversation\n   348\tdiffer. `STRATA_IQ_MT_MIN=1` uses the multi-token kernels for every group, so the answer no longer depends on drafting (IQ3_S\n   349\tdecode -1 to -3% on a Ryzen 7600). For byte-identical repeats also add `--prompt-cache 0 --adapt-swaps 0 --pcie-frac 0` to\n   350\tthe engine args. `--pcie-frac 0` costs decode speed, so keep it for A/B runs.\n   351\t\n  1140\t`STRATA_TC_SM75`, `STRATA_PA_V1_PREFETCH`, `STRATA_SCORES_THREAD` (a thread-per-head block scorer; `=1` works on any CUDA card).\n  1141\tOptions that change answers stay separate: `STRATA_SELECT_SIMT=1|2`, `STRATA_PA_V2T=1`, `STRATA_PF_FUSED=1 STRATA_PF_FUSED_SM75=1`\n  1142\t(Q2_0 pack, 7.5 card only), `STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1` (the native Q2_0 pack's prompt experts on sm_80+, below) and\n  1143\t`STRATA_BF16_TC=1` with `STRATA_XN_F16`.\n  1144\t\n  1145\t### The native Q2_0 pack's prompt experts on the fused int8 kernels (`STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1`, opt-in, sm_80+)\n  1146\t\n  1147\tA PC whose CPU lacks the AVX-512 set that setup looks for gets the native Q2_0 pack. On a CUDA card of sm_80 or newer its\n  1148\tQ2_0 / Q2_0 layers read prompts through MMQ's Q2_0 kernel; the fused int8 kernel for that format (#2145) is set up only on a\n  1149\t7.5 card. `STRATA_PF_FUSED_Q2N=1` sets it up on any sm_80+ card and takes it for those layers. It has its own variable, so\n  1150\t`STRATA_PF_FUSED=1` keeps its bits, and `STRATA_PF_FUSED=0` or `STRATA_PF_FUSED_NATIVE=0` still turn it off. Unset, the engine\n  1151\tis unchanged (first-token logits byte-identical to v0.2.2 on 40 prompts of 1-5K tokens, the author's check). With the switch\n  1152\tthe prompt arithmetic changes and the generated text can change.\n  1153\t\n  1154\tThe author measured it on an RTX 5080 16 GB (sm_120, Linux, CUDA 13.0), i9-14900K, 2,100 experts in VRAM, CPU prompt share off,\n  1155\tagainst the same build unset. At the same 4,864-token chunk (4 interleaved rounds) the 4K, 16K and 23K prompts read 2-3.5%\n  1156\tfaster (4/4 rounds each). With `--prefill auto` (5 rounds) the fused path's smaller buffers make the chunk 5,632 tokens instead of\n  1157\t4,864 and the 16K prompt reads 32-33% faster (2,592 -> 3,438 tok/s), the 23K one 14%, a 4K one 0-2%; so most of the gain is the\n  1158\tlarger chunk, not the kernel. One layer (`prefill_fused_iq_test`, Q2_0 / Q2_0) runs at 1.92x / 1.37x / 1.30x the MMQ path at\n  1159\t2,048 / 4,864 / 8,192 tokens. First-token KL against the FP16 prompt path over 30 prompts of 1.5-4.6K tokens: 0.0110 (MMQ\n  1160\t0.0112), top-1 agreement 28/30 (MMQ 29/30). Not measured: a card that holds every expert, other sm_80+ cards, Windows, the\n  1161\tgenerated text. On an RTX A4000 the same kernel in #2145 stopped matching the old output and read 4K prompts 4.5% slower, so\n  1162\tAmpere is not verified. The ctest entry `prefill_fused_iq_test_q2n` runs the Q2_0 / Q2_0 layer with the switch alone (it skips\n  1163\tbelow sm_80 and without a card).\n  1164\t\n  1165\t---\n  1166\t\n  1167\t## CPU workers and thread placement\n  1168\t\n  1169\t### How many CPU threads (`--pool-workers`)\n  1170\t\n  1171\tWithout the setting the engine picks the number of CPU expert workers from the CPU, not from the count of logical threads: one\n  1172\tworker per physical core (never an SMT sibling) except the core of the main thread, one core less on a CPU with 17 or more\n  1173\tcores, only the cores of the main thread's socket on a multi-socket machine, at most 32, no more than a container's CPU limit\n  1174\tallows, and on a hybrid CPU with more efficiency than performance cores the performance cores but one plus half of the\n  1175\tefficiency cores. The start line says what bounded the number (`CPU pool: 31 workers by default (32 physical cores, ...)`).\n  1176\tWorkers wait by spinning, so a worker without a core of its own takes time from the others. A count in the config is always\n  1177\tused as given; when it is above what the CPU uses well, the engine prints a warning with the recommended number\n  1178\t(`strata cpu pool: warning: ...`). Setup writes no worker count except the hybrid CPU's, and gives the CPU vision encoder at most 16\n  1179\tthreads (`--threads` in the `vision` entry changes that).\n  1180\t\n  1181\t`--pool-tasks N` sets the target total number of row tasks in each batched CPU expert gate/up and down phase (the default `0` keeps\n  1182\tthree per participating thread; values `1..4096` are capped by each phase's row count). More tasks can reduce imbalance between\n  1183\tcores and add scheduling overhead, so compare against `0` with the same worker count. Add `\"--pool-tasks\", \"192\"` to the `args`\n  1184\tlist and restart. Setup's `--calibrate` does not tune it.\n  1185\t\n  1186\t### Two sockets and NUMA\n  1187\t\n  1188\tThe default uses the host's socket only. With 2 or more NUMA nodes the pool splits itself by node on Linux: one slice per\n  1189\tnode, each with its own task counter, and each node's half of every CPU-side expert's rows is placed in that node's memory. The\n  1190\tdefault worker count under the split is the physical cores of both sockets minus the host core (minus one more from 16 workers\n  1191\ton, at most 32). An explicit `--pool-workers N` is spread alternately across the sockets.\n  1192\t\n  1193\t- It runs when Linux has exactly 2 nodes holding the pool's threads, balanced slices, pinned threads and no\n  1194\t  `STRATA_EXCHANGE_ROTATE=1`; otherwise the log says `NUMA pool off: <reason>` and the pool runs as before. One node is exactly\n  1195\t  the pool as before and logs nothing. Windows reads the node count but does not split yet.\n  1196\t- `STRATA_NUMA_POOL=0` turns it off (also `off`, `false`, `no`) and wins over `STRATA_NUMA_SPLIT=1`. `STRATA_NUMA_SPLIT=1` asks for\n  1197\t  the split on any machine; `=0` is off; `=test` builds the slices on any machine for scheduling tests, not for speed.\n  1198\t- Only native expert layers are split. If a node lacks free memory for its half, the log warns `NUMA placement at risk` and\n  1199\t  after the load prints FAILED when a node holds under 95% of its slice.\n  1200\t- Do not wrap the server in `numactl --cpunodebind` when testing workers across sockets: it pins them to one node.\n  1201\t- Workers on both sockets paid off on one box and cost on another, so measure on yours: `--pool-workers N` with N above one\n  1202\t  socket's cores, a fresh server per value, the same prompt, decode tok/s from the log.\n  1203\t- Check the IOMMU. On a distro kernel where it translates every DMA (`cat /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*/type | sort | uniq -c`\n  1204\t  shows `DMA-FQ`), pinned copies ran slower than with `iommu=pt` on the kernel command line.\n  1205\t\n168:  # prohibition: -DSTRATA_BUILD_TESTS=ON still builds what builds (classifying the tests by capability is a follow-up).\n171:option(STRATA_BUILD_TESTS \"Build the test suite\" ${_strata_tests_default})\n340:if(STRATA_BUILD_TESTS)\n364:  add_executable(gguf_geometry_test tests/core/gguf_geometry_test.cpp)\n365:  target_link_libraries(gguf_geometry_test PRIVATE st