in shared memory must equal gguf-py's dequantization of the file's expert, rounded once to f16 (a tile value may differ by one f16 step where the two compute the f32 product in anot\ntests/desktop/test_app_version.py: The desktop app's version is written in one place: apps/desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.toml (Tauri reads it for the installer and file names when tauri.conf.json has no version; the shell passes it to the host as STRATA_APP_VERSION; the host reads the same file in a checkout).\ntests/desktop/test_control_install.py: The desktop app's installer: tools/strata_mcp.py and setup.py run on Control's Python in STRATA_CONTROL_INSTALL mode. What the owner asked for: a user installs the app, picks a model in it and downloads and prepares it, like any normal user. What must stay true: a start or a serve never acquires anything (tests/terminal/test_no_acquisition.py), and the install itself downloads model files only - no pip, no git, no compiler, no engine fetch, only \ntests/desktop/test_fetch_node.py: fetch-node.ps1 (Windows) and fetch-node.sh pin the same Node archive digest, and the PowerShell script refuses a download that does not match it.\ntests/desktop/test_nsis_layout.py: The Windows installer must never touch the user's data. The app lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Strata; %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Strata holds engines, model run configs, tuning. A build that installed the app into the data folder is migrated by removing only its app files. Exercised with makensis on a temp layout (the template's own migration macro, extracted and run); skipped without NSIS.\ntests/desktop/test_tester_readme.py: The tester READMEs get their checksums from the files themselves (apps/desktop/scripts/make-tester-readme.sh).\ntests/sycl/test_intel_two_card_spec.py: strata_control/intel_spec.py: --spec 2 for a run config on exactly two Arc B-series (Battlemage) cards. The rule changes only the generated default (--spec 4). Single cards, Alchemist cards, three cards, a config with no draft layer and anything the person set stay as they are. Also checks the two places that apply it: sycl/setup_intel.py's config rewrite (through apply_to_config) and the Intel server's engine_args wrapper.\ntests/sycl/test_setup_intel_prefill.py: sycl/setup_intel.py: the prompt chunk size setup recommends for an Intel card (--prefill). A Battlemage card (xe driver, not an A-series) with 24 GB or more and a context over 32K (the prompt borrows cache slots) gets 8192; everything else keeps 4096; a chunk size the user put in the config is kept.\ntests/tuning/stub_engine.py: A stub engine for exercising `strata tune run` end to end, on every OS. Run it as ``[sys.executable, \"stub_engine.py\", \"--serve\", ...]`` (``strata_control.tuning.engine_command`` does that for any ``.py`` engine). It speaks the real serve protocol (READY / PP / T / DONE) and makes its decode speed depend on one flag, so a sweep has something real to find. It is NOT a model and its numbers mean nothing about any hardware -- it exists so the *sweep\ntests/tuning/stub_session.py: A stand-in for `tune_session.WorkerSession`: speeds are computed from the flags it is given, so the tuner's decisions can be tested without an engine. The numbers mean nothing about any hardware.\ntests/tuning/test_autotune.py: The auto-tuner's decisions, tested with a stub engine whose speed is computed from the flags it is given.\ntests/tuning/test_components.py: Component store tests (checklist P06, P07, P11, P12, P13). The security tests here use the actual attack shapes rather than a generic \"bad path\", because the failure this guards against is arbitrary file writes from a hostile archive, and each shape defeats a different naive check: * ``../`` escapes a destination that only joins paths; * a leading ``/`` defeats a check that only looks for ``..``; * a Windows drive letter defeats a POSIX-only chec\ntests/tuning/test_offline.py: Offline policy tests (checklist U08). The checklist asks for two claims that are easy to conflate, and the whole point of these tests is that they are separate: * **runtime-only no-download** — the server fetches nothing while serving; * **strict offline** — nothing in the process tree reaches the network, including children, tools and the agent runtime. A passing runtime-only test says nothing about the second: the first is about the server's ow\ntests/tuning/test_preset_persistence.py: A saved tuning is applied at every start: after an app restart (a new process), after an unload and a load, and it can be seen (`presets effective`, `presets status`) and switched off and on again.\ntests/tuning/test_presets.py: The preset store: save, name, apply on load, stale detection, undo.\ntests/tuning/test_session.py: The real session's request side, against a tiny HTTP server that answers like the worker does (a `timings` block on the engine's own clock).\ntests/tuning/test_tune_api.py: API-path measurement tests (checklist T04). `tune_api.ApiHarness` drives a real worker over HTTP. Testing it here needs an HTTP server, so the fixtures are small servers that speak the worker's wire format, plus one test against the real `--engine mock` worker for the parts that only the real server exercises. What is being pinned down is what counts as a usable measurement. The failure modes are all quiet: a reasoning-only stream measured as a g\ntests/tuning/test_tune_apply.py: Applying and rolling back a tuning recommendation (checklist T07). The requirement is that a separate Apply revalidates the report and profile, snapshots, restarts and verifies, and rolls back on failure — with no automatic winner application. The risk these tests exist for is the gap between measuring a configuration and running it for real. A sweep can recommend something that boots but cannot generate, or that was measured against different mo\ntests/tuning/test_tune_engine.py: Engine measurement tests. The parsing is tested against the engine's real line formats, taken from `src/program/generate.cpp`, and the failure handling is tested with a stub engine that speaks the same protocol. That combination is deliberate: real hardware needs a model that does not fit this machine, but the *contract* — what counts as a measurement and what must be rejected — is fully testable here, and it is the part that decides whether a tu\ntests/tuning/test_tune_history.py: Tuning history tests (checklist T06). The requirement is that an engine, model, driver, workload or context change invalidates incompatible history, and that model identity is more than a name. The risk being tested is specific and quiet: a report that mixes two configurations' numbers produces a confident recommendation from incomparable measurements, and nothing about the output looks wrong. So each element of the comparison key gets its own te\ntests/tuning/test_tuning.py: Tuning job contract tests. The measurements are injected, which is the point: these tests are about the *contract* — which dimensions a backend may search, what happens to a candidate that fails, how a winner is chosen, and what the job refuses to do. A GPU is not needed and, more importantly, a hardcoded benchmark number can never leak into the module under test. The negative cases carry the weight: a sweep must never promote a candidate the eng\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
